\emph{Abstract}---\textbf{ Multiattribute utility theory has a long history of}
\par
\textbf{application in engineering and systems design. These}
\par
\textbf{applications rely almost exclusively on two types of utility}
\par
\textbf{functions, the additive and multiplicative forms. The}
\par
\textbf{foundations of utility theory, however, do not place restrictions}
\par
\textbf{on the functional form of the utility function, meaning many}
\par
\textbf{methods of representing preferences remain unexplored in}
\par
\textbf{systems design. This paper reviews the literature on}
\par
\textbf{applications of utility theory in design to bring light to}
\par
\textbf{potential new directions for research and to clarify a few subtle}
\par
\textbf{misapplications of utility theory. These misuses include the}
\par
\textbf{failure to distinguish between direct and indirect value}
\par
\textbf{attributes, the use of probability as an attribute, treating costs}
\par
\textbf{from different sources differently, and restricting the}
\par
\textbf{functional form of utility. This paper introduces a value-based}
\par
\textbf{approach that is based on the creation of a deterministic value}
\par
\textbf{function for design and the assignment of a one-dimensional}
\par
\textbf{utility function over the value measure. We use a conceptual}
\par
\textbf{and numeric example to illustrate the greater flexibility of this}
\par
\textbf{approach. We also present the concept of utility transversality}
\par
\textbf{in engineering design. We show that several criticisms of utility}
\par
\textbf{theory that have appeared in the engineering design literature}
\par
\textbf{are actually criticisms of these artificial limitations and that}
\par
\textbf{these limitations are overcome by the value-based approach.}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Keywords- Utility theory, multi attribute utility, value-based}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{design, systems design, engineering design}}
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
The important role of decision making has long been
\par
recognized in engineering and systems design [1-7]. Early
\par
texts on the subject describe the need to identify relevant
\par
criteria [1, 2], the importance of characterizing uncertainties
\par
[3-5], and the application of weighting functions [2-4].
\par
Tribus [8] underscores the fundamental role of decision
\par
making when explaining engineering design:
\par
Design has been defined by Rosenstein as an `iterative
\par
\emph{decision-}making process' comprising certain well
\par
\emph{recognized steps. The emphasis should be placed on decision}
\par
\emph{making, for this is the heart of the design process. (page 391)}
\par
More recently, the phrase ``decision-based design'' has
\par
been introduced to emphasize the decision making role of the
\par
design engineer [9, 10]. Subsequently, a large volume of
\par
work on decision making in engineering and systems design
\par
has emerged that uses a variety of terms to reference the role
\par
of decisions: decision-based design [11-13], decision theory
\par
[14], and utility theory [15, 16]. Tomiyama et al. [17]
\par
categorize the various approaches to decision making into
\par
those that are heuristics-based and those that are based on
\par
decision theory.
\par
The majority of the work in this area applies decision
\par
making to the design artifact itself. It is worth noting that
\par
defining the scope of the decision, i.e. the decision framing,
\par
is an important first step in the analysis. It is possible to
\par
define a decision frame to examine other aspects of the
\par
design process. Thompson and Paredis, for example, apply
\par
decision analysis to the decisions about the design process
\par
itself [18], while Shukla, Auriol, and Hipel examine the
\par
problem of aggregating preferences in systems design [19].
\par
Although the design literature has described the
\par
application of decision analysis to engineering design [8], we
\par
have observed a persistent gap in the understanding of utility
\par
theory and the application of decision analytic principles to
\par
the design artifact. A literature review is used to identify four
\par
concepts in utility theory that have been misapplied in
\par
systems design. Clarifying these concepts is the first
\par
motivation for this paper. This paper explains each in detail.
\par
This clarification is necessary given the stakes involved in
\par
systems design; some studies have suggested that the current
\par
systems engineering processes contribute to \$150 million per
\par
day in delay and overrun costs at the Department of Defense
\par
alone [20].
\par
This paper also seeks to expand the flexibility of utility
\par
theory in systems design by presenting a value-based
\par
approach to preference functions. The approach requires the
\par
specification of a deterministic value function and a utility
\par
function over a single value measure. Tradeoffs are assessed
\par
in a deterministic setting. A one-dimensional utility function
\par
is assessed. The approach ensures consistency in interactions
\par
among variables and risk attitude toward each attribute.
\par
It is advantageous to acknowledge that doing decision
\par
making work within a field of engineering does not make a
\par
person an expert in decision analysis. Conversely, knowing
\par
decision analysis does not make a person an expert within a
\par
field of engineering. However, through appropriate
\par
communication and the exchange of ideas, the intersection of
\par
the fields can lead to superior designs. An additional
\par
motivation for this paper is to facilitate this conversation
\par
between academic communities. The contributions of thispaper include a literature review of the application of utility
\par
theory to systems design, the introduction of a value-based
\par
approach that allows preference tradeoffs to be made in a
\par
deterministic setting, and to spark collaboration between
\par
different academic communities.
\par
In the pursuit of understanding and academic dialogue,
\par
Section 2 explains the rationale for the expected utility
\par
criterion and why it is not appropriate to use arbitrary
\par
measures in a decision problem. Section 3 discusses common
\par
misuses of utility theory in engineering design, and Section 4
\par
explains the value-based approach to decision-based design.
\par
Section 5 summarizes the main insights and explores future
\par
directions and opportunities for research.
\par
II. THE RATIONALE FOR EXPECTED UTILITY
\par
Utility theory as a foundation for decision making is
\par
based on a set of axioms. If a decision maker agrees with the
\par
concept described by axiom, then he or she should make
\par
decisions consistent with utility theory in order to be
\par
consistent with the axiomatic principles. Decisions that are
\par
inconsistent with utility theory imply that the decision
\par
maker is violating at least one of the foundational concepts.
\par
Thus, clarity in understanding these axioms is crucial to
\par
understanding the usefulness---and limitations---of applying
\par
decision analysis to engineering design, and to
\par
understanding the distinctions discussed in this paper.
\par
These axioms, as originally stated by von Neumann and
\par
Morgenstern [21], are completeness, transitivity, continuity,
\par
and independence. Howard [22] and others [23, 24] present
\par
these axioms in the form of five rules of decision making.
\par
To facilitate understanding utility theory and provide a basis
\par
for the discussion in this paper, the axioms as formulated in
\par
five rules are as follows.
\par
1) The Probability Rule: This rule states that (i) I can
\par
characterize each decision alternative I face in terms of the
\par
set of possible prospects that may occur; and (ii) I can
\par
assign a probability of receiving each prospect for every
\par
alternative I choose. Put very generally, this rule states that
\par
we can draw a decision tree with all of its nodes and
\par
numbers.
\par
2) The Order Rule: states that I can rank order the
\par
deterministic prospects that I have characterized in a list
\par
from the most preferred to the least preferred. Ties are
\par
allowed but preferences must be transitive, i.e. if I prefer
\par
prospect \emph{A }to prospect \emph{B}, and if I prefer prospect \emph{B }to
\par
prospect \emph{C}, then I must prefer prospect \emph{A} to prospect \emph{C}.
\par
3) The Equivalence Rule (von Neumann Morgenstern
\par
utility rule): states that given three ordered prospects with
