\par
focuses specifically on applications of utility theory. We
\par
distinguish utility theory and decision analysis from other
\par
methods that do not follow utility theory such as the analytic
\par
hierarchy process [25] or the quality function deployment
\par
method [26]. Comments and critiques on these methods may
\par
be found elsewhere [27-30], and are not the focus of this
\par
paper.
\par
III. SOME COMMON MISUSES OF UTILITY THEORY
\par
The application of utility theory requires attention to
\par
some important distinctions that may not be readily apparent
\par
to experts in fields outside decision analysis. A literature
\par
search has shown four common misuses of utility theory.
\par
This section provides a literature review and to bring clarity
\par
to the seemingly small details that have a large effect on the
\par
validity of an analysis.
\par
3.1 The Difference between Direct and Indirect Value
\par
Attributes
\par
For any given decision or design, there may be numerous
\par
factors important to the decision maker. A business may
\par
care about market share, brand recognition, product quality,
\par
and profit. A closer inspection reveals, however, that the
\par
business cares about market share, brand recognition, and
\par
product quality because they all update the information about
\par
profit. If the profit were known in advance for all future time
\par
periods, information about market share would no longer
\par
have value as it would not affect the deterministic profit.
\par
This example illustrates that not all factors in a decision
\par
should be treated the same. The distinction between direct
\par
and indirect attributes must be made [23, 31, 32].
\par
Direct value attributes are those items that a decision
\par
maker cares about after all uncertainties are resolved. For
\par
profit-maximizing firms, a direct value is profit [10, 13, 33-
\par
34]. An indirect value is something that the decision maker
\par
cares about because it updates his state of information about
\par
the direct values or affects the probability distribution of the
\par
direct value attributes. A question that can be asked to help
\par
distinguish between two, is would I accept a lower profit in
\par
exchange for a better measure on this variable? If the answer
\par
is yes, then the variable represents a direct value attribute. If
\par
the answer is no, then the variable is likely an indirect value
\par
attribute.
\par
This distinction is represented in a decision diagram by
\par
the presence (direct value attribute) or absence (indirect
\par
value attribute) of an arrow into a final value node. Consider
\par
the two different formulations of a design decision shown in
\par
Figure 2. Square nodes represent decisions; ovals represent
\par
uncertainties; double-lined ovals are deterministic; hexagons
\par
are values. The diagram on the left presents product quality
\par
as an indirect value attribute. Quality affects both revenue
\par
and cost but is not valued by itself. The diagram on the right
\par
shows how the analysis changes if quality is treated as a
\par
direct value. In addition to profit, another value node is
\par
needed to represent how the decision maker values both
\par
profit and quality. In this case, some tradeoff exists between
\par
quality and profit.
\par
Treating a variable as an indirect versus a direct value
\par
attribute changes the objective function of the analysis. If
\par
quality is an indirect value attribute, then any quality-related
\par
decision variables affect the distribution over profit. On the
\par
other hand, if quality is a direct value attribute, then it
\par
appears as an argument in the utility function, and is an
\par
additional variable over which the expectation is calculated.
\par
Figure 2. Decision diagrams contrasting the treatment of
\par
quality as an indirect value attribute (left) versus the
\par
treatment of quality as a direct value attribute (right).In the absence of a clear distinction between indirect and
\par
direct value attributes, it seems logical to include indirect
\par
value attributes in a multiattribute utility function. In a
\par
probabilistic setting, these variables are in fact important in
\par
affecting outcomes. However, the way in which they affect
\par
the outcomes must be correctly modeled for consistency with
\par
utility theory. Multiattribute utility functions should be
\par
constructed to include only direct value attributes in their
\par
arguments. Indirect value attributes contribute to the
\par
expectation portion of the objective function.
\par
The specification of direct versus indirect attributes also
\par
highlights an important distinction about whose preferences
\par
should be represented by the utility function. If the design
\par
decision is being made by a company, then the company's
\par
utility function should be optimized. Some analyses in the
\par
literature use the customer's utility function [35, 36].
\par
Although customer preferences are important in determining
\par
the willingness to pay and thus the distribution over profit,
\par
optimizing the customer's utility function does not optimize
\par
the profit realized by the company.
\par
3.2 Money is Money
\par
Money, whether in the form of revenue, cost, or profit, or
\par
something else, is a special case among direct value
\par
attributes deserves attention. The reason for the special
\par
consideration is that money itself has an inherent value equal
\par
to its magnitude. Therefore, money from different sources
\par
must be treated equally. For example, costs may arise from
\par
the purchase of materials and the time required for
\par
assembling those materials. But the overall cost is the sum of
\par
the two.
\par
This clarification may seem trivial, but it is important.
\par
While some authors correctly sum costs from multiple
\par
sources to create a single cost attribute [37], others treat costs
\par
from different sources as multiple cost attributes. This issue
\par
can appear in subtle ways. For example, slowed productivity
\par
is a cost that is affect by factors such as load capacity and
\par
work speed. If there are costs associated with smaller load
\par
capacity and costs associated with slower work speed, then
\par
these costs should be summed and represent a single
\par
monetary attribute. This approach requires the specification
\par
of how factors such as work speed affect cost, but if a
\par
detailed decision analysis is to be conducted, such a task
\par
should be considered an important component of the
\par
analysis. Some studies in the literature, however, do not
\par
consider how such factors affect cost and therefore risk
\par
treating costs due to load capacity differently from costs due
\par
to work speed [38, 39]. Other examples include treating costs
\par
from the usable life of a part differently from other costs [40,
\par
41] or treating costs from disassembly of a product
\par
differently than costs from reassembly [42].
\par
Another subtle way sources of money are treated
\par
differently is in the inclusion of environmental impacts. In
\par
some cases a direct value may be placed on environmental
\par
impact. For example, a non-profit entity dedicated to
\par
environmental protection clearly places value directly on the
\par
environment. In most cases of a profit-maximizing, publicly
\par
traded company as the decision maker, such a direct value
\par
does not exist. The company ensures that all applicable
\par
environmental regulations are met, but does not value them
\par
directly. This type of company is described by Fitzgerald et
\par
al. [43]:
\par
\emph{Product development organizations are}
\par
\emph{unwilling to compromise product}
\par
\emph{functionality, unit cost, or time to market}
\par
\emph{in order to create products that have less}
\par
\emph{environmental impact than that required}
\par
\emph{by regulations.}
\par
The actions required to meet environmental regulations
\par
have costs associated with them. Thus, when considering a
\par
set of decision alternatives, the environmental impact of each
\par
alternative is important because of the costs it represents and
\par
any effect it may have on the probability distribution over
\par
customer purchasing. In other analyses in the literature, the
\par
cost of the environmental impact of a decision alternative is
\par
not treated as a cost at all. Rather, the environmental impact
\par
is treated as a unique direct value attribute [44, 45].
\par
3.3 Using Probability as an Argument in the Utility Function
\par
Another common misuse of utility theory occurs when
\par
probability is itself used as an attribute. On occasion
\par
probability has been explicitly defined as an attribute [42],
\par
but more often, the practice is much more subtle. For
\par
example, reliability is important to many designs. But if
\par
reliability is defined as a measure of the probability of a
\par
breakdown, then treating reliability as an attribute means that
\par
probability has become an attribute even if not explicitly
\par
stated as such [46, 47]. Such a treatment is no longer
\par
consistent with the axiomatic basis of utility theory. An
\par
analysis consistent with utility theory would have probability
\par
contribute to the objective function in the expectation, i.e. the
\par
probability distribution, only.
\par
We do not mean to say that reliability is not an important
\par
concept or that reliability should not be included in the
\par
analysis. Rather, we wish to highlight that a decision analytic
\par
treatment of reliability requires a different approach to the
\par
concept. Utility theory demands the decision maker consider
\par
why reliability matters and model its consequences. For
\par
example, if a breakdown results in additional costs, then the
\par
probability of the breakdown should be included in the
\par
expectation, and the costs associated with it should be
\par
included within the measure of money. If a breakdown
\par
affects consumer demand for the product, then the
\par
appropriate probability distribution over demand should be
\par
specified for those variables that affect the reliability. Thus,
\par
while we understand that it is tempting to define reliability as
\par
an attribute, the resulting analysis will be inconsistent with
\par
utility theory.
\par
3.4 Using Only Traditional Representations of Preferences
\par
After the indirect and direct value attributes are specified,
\par
the application of utility theory generally requires thespecification of some utility function to represent the
\par
decision maker's preferences under uncertainty. The utility
\par
function must represent the decision maker's preferences in a
\par
way that is consistent with the Equivalence Rule explained in
\par
Section 2. The form of the utility function is neither
\par
specified nor restricted by the axioms of von Neumann and
\par
Morgenstern [21].
\par
Within the engineering design literature, however, utility
\par
theory has largely become synonymous with the application
\par
of just two objective functions: the weighted sum and the
\par
multiplicative utility function. See for example analyses
\par
using the weighted sum [37, 48-54], or the multiplicative
\par
utility function [15, 16, 42, 46, 55-60]. The extensive
\par
number of cases using these two utility functions makes
\par
them a traditional choice. However, these functional forms
\par
place additional restrictions on the types of preferences that
\par
can be modeled. These restrictions appear to be largely
\par
unappreciated in the literature. But when they are mentioned,
\par
they seem to be cited as shortcomings of utility theory [61-
\par
63]. This section explains the preference restrictions implied
\par
by these functional forms. Further, we highlight that utility
\par
theory is not limited to these functions and absolutely does
\par
not require these restrictions.
\par
Analyses employing the additive or multiplicative utility
\par
functions are generally formulated with a set of n attributes.
\par
A utility function over each attribute is specified. The utility
\par
