the decision variables in an engineering design problem.
\par
Specifying the relationship between these design attributes
\par
using the additive or multiplicative forms can lead to
\par
misrepresentations of the decision maker's preferences. It
\par
seems more prudent for the case of engineering design
\par
problems to use a value function. The value function
\par
approach for applying utility theory has not received
\par
attention in the engineering design community. The next
\par
section explains this approach and its benefits in an
\par
engineering design context.
\par
IV. VALUE-BASED DESIGN: IMPLICATIONS FOR THE
\par
UTILITY FUNCTION
\par
The presence of uncertainty motivates the use of utility
\par
theory for normative decision making. However, the very
\par
presence of uncertainty has been cited as a factor that
\par
contributes to difficulty in specifying preferences [62].
\par
Fortunately, a value-based approach exists in which the
\par
decision maker first specifies her preferences for
\par
deterministic outcomes in terms of a value function and then
\par
specifies a utility function over value [65, 66]. While the
\par
engineering design community has suggested correlating
\par
objective functions with value functions [67], the concept of
\par
constructing a single dimensional utility function over value
\par
as a first step in the construction of a multiattribute utility
\par
function has not been explored. While this value-based
\par
approach seems to be the most appropriate for constructing
\par
multiattribute utility functions in engineering design, we
\par
have not found many of its applications in the literature.
\par
Before explaining the steps of the value-based approach,
\par
an important distinction between utility and value must be
\par
made. Utility describes preferences under conditions of
\par
uncertainty while value describes preferences under
\par
conditions of certainty. This distinction is not always made
\par
clear in the engineering design literature. Some authors use
\par
value and utility interchangeably [36, 68], while others
\par
reference the worth of a design without clarifying the
\par
meaning to be a value or utility [69]. Failure to clarify this
\par
distinction can lead to confusion in the literature, for
\par
example, fixing certain variables in a multiattribute utility
\par
function and calling the resulting one-dimensional utility
\par
function a value function [16], using a measure of cost as a
\par
utility function [70], or using a utility function to construct
\par
an estimate of costs [71].
\par
The value-based approach [65, 66] is predicated on a
\par
structural model of the system that includes both state
\par
variables and decision variables. This model is necessary to
\par
understand how the system performs under different
\par
configurations. A value function is then determined, and this
\par
function may include performance measures. If the system
\par
value will not be realized for some time period, a time
\par
preference model can be included. Finally, a utility or risk
\par
preference model over value is defined. This process isFig. 3. Value-based design requires that utility (risk
\par
preference) and value models are consistent with the
\par
underlying system structural model [72, 32].
\par
illustrated in Figure 3 which shows that each step provides
\par
the foundation for the next part of the analysis, a
\par
conceptualization that originally appeared in [72]. This
\par
approach provides a consistent link between the underlying
\par
state and decision variable, the system value, and the utility
\par
function.
\par
