4.3 Implications for Risk Attitude
\par
An interesting and useful implication of the value-based
\par
approach is the implication for the risk attitude towards the
\par
different attributes. Matheson and Abbas show that in the
\par
presence of a value function and a risk attitude towards
\par
value, the risk attitudes toward each attribute must be
\par
consistent with the utility and value functions [65]. This
\par
consistency results in the derivation of utility transversality,
\par
a closed form expression that relates the risk aversion
\par
functions of each attribute to the value trade-offs between
\par
them [65].
\par
Although a full derivation and explanation of utility
\par
transversality is beyond the scope of this paper, it is
\par
worthwhile to point out a few implications for constructing
\par
multiattribute utility functions. First, the presence or
\par
absence of utility independence can be checked by deriving
\par
the risk attitude towards a single attribute following the
\par
Pratt [75] and Arrow [76] definition of risk attitude and
\par
using the partial derivatives of the utility function over
\par
value. If this expression contains any of the other attributes
\par
in it, then utility independence does not hold between the
\par
attributes. Second, the utility transversality relation provides
\par
a useful way to reconstruct preference relations in a
\par
consistent manner when the decision maker can specify
\par
preferences over one attribute but has difficulty specifying
\par
them over another attribute.
\par
The concept of utility transversality has not been
\par
previously examined in the context of engineering design.
\par
However, we find it to be a helpful tool in understanding
\par
and constructing multiattribute utility functions.
\par
V. CONCLUSION
\par
This paper seeks to clarify utility theory and its
\par
application to engineering design. Towards this end, we
\par
describe the basis of utility theory and survey how it has
\par
been commonly applied in engineering design. Through
\par
clear examples, we seek to highlight some of the
\par
misconceptions about utility theory and open doors to new
\par
research and dialogue between the engineering design and
\par
decision analysis communities.
\par
A major misconception about utility theory is that it is
\par
limited to the multiplicative and additive functional forms.
\par
In reality, utility theory places no restrictions on the
\par
functional form used to represent preferences. The
\par
limitations imposed by these forms are underscored by the
\par
comparison of the use of these approaches to the use of the
\par
value-based approach introduced by this paper.
\par
The value-based approach that is introduced in this paper
\par
is based on the creation of a deterministic value function for
\par
design and the assignment of a one-dimensional utility
\par
function over the value measure. We use a conceptualexample and a numeric example to illustrate the consistency
\par
of the method with underlying, structural models and the
\par
flexibility of the method to hands cases where utility
\par
independence may or may not hold.
\par
Overall, this paper highlights the need for new ways to
\par
represent preferences without placing arbitrary restrictions
\par
on them. Both the decision analysis literature [65, 78-79]
\par
and the engineering design literature [80-82] describe new
\par
and ongoing work in representing preferences. This research
\par
is motivated by the need to incorporate possible
\par
dependencies among attributes, as discussed by several
\par
authors [83-87]. Exciting opportunities for future research
\par
may be realized once it is recognized that these new and
\par
developing ideas in engineering design need not be at odds
\par
with utility theory. For example, several authors have
\par
examined preference consistencies and inconsistencies [81,
\par
88]. This work does not invalidate the use of utility theory
\par
or decision analysis. On the contrary, concepts from
\par
decision analysis such as the value of information can be
\par
used in conjunction with these ideas to derive additional
\par
insights. Recent work has also explored the implications of
\par
setting targets and requirements on the selection of design
\par
projects [89-91].
\par
Because of the generality of utility theory, there are great
\par
opportunities for research in the engineering design
\par
community in the areas of utility theory and preference
\par
representation. There is a need for ways to help the decision
\par
maker contemplate and specify preferences. There is also a
\par
need for ways to represent those preferences accurately,
\par
accounting for possible dependencies and nonlinearities that
\par
may exist among the attributes.
