@article{williamsTechnicalChallengesApplication2016,
  title = {Technical Challenges in the Application of Adaptive Management},
  author = {Williams, Byron K. and Brown, Eleanor D.},
  year = 2016,
  journal = {Biological Conservation},
  volume = {195},
  pages = {255--263},
  publisher = {Elsevier},
  doi = {10.1016/j.biocon.2016.01.012},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Biological Conservation 195 (2016) 255--263
\par
Contents lists available at ScienceDirect
\par
Biological Conservation
\par
j o u r n a l h o m e p a g e : w w w . e l s e v i e r . c o m / l o c a t e / b i o c
\par
Technical challenges in the application of adaptive management
\par
Byron K. Williamsa, Eleanor D. Brown b
\par
a The Wildlife Society, 5410 Grosvenor Lane, Suite 200, Bethesda, MD 20814, USA
\par
b Science and Decisions Center, U.S. Geological Survey, 12201 Sunrise Valley Drive, Reston, VA 20192, USA
\par
a r t i c l e i n f o
\par
a b s t r a c t
\par
Article history:
\par
Received 8 July 2015
\par
Received in revised form 4 January 2016
\par
Accepted 18 January 2016
\par
Available online 30 January 2016
\par
Keywords:
\par
Adaptive management
\par
Learning
\par
Nonstationarity
\par
Resilience
\par
Uncertainty
\par
Value of information
\par
Adaptive management is an approach for simultaneously managing and learning about natural resources, by
\par
acknowledging uncertainty and seeking to reduce it through the process of management itself. Adaptive decision
\par
making can be applied to pressing issues in conservation biology such as species reintroduction, disease and in-
\par
vasive species control, and habitat restoration, as well as to management of natural resources in general. After
\par
briefly outlining a framework and process for adaptive management, we focus on an overview of the key techni-
\par
cal issues related to problem framing and the ability of resource managers to learn from their experience. These
\par
technical issues include the treatment of uncertainty and its propagation over time; nonstationarity in long-term
\par
environmental trends; the applicability of adaptive management across scales; requirements for models and
\par
management alternatives that promote learning; the value of the information produced with adaptive manage-
\par
ment; the challenge to management of uncertainty and surprise; and institutional (social) learning. To accommo-
\par
date these and other challenges that are now coming into focus, the learning-based approach of adaptive
\par
management will need to be adjusted and expanded in the future.
\par
Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license
\par
(http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Adaptive management, an approach that involves the dual pursuit of
\par
management and learning, has been a part of natural resources manage-
\par
ment for many decades. In natural resources, adaptive management
\par
means learning by doing (managing), and altering management activities
\par
to reflect new information (learning) (Walters and Holling, 1990). Learn-
\par
ing in adaptive management occurs through the practice of management
\par
itself, with adjustments to decision making as understanding improves.
\par
For many resource management problems, the use of management in
\par
an experimental, learning-oriented context is the best, and may be the
\par
only, way to gain the understanding needed to manage more effectively
\par
(Williams et al., 2007). For many systems such as ecological communities
\par
or species populations undergoing rapid change, managers often cannot
\par
wait for thorough knowledge. Adaptive management can be used to
\par
move forward with a strategy of action that articulates and systematically
\par
reduces the multiple aspects of uncertainty that managers face. Though
\par
there are of course other approaches to management, some of which
\par
may be useful under specific circumstances (Williams et al., 2002), adap-
\par
tive management serves to fill a need for smart management in the face of
\par
uncertainty.
\par
Advances in decision analytic theory and methodology since Holling's
\par
(1978) and Walters and Hilborn's (1978) early studies of adaptive man-
\par
agement of natural resources have led to a proliferation of applications
\par
in the conservation of animal and plant species over the last decade.
\par
E-mail address: ebrown@usgs.gov (E.D. Brown).
\par
There are by now a great many cases in the literature that document
\par
the use of an adaptive approach to resource management. The following
\par
examples are by no means exhaustive, but serve to highlight the range
\par
of real-world applications and issues that can be addressed through adap-
\par
tive decision making. For example, studies have involved threatened and
\par
endangered species, like Mead's milkweed (Asclepias meadii) (Moore
\par
et al., 2011a), red-cockaded woodpeckers (Picoides borealis) (Moore and
\par
Conroy, 2006), red knots (Calidris canutus rufa) (McGowan et al., 2011),
\par
and endemic fishes in the Tallapoosa River (Irwin and Freeman, 2002).
\par
Runge (2011) discussed adaptive management for specific issues such
\par
as species recovery and regulatory listing under the U.S. Endangered Spe-
\par
cies Act. Other studies have focused on particular elements of the decision
\par
process, for example, population modeling in wildlife management
\par
(reviewed by Lahoz-Monfort et al., 2014) and the central role of monitor-
\par
ing (Lyons et al., 2010) and science--management partnerships (Moore
\par
et al., 2011b) in managing habitats. Methodological issues are also at
\par
the forefront, such as the role and degree of experimentation in active
\par
adaptive management. For example, Parkes et al. (2006) experimented
\par
with competing models of control of invasive vertebrates in New
\par
Zealand, Runge (2013) examined how optimal decisions could change
\par
over the course of a simulated reintroduction of griffon vultures (Gyps
\par
fulvus), and Rout et al. (2014) considered tradeoffs among prevention,
\par
search, and eradication of an invasive species. McCarthy and
\par
Possingham (2007) demonstrated optimization of decisions with a case
\par
study of native plant revegetation in Australia, and McDonald-Madden
\par
et al. (2010) used this method to develop a framework for accelerated
\par
learning about populations of the Tasmanian devil (Sarcophilus harrisii),
\par
http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2016.01.012
\par
0006-3207/Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).256 B.K. Williams, E.D. Brown / Biological Conservation 195 (2016) 255--263
\par
a species threatened by an emergent fatal infectious disease. Climate
\par
change is another major theme, and many researchers have focused
\par
on dealing with uncertainties driven by changing climate patterns.
\par
For example, adaptive approaches were outlined by Conroy et al.
\par
(2011) to mitigate impacts on mid-montane bird communities in
\par
the southeastern U.S., by Martin et al. (2011a) to manage fresh
\par
water to benefit Florida manatees (Trichechus manatus latirostris), and
\par
by McDonald-Madden et al. (2011) to determine optimal timing of spe-
\par
cies translocations.
\par
Nearly all definitions of adaptive management emphasize uncertainty,
\par
its integration into a decision making framework, and its reduction
\par
through management itself. We use the definition of adaptive man-
\par
agement given by Williams et al. (2007): ``Adaptive management is
\par
a systematic approach for improving resource management by
\par
learning from management outcomes.'' Learning and adaptation is
\par
an ongoing process in which learning is used to improve manage-
\par
ment, and management is used to investigate the resource system
\par
(Williams and Brown, 2014). In some cases management interven-
\par
tions can be designed as experimental``treatments.'' Nonetheless,
\par
the ultimate focus is management, and learning is valued for its con-
\par
tribution to better management (Walters, 1986). Adaptive manage-
\par
ment is distinguished from more common forms of decision making
\par
like``trial and error,'' scenario planning, hedging, and other forms of
\par
non-adaptive management that do not focus on uncertainty and its
\par
reduction (Williams et al., 2007).
\par
In this paper, we provide an overview of emerging technical issues in
\par
adaptive management, bringing together these growing points in its im-
\par
plementation for the first time. Our focus is on the current state of think-
\par
ing about adaptive management and some important issues facing this
\par
approach in the future, rather than on a retrospective of past develop-
\par
ments. A number of technical matters in adaptive decision making have
\par
recently become apparent, not only in various areas of natural resource
\par
management but also in operations research, information theory, systems
\par
analysis, and other fields. We hope that by relating these outstanding is-
\par
sues to the practice of adaptive management, their application in biolog-
\par
ical conservation will be advanced.
\par
We first briefly describe adaptive management processes and their
\par
context, including the treatment of uncertainty and its propagation over
\par
time. We then use this framework and context to develop a series of
\par
emerging issues that we believe pose serious challenges to the approach.
\par
These include nonstationarity of long-term environmental trends; the ap-
\par
plicability of adaptive management across scales; attributes of models
\par
and management alternatives that promote learning; the value of the in-
\par
formation produced with adaptive management; management for resil-
\par
ience and sustainability; and institutional (social) learning. Such issues
\par
can be relevant to adaptive decision making for many natural resources,
\par
including ecological systems, plant and animal species, populations, or
\par
habitats, and in this paper we use the generic term``resource'' to refer
\par
to them.
\par
2. Context for adaptive management
\par
Applications of adaptive management typically involve systems char-
\par
acterized by uncertainty and change over time (Fig. 1). Common features
\par
(Williams and Brown, 2014) are: 1) system changes in response to fluctu-
\par
ating environmental conditions and management actions; 2) environ-
\par
mental variation that induces stochasticity in biological and ecological
\par
processes, leading to unpredictable system behaviors; 3) periodic and
\par
potentially varying management interventions to influence system
\par
behaviors either directly or indirectly; and 4) limitation of effective
\par
management by uncertainty about the resource system and how it
\par
responds, such that reducing this uncertainty can lead to improved
\par
management. Most applications involve a systematic approach to
\par
management, including problem framing and the use of formal decision
\par
analysis; statistical tools such as Bayesian updating (Lee, 1989), Markov
\par
processes (Puterman, 1994), and stochastic dynamic programming to
