\par
A key to adaptive management is the recognition and treatment of
\par
uncertainty. Here we emphasize four uncertainty factors that affect nat-
\par
ural resources, namely environmental variation, partial controllability,
\par
partial observability, and structural uncertainty (Williams, 2011a).
\par
These uncertainties influence resource management in different ways
\par
and at different points in a resource system (Fig. 1), and in combination
\par
they can restrict our management ability.
\par
2.1. Environmental variation
\par
Environmental conditions can be viewed as external factors that in-
\par
fluence, but are not influenced by, resource conditions and dynamics.
\par
Examples include precipitation patterns, temperature regimes, ambient
\par
light conditions and other measures, as well as extremes in these condi-
\par
tions. Fluctuations in the environment can be treated as if they vary
\par
randomly over time, and interact with landscape changes that co-occur.
\par
It often is useful to include unrecognized landscape heterogeneity and un-
\par
predictable human impacts on the landscape as a part of``environmental
\par
variation.''
\par
Environmental conditions combine with demographic stochasticity
\par
and other factors to induce random variation in the state transitions in
\par
Eq. (1). These transitions can be conveniently represented by a proba-
\par
