2.3. Partial observability
\par
Confidence increases for models that make accurate forecasts of
\par
resource status, and confidence declines for models that do not make
\par
accurate forecasts. Unlike environmental variation (and in some cases
\par
partial controllability), which are effectively uncontrolled, structural
\par
uncertainty can be reduced with management that targets learning.
\par
We note that approaches such as experimentation with analysis of var-
\par
iance statistics, ad hoc metrics that measure distances between and
\par
among models (Williams et al., 2002), and other informal procedures
\par
also could be used to update model confidence values.
\par
Partial observability expresses the inability to observe completely
\par
the resource system that is being managed. For example, only a part
\par
of the area where a fish population occurs can be monitored, and
\par
individuals (e.g., plants and animals) often escape detection even
\par
in areas that are intensively monitored. As a result, observations
\par
are associated with, but not the same as, actual system states. Partial
\par
observability obscures the resource status on which effective
\par
management depends, which reduces management effectiveness
\par
even if environmental variation is minimal and management actions
\par
are precisely controlled.
\par
There are several ways partial observability can be addressed. One is
\par
to estimate resource status with field data, and then treat the estimate
\par
as if it accurately represents resource conditions. Another is to state
\par
the uncertainty about resource status explicitly with probabilities for
\par
possible resource states, and incorporate them directly into the
\par
decision-making process (Williams, 2009). Here we represent partial
\par
observability with a distribution bt of resource states at each time,
\par
where bt(x) denotes the probability or``belief'' that the resource
\par
state is x at time t. This distribution, which often is referred to as a
\par
belief state (Kaelbling et al., 1998), changes over time as actions
\par
are taken, the resource responds, and monitoring data are collected
\par
and used to update resource status (Regan et al., 2011; Chad\`es et al.,
\par
2008, 2011).
\par
3. Adaptive decision making
\par
A framework for adaptive decision making can be characterized as a
\par
two-phase process, which we briefly recapitulate from Williams and
\par
Brown (2014) (Fig. 2). A deliberative phase involves the framing of
\par
the resource problem in terms of stakeholders, objectives, management
\par
alternatives, models (including metrics for the confidence one places on
\par
them), and monitoring protocols. An iterative phase uses these
\par
elements in an ongoing cycle of technical learning about system
\par
structure and function, and resource management based on what
\par
is learned. Finally, institutional learning (a form of social learning
\par
[Pahl-Wostl, 2009]) about the decision process is obtained by peri-
\par
odically interrupting the iterative cycle of technical learning to re-
\par
consider project objectives, management alternatives, stakeholder
\par
engagement, and other elements of the deliberative phase (Fig. 2).
\par
The institutional learning cycle complements, but differs from, the
\par
cycle of technical learning.
\par
