\par
Adaptive management is usually framed in terms of an (often un-
\par
stated) assumption that the processes influencing resource dynamics
\par
are stable over the management time frame, in that patterns of uncon-
\par
trolled fluctuations change little in their overall direction or range of
\par
variation (Williams and Brown, 2012). Approaches to system analysis
\par
and control, including the framework typically used in adaptive decision
\par
making, have traditionally rested on that assumption of stability. That is,
\par
the transition functions f(xt, at, et) and transition probability structures
\par
P(xt+ 1 \textbar{} xt, at) are assumed to be fixed over time, with the recognition
\par
that the system can fluctuate randomly because of environmental
\par
drivers and other stochastic factors.
\par
An increasingly important need is to extend the framework for adap-
\par
tive decision making to incorporate nonstationarity, i.e., to allow for de-
\par
cision making when system dynamics do not exhibit stationary patterns
\par
as described above. For a great many resource systems, the ecological
\par
structures and processes controlling resource dynamics are changing
\par
in ways not fully expressed by the standard stationarity assumptions.
\par
For example, environmental conditions and the ecological processes in-
\par
fluenced by them are exhibiting directional patterns of change. An obvi-
\par
ous example is climate change, in which environmental variables such
\par
as temperature and precipitation change directionally and cause sys-
\par
temic change in resource dynamics. Large-scale human actions on the
\par
landscape can also produce systemic change. A simple way to denote
\par
such nonstationarity is to include a temporal index to characterize sys-
\par
tem transitions. The transition function then becomes ft(xt, at, et), where
\par
the functional index indicates that the function can change over time,
\par
i.e., is nonstationary. In like manner, transition probabilities can be de-
\par
noted by P(xt+1 \textbar{} xt, at; t) to indicate nonstationarity. In terms of adaptive
\par
management, the models used to represent uncertainty can be denoted
\par
by Pk(xt+1 \textbar{} xt, at; t).
\par
Nonstationarity is a newly recognized and serious challenge to adap-
\par
tive decision making, one for which we need new approaches that go be-
\par
yond the standard framing of learning-based management. The cycle of
\par
learning becomes more difficult when the subjects of investigation-- the
\par
ecological processes that determine resource change-- are themselves
\par
changing.B.K. Williams, E.D. Brown / Biological Conservation 195 (2016) 255--263
