5. Spatial and ecological scale
\par
One concern in applications of adaptive management is the appropri-
\par
ate scale for decision making. Adaptive management is often associated
\par
with big, complex ecosystem-wide applications, such as large-river man-
\par
agement (Columbia, Platte, and Missouri Rivers [Quigley and Arbelbide,
\par
1997; Wissmar, and Bisson, 2003; Williams, 2006; Freeman, 2010];
\par
Glen Canyon Dam on the Colorado River [U.S. Geological Survey, 2008]);
\par
continental waterfowl harvest management (Williams and Johnson,
\par
1995; Williams, 2006); commercial fisheries (Hilborn, 1992; Conover
\par
and Munch, 2002); pest management in forest ecosystems (Shea et al.,
\par
2002); and water management (Everglades [Holling et al., 1994]).
\par
Ecosystem management at this scale involves economic, social,
\par
institutional, and ecological linkages across large landscapes with
\par
high degrees of heterogeneity.
\par
However, adaptive decision making as we describe it here applies
\par
equally well to local issues, as long as the basic conditions are met
\par
(e.g., Williams et al., 2007). Moore et al. (2011b) provide a conservation
\par
example involving prairie and wetland habitat restoration on national
\par
wildlife refuges. There probably are many more potential applications
\par
of adaptive management at local versus larger scales, not only because
\par
of the prevalence of such problems but also because they can often be
\par
framed more easily, their uncertainties can be identified more readily,
\par
stakeholder involvement can be facilitated more directly, and man-
\par
agement can often be implemented more easily (McConnaha and
\par
Paquet, 1996).
\par
Whatever its scale, the careful framing of a decision problem
\par
often requires a combination of both larger and smaller scales than
\par
the focal scale of decision making (Holling, 2001), to capture scale-
\par
specific factors that influence resource dynamics. Broader and more inclu-
\par
sive scales also are needed when conservation actions conflict with
\par
other socio-economic goals. With large spatial scales a problem
\par
can arise in linking conservation decisions that are applied locally,
\par
and the challenge is to recognize the circumstances when such deci-
\par
sions must be explicitly linked, and when they can be handled inde-
\par
pendently. Though adaptive management provides a framework
\par
and general approach for problems at different scales, these and
\par
other scale-specific issues can arise that can make its systematic im-
\par
plementation problematic for complex systems.
\par
6. Models, management alternatives, and learning
\par
Here we explore the roles of models and management alterna-
\par
tives on the potential for learning and learning rate in adaptive
\par
management. Smart decision making with adaptive management
\par
is facilitated by learning, which in turn is facilitated by the presence
\par
of variation in model performance as well as variation in decision
\par
impacts. To aid in the identification of smart management strate-
\par
gies, alternative actions should generate distinctly different predic-
\par
tions. Similarly, learning is promoted when the models under
\par
consideration lead to different predictions of resource conditions
\par
over time. Put simply, adaptive decision making works best when
\par
there is variation in the predictions across models for particular ac-
\par
tions, and variation across actions for particular models.
\par
The effect on learning of similar responses can be seen in the Bayesian
\par
updating of model weights qt(k) in the model state. Assume that the tran-
\par
sition probability structures across the models representing different
\par
Thus, the absence of differences in model performance means there
\par
can be no reduction of structural uncertainty, i.e., no learning. Basically,
\par
learning as represented by the change in model weights does not occur
\par
under these conditions.
\par
A special case of adaptive decision making treats the management
\par
alternatives themselves as hypotheses (Williams, 2011a), and examines
\par
them using experimental design and hypothesis-testing procedures
\par
(Graybill, 1976). Alternatively, when interventions are carried out se-
\par
quentially one can compare monitoring data against predictions for
\par
each alternative to update confidence in the alternatives. One common
\par
method is to update confidence weights at each decision point by com-
\par
paring predicted responses with post-decision monitoring data
\par
(Williams et al., 2002), with the change in the weights leading gradually
\par
to a recognition of the best intervention. The relevance of Bayesian
\par
updating for conservation applications is shown by a few examples,
\par
such as optimal control of human disturbance of nesting golden eagles
\par
(Aquila chrysaetos) (Martin et al., 2009, Martin et al., 2011b), dam re-
\par
lease prescriptions to protect aquatic habitats (Irwin and Freeman,
\par
2002), and native plant revegetation strategies in Australia (McCarthy
\par
and Possingham, 2007).
\par
7. Monitoring
\par
Monitoring plays a critical role in adaptive management in allowing
\par
the comparison of model-based predictions and estimated responses to
\par
facilitate learning. In fact, monitoring programs provide data for four
\par
key purposes: 1) evaluation of progress toward achieving objectives;
\par
2) determination of resource status, in order to identify appropriate
\par
management actions; 3) learning about resource dynamics via the
\par
comparison of predictions against survey data; and 4) development
\par
and enhancement of models of resource dynamics as needed (Williams
\par
et al., 2007).
\par
In consideration of its critical role in any adaptive management ap-
\par
proach, monitoring needs to be well-designed and ongoing over the
\par
life of an adaptive management project. Some situations that challenge
\par
these conditions are the following.
\par
{$\bullet$} The frequency of monitoring cannot keep pace with changes in the
\par
natural system.
\par
{$\bullet$} A design for experimental management and monitoring cannot be
\par
developed to test hypotheses, either because understanding of the
\par
resource system is too limited or management is too constrained to
\par
design a meaningful experiment.
\par
{$\bullet$} There is not a firm commitment to funding and institutional support
\par
for monitoring for the duration of the learning effort. Though fun-
\par
damental to adaptive management, monitoring is often the first
\par
function to be curtailed by some administrators and managers
\par
due to budget pressures.
\par
It may be that with some limited re-orientation and re-designing, an
\par
extant monitoring effort can prove useful in meeting the purposes of
\par
adaptive decision making. The challenge is to integrate monitoring
\par
into the management framework in such a way that it is seen as critical
\par
rather than optional. Examples of the crucial role of monitoring in adap-
\par
tive management applications for conservation have been provided by260 many authors, such as Conroy et al. (2011), Lyons et al. (2010) and
\par
Nichols and Williams (2006).
\par
8. Valuing information
\par
A natural question that arises in adaptive decision making concerns
\par
the value of the added information produced. Though the concept of a
\par
measurable value for information has been recognized for several de-
\par
cades (Raiffa and Schlaifer, 1961; Yokota and Thompson, 2004), more
\par
recently the``value of information'' has begun to surface with increasing
\par
frequency in conservation, for example in considering costs and benefits
\par
associated with obtaining information from particular sampling tech-
\par
niques, or with resolving particular sources of uncertainty. Conservation
\par
examples that incorporate analysis of the value of information include
\par
reintroduction of whooping cranes (Grus Americana), as discussed by
\par
Runge et al. (2011); recovery of the Florida scrub-jay (Aphelocoma
\par
coerulescens), as discussed by Williams et al. (2011); and management
\par
of pink-footed geese (Anser brachyrhynchus), as discussed by Williams
\par
and Johnson (2015a) and Johnson et al. (2014). In simple terms, the
\par
value of information represents a potential increase in value resulting
\par
from better information to guide management. If we assume an ongoing
\par
if imperfect monitoring effort that informs decision making, the value of
\par
information is effectively an indicator of the marginal value of additional
\par
monitoring.
\par
In the context of structural uncertainty, an intuitive way to under-
\par
stand the value of information is as a comparison of aggregate utility
\par
that could be produced if the resource system were fully known, against
\par
the aggregate utility produced in the presence of uncertainty (Williams
\par
and Johnson, 2015b). The latter expression corresponds to decision
\par
making under structural uncertainty, i.e., adaptive management. In
\par
that sense the adaptive decision framework serves as a platform for
\par
the value of information in iterative decision making. Variations of the
\par
value of information include the expected value of partial information,
\par
which accounts for the increased value accruing to the elimination of
\par
uncertainty from some but not all sources, and the expected value of
\par
sample information, which accounts for the gain in value with addition-
\par
al sampling. In both these situations, valuation through adaptive deci-
\par
sion making serves as the baseline to assess the improvement of value
\par
with reduced uncertainty (Yokota and Thompson, 2004; Williams and
\par
Johnson, 2015a).
\par
9. Surprise, resilience, and flexibility
\par
Natural resources management is always vulnerable to a``discon-
\par
nect'' between the ecosystem behaviors we expect and those that actu-
\par
ally occur (Gunderson, 1999). Surprise can never be eliminated, no
\par
matter how learning-based and carefully framed resource management
\par
is. Surprise and associated concerns with uncertainty and resilience are
\par
central issues in an expansive literature that comes under the rubric of
\par
``resilience thinking'' (Gunderson and Holling, 2002; Walker and Salt,
\par
2006; Scheffer, 2009). According to resilience thinking, natural systems
\par
are subject not only to reversible short-term change, but also to long-
\par
term change that is effectively irreversible. A developing theory of resil-
\par
ience emphasizes``stability basins'' for ecological conditions, and
\par
thresholds beyond which reversible change within a zone is unlikely
\par
once a threshold is crossed. Adaptive management can play an impor-
\par
tant role in accumulating the knowledge needed to manage for
\par
resilience-based objectives over time and improve long-term resource
\par
viability (Zellmer and Gunderson, 2009).
\par
One conclusion of resilience thinking is that when management
\par
focuses on only one or a few ecosystem attributes, a result is the loss
\par
of resilience and an increased vulnerability to unexpected and
\par
destructive change (Johnson et al., 2013). Well-known examples
\par
include the intensive management of grazing, which can increase the
\par
vulnerability of grasslands to drought (Walker and Salt, 2006); and
\par
intensive management of commercial fishing, which can lead to the
\par
B.K. Williams, E.D. Brown / Biological Conservation 195 (2016) 255--263
\par
unexpected collapse of a commercial fishery (Walters, 1986). Surprises
\par
like these usually are a result of managing in ways that induce stability
\par
in targeted ecosystem components in the short term but result in the
\par
loss of ecosystem resilience over the long term, and an increase in the
\par
vulnerability of the system to extreme and often unwanted changes
\par
(Williams and Brown, 2014).
\par
Williams and Brown (2012) identified some steps that can be taken
\par
to deal with surprise in the management of ecosystems, as follows.
\par
{$\bullet$} Recognize that in any managed ecosystem uncertainty and the poten-
\par
tial for surprise are implicit in the scenarios under consideration.
\par
{$\bullet$} Incorporate models that are based on broadly differing assumptions,
\par
with broadly differing predictions.
\par
{$\bullet$} Retain enough management flexibility to adapt to surprise when it
\par
occurs.
\par
{$\bullet$} Manage the system for sufficient resilience to maintain structure and
\par
function when external shocks occur.
\par
{$\bullet$} Increase the range of ecosystem conditions, management alternatives,
\par
and sources of evidence that are considered.
\par
{$\bullet$} Use experimental management and monitoring to learn and manage
\par
adaptively.
\par
10. Institutional learning
\par
Adaptive management involves not only the potential for technical
\par
learning about a natural resource system and how it responds to man-
\par
agement interventions, it also involves the potential to learn about the
\par
decision making process itself. In this sense there are really two learning
\par
cycles in adaptive management, with the technical learning cycle nested
\par
within a larger cycle of institutional learning (Fig. 3). A typical situation
\par
involves multiple iterations of the technical learning cycle during which
\par
the institutional framework remains more or less unchanged, followed
\par
by a break in technical learning to revisit and potentially restructure
\par
some of the institutional elements (Williams and Brown, 2014). Togeth-
\par
er the two cycles are referred to as double-loop learning (Argyris and
\par
Shon, 1978). Pahl-Wostl (2009) expanded this model to include three
\par
cycles, by distinguishing socio-political and governance aspects of
\par
stakeholder involvement as yet another cycle. In combination the
\par
three cycles are held to address three different questions:``Are we
\par
doing things right? (technical learning loop);``Are we doing the right
\par
things?'' (process learning loop); and``Who has the rights?'' (socio-
\par
political and governance loop) (Johnson et al., 2015). Here we restrict
\par
our further discussion to double-loop learning, with governance issues
\par
folded into the institutional learning cycle.
\par
Fig. 3. Adaptive management displayed as cycles of technical and institutional learning.
\par
The implementation component refers to implementation of a designed process based
\par
on problem assessment, which then is used to initiate technical learning. Solid lines
\par
represent the complete adaptive cycle, involving deliberative and iterative elements as
\par
well as institutional learning. The dashed line indicates feedback defining the technical
\par
learning cycle.B.K. Williams, E.D. Brown / Biological Conservation 195 (2016) 255--263
\par
261
\par
Because of the dynamic nature of governance, the need to revisit and
\par
adjust the process and governance elements of adaptive management
\par
often becomes more pressing as adaptive management proceeds over
\par
time. Stakeholder perspectives and values can and do change over
\par
time, as previously unanticipated patterns in resource dynamics are ex-
\par
posed and social and cultural values and norms change. Any of these
\par
changes can produce a need to adjust objectives, alternatives, and
\par
other set-up elements.
\par
Institutional learning sometimes can serve a critical role in helping
\par
to overcome disagreements among stakeholders about management
\par
objectives, alternatives, and projected management consequences. It is
\par
much easier to agree to move forward with a particular management
\par
strategy if it is understood that objectives, management alternatives,
\par
and the other elements of decision making can be reviewed and
\par
renegotiated as new evidence about management performance be-
\par
comes available. Institutional learning offers an incentive to stake-
\par
holders to agree on an initial approach that involves compromise on
\par
all sides. In a conservation example, Irwin and Kennedy (2008) found
\par
this to be the case during stakeholder negotiations for an initial dam-
\par
release prescription to conserve aquatic habitats of fishes endemic to
\par
the Tallapoosa River (Irwin and Freeman, 2002). Without such a possi-
\par
bility, on the other hand, negotiation to establish a fixed and permanent
\par
approach leaves all parties more entrenched in their positions because
\par
they believe the outcome can't be changed.
\par
Challenges in institutional learning include the identification of
\par
criteria for when to break out of the technical learning cycle and revisit
\par
the decision process elements. If the revisitation is too frequent, the
\par
effects of change at the technical and institutional levels become con-
\par
founded, significantly slowing the rate of learning for both. If it is too in-
\par
frequent, there is a risk of the loss of commitment of stakeholders as
\par
values change, alternatives are marginalized, models cease to perform
\par
effectively in predicting system dynamics, objectives lose their rele-
\par
vance, etc.
\par
An additional challenge is how to address potential changes in pro-
\par
cess elements once they are revisited. One of the biggest challenges in
\par
institutional learning involves the identification and updating of objec-
\par
tives, which play a crucial role in driving decisions and evaluating their
\par
consequences. Yet it is not uncommon for stakeholders to have very dif-
\par
ferent ideas about what attributes are important and how to measure
\par
their importance over time, so that finding common ground is difficult
\par
in the first place, and a process by which to reconsider and possibly ad-
\par
just previously agreed-upon objectives on the basis of experience and
\par
performance is an even bigger challenge. There have been a few at-
\par
tempts to formalize a learning process for objectives and other elements
\par
(Williams, 2012), but much more needs to be done.
\par
11. Discussion
\par
Adaptive management recognizes uncertainties about resource pro-
\par
cesses and their responses to management, and emphasizes the track-
\par
ing and reduction of uncertainty through management on the basis of
\par
what is learned over time. In this paper we have highlighted some of
\par
the challenges in the implementation of adaptive management, build-
\par
ing on a framework that recognizes both an architecture for decision
\par
making and a learning process that folds directly into management.
\par
Learning is seen as improvements in technical understanding that aim
\par
at biological processes, and institutional improvements that aim at the
\par
architectural elements of decision making. We have used this frame-
\par
work to explore a number of practical challenges to adaptive decision
\par
making.
\par
In terms of the institutional context of learning, we believe that iden-
\par
tifying objectives and getting stakeholders to buy in constitute two of
\par
the biggest challenges in adaptive management. Stakeholders often
\par
have different ideas about what importance to assign to system attri-
\par
butes. We do not address this issue to any great extent here, and simply
\par
note that there is a great wealth of information and advice in the social
\par
literature about how to achieve consensus among stakeholders with
\par
disparate views. Here we have focused primarily on structural uncer-
\par
tainty, since its reduction is a distinctive point of emphasis in adaptive
\par
decision making.
\par
We note that the framework for adaptive management can also
\par
accommodate other sources of uncertainty, and in particular partial ob-
\par
servability. In fact, there are many similarities in the treatments of par-
\par
tial observability and structural uncertainty (Williams, 2009, 2011b).
\par
For example, both problems involve the averaging of transitions and
\par
utilities across an uncertainty attribute, and both use value functions
\par
that incorporate present and expected future utilities. The key differ-
\par
ences between them are the nature of the uncertainty attribute, and
\par
the way uncertainty is handled in valuation. Each problem is subject
\par
to its own form of uncertainty, either about process functions and pa-
\par
rameters or about system status. With structural uncertainty the system
\par
state is observed, but the process structure is only partially known and
\par
must be characterized with time-specific model probabilities. Converse-
\par
ly, with partial observability it is the system structure that is known, but
\par
the system state is only partially observed and must be characterized
\par
with time-specific state probabilities.
\par
There are other important differences that must be accounted for as
\par
well. Under partial observability valuation is conditioned on belief state
\par
(Eq. (4)), whereas under structural uncertainty it is conditioned on both
\par
resource and model states (Eq. (3)). On the other hand, the inclusion of
\par
stochastic observations under partial observability results in a more
\par
complicated transition structure than is the case with structural uncer-
\par
tainty. Which of these situations is more difficult to handle no doubt de-
\par
pends on the process structure and stochastic components.
\par
As mentioned earlier, a significant challenge with adaptive manage-
\par
ment is to integrate multiple sources of uncertainty into decision making,
\par
and to assess their interactions. This challenge is made more difficult
\par
when one seeks to identify optimal strategies (Williams, 2009), and the
\par
challenge is greater still in the presence of nonstationarity. In order to
\par
contain costs and maximize value with adaptive decision making, a
\par
good deal more thinking will need to go into designing adaptive manage-
\par
ment projects that successfully deal with multiple sources of uncertainty.
\par
A technical assessment of the value of the information may contribute to
\par
improved project design, but a low value should not be misinterpreted as
\par
meaning that there is little value in using an adaptive approach to man-
\par
agement. It should be recognized that low values are often recorded in
\par
conservation and natural resources applications (Walters, 1986; Moore
\par
and McCarthy, 2010; Johnson et al., 2014), and this information metric
\par
is not the only, and possibly not even the most relevant, measure of
\par
value for the decision framework. Among other things, a systematic and
\par
structured accounting of the elements of decision making can serve as a
\par
mechanism for collaboration and shared decision making, lowering the
\par
potential contentiousness and conflict among stakeholders. The value of
\par
information can certainly contribute to, but should not obscure, these
\par
and other benefits accruing to a structured, adaptive process of decision
\par
making.
\par
12. Conclusions
\par
It has been a long road in making adaptive management legitimate
\par
and useful as a paradigm to guide natural resources decision making,
\par
and in particular decision making about biodiversity conservation
\par
(Runge, 2011). At this point there is a well-developed framework and
\par
process with which to recognize and characterize uncertainties, and re-
\par
duce them through the use of management itself. But a changing cli-
\par
mate, widespread landscape change, accelerating biodiversity loss, and
\par
changing cultural values expand the context for resource management
\par
and create new problems for the application of an adaptive approach
\par
to management.
\par
In many ways these challenges are generic problems for the 21st
\par
century, and they apply to the management of many urgent conservation
\par
problems. Together they increase the difficulties faced by practitioners in262 making smart decisions in the face of new problems. Adaptive man-
\par
agement provides a context and model for addressing many of these
\par
challenges, and in some cases it may be the only way forward for
\par
their resolution. However, the adaptive management paradigm
\par
will need to be adjusted and expanded to accommodate the new is-
\par
sues that are only now coming into focus.
