1Abstract
\par
In designing economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, analysts are frequently faced with
\par
alternative methods of collecting the same data, the extremes being top-down (``gross
\par
costing'') and bottom-up (``micro-costing'') approaches.
\par
A priori, bottom-up approaches may be considered superior to top-down but are also more
\par
expensive to collect and analyse. In this paper, we use value of information analysis to
\par
estimate the efficient mix of observations on each method in a proposed clinical trial.
\par
By assigning a prior bivariate distribution to the two data collection processes, the predicted
\par
posterior (i.e. preposterior) mean and variance of the superior process can be calculated
\par
from proposed samples using either process. This is then used to calculate the preposterior
\par
mean and variance of incremental net benefit and hence the expected net gain of sampling.
\par
We apply this method to a previously collected dataset to estimate the value of conducting a
\par
further trial and identifying the optimal mix of observations on drug costs at two 'levels': by
\par
individual item (``process A'') and by drug class (``process B''). We find that substituting a
\par
number of observations on process A for process B leads to a modest \pounds 35,000 increase in
\par
expected net gain of sampling (ENGS). Drivers of the results are the correlation between the
\par
two processes and their relative cost.
\par
This method has potential use following a pilot study, to inform efficient data collection
\par
approaches for a subsequent full-scale trial. It provides a formal quantitative approach to
\par
inform trialists whether it is efficient to collect resource use data on all patients in a trial, on
\par
a subset of patients only, or to collect limited data on most and detailed data on a subset.
\par
21.\textbf{ }Introduction
\par
In designing economic evaluations alongside randomised controlled trials, analysts are faced
\par
with alternative methods to collect the same data. For example, hospitalisation costs can be
\par
estimated using time-and-motion studies and detailed measurement of all drugs dispensed,
\par
tests conducted and appropriate allocation of overhead costs; alternatively they can be
\par
approximated on a per-admission or per bed-day basis. Drug costs can be estimated by
\par
quantifying exact consumption of every drug by every patient; alternatively they can be
\par
approximated based on recorded prescriptions of a particular drug or drug class and
\par
assumptions over dose and frequency.
\par
The two extremes are known as top-down or gross costing and bottom-up or micro-costing.
\par
The choice can, to a certain extent, be determined by the study question. For example, in an
\par
economic evaluation comparing two surgical procedures, it would be appropriate to micro-
\par
cost the index procedure. However, resources may only permit a more top-down approach
\par
to costing other elements such as post-operative length of stay, readmission etc. Indeed,
\par
the considerable effort required to accurately measure and value resource use has long
\par
been recognised,(1) and some quite dramatic reductions in the cost of projects as a result of
\par
careful scrutiny of trial logistics have been documented.(2, 3)
\par
There are numerous examples of comparisons of alternative approaches to collecting the
\par
same data (e.g.(4-13)), but very few attempts to quantify the cost-effectiveness of one
\par
approach compared with another, and thus to judge when a detailed approach is warranted
\par
or whether a more approximate approach is sufficient for purpose, releasing scarce research
\par
funds for greater benefit elsewhere. One study that did consider the cost-effectiveness of
\par
research focused on a comparison of a prospective RCT versus a retrospective study
\par
design,(14) rather than on collecting specific (resource use) elements within a proposed RCT.
\par
In this paper we present an adaptation of the principles of value of information analysis (15-
\par
18) to compare the expected return on investment from collecting data using one process
\par
compared with another. The scenario we analyse is where a bottom-up data collection
\par
process is considered a priori superior to a top-down process, but is also more expensive.
\par
We apply the method and set it in context with a previously collected dataset by firstly
\par
predicting whether a repeat of the trial to further reduce decision uncertainty would be
\par
efficient, and if so, the optimal mix of observations on a specific parameter using two data
\par
processes (defined as the mix that maximises the expected return on investment).
\par
32.\textbf{ }Method
\par
In this section we present a narrative explanation of the principles followed by the algebra.
\par
We then describe the analyses to be presented in the results. Appendices detailing all
\par
working are provided as online supplementary material. A Microsoft Excel spreadsheet with
\par
all calculations is available on request from the corresponding author.
\par
2.1 Narrative explanation
\par
2.1.1 Value of information analysis
\par
Value of information analysis is a technique to predict the expected return on investment in
\par
research and is rooted within Bayesian statistical decision theory.(15, 19) The Bayesian
\par
statistical approach of defining a prior and updating it with data to generate a posterior is
\par
known as posterior analysis. Value of information analysis involves predicting the data
\par
based on the prior, which are then combined with the prior to generate a predicted
\par
posterior (or `preposterior') distribution, and so is sometimes known as preposterior
\par
analysis.(15)
\par
Suppose a new intervention is proposed to replace an existing treatment. Whether this
\par
represents an overall increase in health to an economy is determined by the (mean)
\par
incremental net benefit, which we denote {$\Delta$}B. This is a rearrangement of the incremental
\par
cost-effectiveness ratio(20) and if positive, the new intervention should be adopted; if
\par
negative, the existing treatment should be retained. Decision makers are assumed risk
\par
neutral(21) and thus make adoption decisions on expected values only, irrespective of
\par
uncertainty.
\par
Decision uncertainty is represented by the (prior) probability distribution of {$\Delta$}B, denoted
\par
f({$\Delta$}B)0 in Figure 1. The mean is positive so the decision would be to adopt the new
\par
treatment. However, the proportion of the probability mass to the left of the Y-axis shows
\par
that there is a probability (approximately 33\% in this example, the sum of the two shaded
\par
areas) that {$\Delta$}B is negative and a decision to adopt would be wrong. The expected loss due to
\par
uncertainty is approximately the probability of being wrong multiplied by the average
\par
consequence of being wrong, (18)(22) i.e. the absolute value of the area under the f(∆D)0
\par
curve from -{$\infty$} to zero. Equivalently, this is the expected gain from eliminating that
\par
uncertainty, or the expected value of perfect information (EVPI).
\par
New information (e.g. a clinical trial, database analysis or survey) is expected to reduce the
\par
standard error of mean incremental net benefit and so will tighten the distribution around
\par
4the (updated) mean, yielding f({$\Delta$}B)1 in Figure 1. The proportion of the probability mass to
\par
the left of the Y-axis will therefore decrease (dark shaded area) and thus reduce the
\par
expected loss (i.e. the predicted posterior EVPI will be less than the prior EVPI). The
\par
expected reduction in the expected loss is the expected gain from a particular study, or the
\par
expected value of sample information (EVSI). A larger study will provide more information
\par
than a smaller one, but also cost more. The EVSI of a study of sample size n less the
\par
(expected) cost is the expected net gain of sampling (ENGS). The predicted optimal sample
\par
size for a new study, n*, is that which maximises ENGS.
\par
It may be more efficient to concentrate data collection on one or more components of ∆B,
\par
such as health outcomes or some component of cost. To calculate the ENGS of such a study,
\par
the expected reduction in standard error of that component from n observations is followed
\par
through to the expected reduction in standard error of {$\Delta$}B (i.e. the expected reduction in
\par
parameter uncertainty is translated into an expected reduction in decision uncertainty).(18)
\par
The EVSI and ENGS are then calculated as before.
\par
2.1.2 Extension of principles to compare alternative data collection processes
\par
Pratt, Raiffa \& Schlaifer provide an extension to these principles to compare two alternative
\par
data collection processes.(15) We adapt their technique to the healthcare field as follows:
\par
Given a choice between a top-down and bottom-up approach to calculating a component of
\par
{$\Delta$}B (for example incremental cost of drugs), the prior distribution of the two is assumed
\par
bivariate Normal. Note the covariance provides information on the relationship between
\par
the two. Such data could be obtained from a pilot study where both approaches (hereafter
\par
termed `data processes') are observed in the same patient group, a review of the literature,
\par
or elicited from experts (e.g. (23, 24)).
\par
As stated, we assume the bottom-up process is superior to the top-down in that it is a more
\par
accurate measure of cost (that is, it provides the least biased estimate of the mean and the
\par
most appropriate characterisation of the dispersion of individual costs around the mean).
\par
We label the bottom-up process A and the top-down process B. The estimate of (mean
\par
incremental) drug cost yielded from process A, {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$A$} should be used in the calculation of {$\Delta$}B
\par
as it is believed to be a `better' estimate than that yielded from process B, {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$B$} (the
\par
subscript `d' refers to drugs). Specifying a prior bivariate distribution allows one to
\par
determine how belief about {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$A$} should be revised given information on {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$B$} alone, or a
\par
5mix of information on {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$A$} and {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$B$}
\par
. In other words, given a reduction in standard error of
\par
{$\DeltaCd$}
