112.3 Analyses and layout of results
\par
The ultimate objective is to identify the optimal mix of top-down and bottom-up
\par
observations to collect drug costs, that is, the mix that maximises the expected return on
\par
investment, as part of any future study. However we set this in context by also presenting
\par
standard value of information analyses on other components of {$\Delta$}B. This is then broadened
\par
to identify the overall optimal number of observations on each drug cost process, as well as
\par
other parameters (non-drug costs and QALYs gained), thus providing a decision analytic
\par
approach to overall trial design. Therefore, we report the following:
\par
i.\textbf{ }Value of information analysis for a repeat of the subject trial.
\par
Analysis of uncertainty in ∆B and standard value of information analysis (reporting the EVPI,
\par
EVSI and optimal sample size of a trial reporting ∆B and collecting all data on all patients).
\par
ii.\textbf{ }Value of information analysis for studies collecting one component of data alone.
\par
We report analyses pertaining to studies collecting (a) incremental QALYs and (b)
\par
incremental cost alone. We then sub-divide cost into two individual studies collecting (c)
\par
incremental non-drug costs ({$\DeltaCn$} ) and (d) incremental cost of drugs ({$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$A$}) alone.
\par
iii.\textbf{ }Comparison of value of alternative data collection processes on drug costs.
\par
This is the key analysis of the paper. Here we introduce the top-down `process B' for
\par
