154. Discussion
\par
4.1 Implications of results
\par
In this paper we demonstrate how value of information analysis can be extended to consider
\par
the efficient choice between two methods for collecting the same data, thus providing
\par
guidance for researchers planning an economic evaluation alongside a clinical trial.
\par
The results demonstrate high expected value from eliminating all known decision
\par
uncertainty (EVPI \pounds 32.1m, Table 3), due to both high per patient decision uncertainty
\par
(coefficient of variation of 217.15/56.41 = 3.8) and the large population who could benefit
\par
from this information. There are very few other VoI studies in respiratory disease. The only
\par
other study we identified was of pharmacogenomic approaches to diagnosing non-small-cell
\par
lung cancer, which estimated an EVPI to the US economy of \$31.4m.(31)
\par
If a trial were proposed with the objective of estimating ∆B, the optimal sample size would
\par
be 2,277 per arm, costing \pounds 2.6m (plus an opportunity loss of \pounds 0.1m leading to a total cost of
\par
\pounds 2.7m), but would yield an expected net gain of sampling of \pounds 27.3m. This would be a large
\par
trial, approximately 12 times the size of the original.(28) Nevertheless it is the predicted
\par
optimal sample size taking into account the cost of acquiring the data and the expected
\par
value of the information to the population.
\par
The key analysis in this paper estimated the expected return on a study of incremental drug
\par
costs alone, comparing two alternative approaches to collecting the data. We estimate an
\par
optimal mix of 1621 observations using the bottom-up process (A) and 819 observations
\par
with the top-down (B). The cost of such a study would be \pounds 1.7m (plus \pounds 0.1m opportunity
\par
loss), yielding an ENGS of \pounds 0.855m. By using a mix of both processes, a small increase (of
\par
\pounds 35,000) in the expected return can be obtained compared with using process A alone (rows
\par
5 and 6 of Table 3).
\par
Finally, the optimal numbers of observations for each data component within one study,
\par
including the optimal mix between the two data processes for drug cost data, are 2913 on
\par
QALYs, 1064 on non-drug cost, 736 on drug cost using the process A and 901 observations
\par
using the process B (row 7, Table 3). This would yield an ENGS of \pounds 27.846m, an increase of
\par
\pounds 534,000 on a trial collecting all data on 2,277 observations. Thus selectivity in data
\par
collection in this case leads to a higher expected return on investment. It should also be
\par
noted that gathering information on all parameters simultaneously changes the optimal mix
\par
of observations on data processes A and B: where only A \& B are collected (row 6, Table 3),
\par
approximately 2/3rds of observations should be on the superior process A. When other data
\par
16are also collected, only 45\% of the observations should be on process A (736 of 1637, row 7,
\par
Table 3).
\par
4.2 Practical Implementation
\par
Our analyses provide an estimate of efficient sample size unconstrained by budget, defining
\par
the optimum at the point where the marginal gain from an observation is equal to the
\par
marginal cost (analogous to the profit maximising condition in the theory of the firm). When
\par
designing a trial, this technique can be used to provide a rational quantitative approach to
\par
determining what data to collect on which patients. The prior distributions required for
\par
each of the inputs and research cost estimates would ideally be provided by a pilot or
\par
feasibility study conducted prior to a full scale trial. Alternatively uncertainty in parameters
\par
can be captured via a formal elicitation process.(24)
\par
However, trialists are usually faced with exogenous budget or sample size constraints. To
\par
incorporate these constraints, it is simply a question of defining a feasible set of
\par
observations on each component such that the cost of sampling is less or equal to the
\par
budget, K (Equation 27). In this case, for a maximum budget of \pounds 2m the optimal
\par
combination of observations on (n{$\Delta$}E, n{$\Delta$}Cn, n{$\Delta$}CAd, n{$\Delta$}CBd) is (2379, 722, 419, 900). This trial
\par
would cost \pounds 1,999,981 and yield an ENGS of \pounds 27.754m. This solution was identified using
\par
the Nelder-Mead algorithm.
\par
Likewise, it is straightforward to choose the optimal mix from a feasible set where the
\par
sample size has already been determined (for example through a conventional power
\par
calculation based on a clinically important difference in a primary outcome).
