In this analysis, we focused on a very narrow example, driven by the data available to us.
\par
However, there is no reason that the principles cannot be extended to other related
\par
problems such as the decision to use routine or administrative data sources in place of study
\par
specific data. All that is required is a prior belief that one method is measuring the true
\par
quantity of interest, that the other is an approximation, and that there is some prior belief
\par
about the relationship between the two.
\par
17It should be noted that the class of decision problem where this analysis applies is where
\par
there is a genuine choice between two alternative methods to collect the same information,
\par
such as top-down versus bottom-up costing, medical records versus patient questionnaires
\par
or routine administrative data versus study specific collection. Decisions over collection of
\par
different components of cost, such as whether to include dispensing fees in drug cost
\par
calculations, or social services costs as well as hospital costs can be analysed using standard
\par
value of information approaches.
\par
4.3 Determinants of optimal mix of observations between two processes
\par
The optimal mix of observations on two data processes is a function of the relationship
\par
between the two (as expressed in the correlation coefficient, {$\rho$}) and the relative cost of
\par
sampling. Where the data processes are very closely related ({$\rho$} close to \textpm 1), then one would
\par
expect the top-down (process B) to be the optimal choice due to the lower cost of sampling:
\par
observations on B can be used to revise beliefs about A simply by adjusting for the prior
\par
estimate of bias. However, where the processes are less closely related there is a trade-off
\par
between the extra cost of A and the extra information it yields compared to B. Where the
\par
correlation is zero, gathering information using B provides no information on A, therefore it
\par
would never be efficient to use process B.
\par
In the dataset used in this paper, the (prior) correlation coefficient between {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$A$} and {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$B$} is
\par
0.83. Given this, and the relative cost of processes A \& B, it is efficient for 34\% (819/2440) of
\par
observations to be on process B. It is worth investigating how the optimal mix changes with
\par
different values of {$\rho$} (Figure 4). As predicted, at almost perfect positive or negative
\par
correlation, process B provides equivalent information to process A; as process B is cheaper
\par
than process A, it is always preferable to draw observations on that process. As {$\rho$} falls, for a
\par
given sample size process B provides less information on {$\DeltaCd$}
\par
{$A$}, until the value of the
\par
information falls below the marginal cost of sampling at which point it is only worth
\par
collecting data using process A.
\par
4.4 Comparison with other studies
\par
The origins of this analysis lie in statistical decision theory, developed in the 1960s at the
\par
Harvard Business School.(15) However, we are aware of only one previous application of
\par
value of information principles to help choose study designs. Shavit and colleagues(14)
\par
presented a method to compare the 'net information benefit' of an RCT with an
\par
observational study. They define this as a function of the current evidence and estimates of
\par
18the magnitude of five discrete sources of bias associated with the two designs:
\par
representation, selection, time frame, real-life reflection and accuracy of records. Measures
\par
of each bias were quantified and expressed as percentage deviation from the true mean.
\par
A key difference between their approach and ours is the question being asked: Shavit and
\par
colleagues(14) are concerned with the choice between a prospective RCT and retrospective
\par
cohort study to answer a decision question. Our analysis starts where an RCT design has
\par
already been chosen, but the approach to collecting various components of the data is
\par
undecided.
\par
4.5 Strengths and Limitations
\par
We present a quantitative method to assist the design of a clinical trial, specifically
\par
predicting the numbers of observations on different input parameters to incremental net
\par
benefit based on maximising the expected return on investment in research. To this end,
\par
there are a number of limitations and assumptions which must be considered.
\par
Firstly, we referred to costing a particular resource item as a `data process' without explicitly
\par
differentiating between measurement and valuation. However, this has no consequence for
\par
the analysis: two data collection processes may vary in how resource use data are collected
\par
(e.g. medical records vs patient self-report) or by valuation technique (costing individual
\par
items vs average unit costs to classes of items). The example presented here simulates a
\par
hybrid of the two: the simpler data process collects data at a more aggregate level and
\par
applies an average unit cost by drug class based on a representative daily dose.
\par
Secondly, we expressed the covariance between parameters as the product of the
\par
correlation coefficient and standard errors. This allows the correlation coefficient to be
\par
treated as independent from the variances, and potentially as a parameter about which
\par
information could be sought. However, for simplicity, we assumed the correlation
\par
coefficient, {$\rho$}, to be constant. This raises two issues. Firstly, this only allows a very simple
\par
linear relationship between the two parameters and secondly we ignored uncertainty in {$\rho$}.
\par
The first issue can be handled either by transforming one of the parameters (for example,
\par
the relationship may be log-linear), or formal modelling of the relationship between the
\par
parameters. The second issue raises additional complications as {$\rho$} has a non-normal
\par
distribution.
\par
Indeed a key limitation of the analysis is the assumption of Normality: costs are known to be
\par
right skewed, whilst QALYs may be left skewed (depending on the patient population). The
\par
19major advantage of this assumption is ease of computation and availability of analytic
\par
solutions, but at the risk of misleading conclusions should a Normal distribution be a poor
\par
representation of prior beliefs. Simulation approaches may overcome this, but are
\par
computationally expensive. However, techniques have been developed to `short cut' such
\par
processes.(32, 33) The Central Limit Theorem states that the sampling distribution of the
\par
mean will be approximately Normal. However this is only true in data with a small
\par
coefficient of variation and/or large sample size.(34) Therefore an obvious extension to this
\par
work is to consider alternative distributional forms for cost data such as bivariate gamma, or
\par
simulation approaches using appropriate software(35) with appropriate programming
\par
expertise.
\par
Thirdly, we assumed that the overall sample size in analysis 3.4 would be the maximum of
\par
each individual parameter, namely 2913 patients in each arm (on which QALY data would be
\par
obtained). Of those, 1064 would be chosen at random from which non-drug cost data would
\par
be obtained, then 1637 would be chosen from which drug cost data would be obtained, 736
\par
of which using process A and 901 using process B. The cost of the trial was estimated on this
\par
basis. However, where patients provide data on more than one component, the covariance
\par
and hence correlation between those components can be estimated and used to revise the
\par
prior estimates of the correlation coefficient. Our analysis currently ignores this additional
\par
information and so may be overestimating optimal trial sizes. However, incorporating this is
\par
not straightforward and is an area for further research.
\par
Other limitations are exclusion of other developments in the application of value of
\par
information analysis to the healthcare field, such as the appropriate time horizon for an
\par
analysis,(36) delays whilst research is conducted(37) and optimal allocation of projects
\par
across different jurisdictions(38) potentially affecting the ENGS of a study. We also assumed
\par
a constant marginal cost of recruitment. This is an oversimplification of the cost function as
\par
the first patients are likely to be easier to recruit than the last ones, as stocks of `willing
\par
volunteers' get exhausted, and further effort is required to identify new patients.
\par
205.\textbf{ }Conclusion
\par
In this paper we have shown how it is possible to adapt the principles of value of
\par
information analysis to estimate the optimal mix of bottom-up and top-down data collection
\par
processes for a component of resource use data in an economic evaluation alongside a
\par
clinical trial. Furthermore we have shown how this can be incorporated within a broader
\par
decision analytic approach to estimating efficient sample sizes of different data components.
\par
Selectivity in the numbers of observations for each component can help contain cost and
\par
yield a higher expected net benefit of sampling than one measuring all data on all patients.
\par
Whilst the method presented can be used to help researchers design trials, future work will
\par
address the current limitations and incorporate other recent advances in VoI methodology.
