33Appendix 1: Details of data set, beneficial population and cost of
\par
research
\par
The data used in the example are taken from the ELEVATE study, a study of leukotriene
\par
receptor antagonists compared with conventional treatment in asthma patients.(28) The
\par
study comprised two separate trials, on 'step 2' and 'step 3' patients. The data used here are
\par
those relating to the more severe 'step 3' patients,(29) reanalysed from the perspective of
\par
the UK NHS to divide costs into drug ({$Cd$}) and NHS-non drug items ({$Cn$}, comprising primary,
\par
secondary and tertiary resource use) at two years, and outcomes as QALYs ({$E$}) gained at two
\par
years (costs and QALYs incurred in year two were discounted at 3.5\%). Incremental net
\par
benefit ({$\Delta$}B) was calculated at a threshold of \pounds 5000 per QALY in order to illustrate the
\par
method demonstrated in this manuscript.
\par
Drug cost in the original trial analysis was calculated based on individual items with the unit
\par
cost per item extracted from the British National Formulary (BNF) 2005(39) using unique
\par
BNF code at the individual preparation level. There were 27,028 items of data in the raw
\par
dataset extracted from the study database, representing individual prescription items
\par
dispensed to 683 patients over two years enrolled in the two trials comprising the ELEVATE
\par
study. The cost for each datum was recalculated at the BNF chapter section level, using
\par
aggregate cost per prescription as reported in the Prescription Cost Analysis 2005.(30) For
\par
eight observations, no sub-paragraph or chapter section data were available. Four of these
\par
were costs for specific wound dressings so the original unit cost included was applied to
\par
both summary cost estimates. The other four were blank entries that were subsequently
\par
excluded from all analyses. Therefore every patient had two estimates of drug costs over
\par
the two year study period: one bottom-up, based on actual prescribed doses of drugs
\par
(process A yielding {$Cd$}
\par
{$A$}) and the other top-down, aggregated at the BNF section level
\par
(process B yielding {$Cd$}
\par
{$B$}). Complete drug data were available on all patients.
\par
As stated, the other data items were NHS non-drug cost ({$Cn$} ) and QALYs gained ({$E$}) at two
\par
years. 47 (6.9\%) and 283 (41.4\%) of 683 observations on NHS cost and QALYs were missing.
\par
Multiple imputation was performed on the missing data including step, group, sex, age,
\par
education and employment status as coefficients. Five iterations were calculated and the
\par
results combined using Rubin's rules.(40) Data on the step 2 patients was discarded.
\par
34Population able to benefit from the information
\par
In 2004 there were an estimated 5.2m people with asthma in the UK.(41) Approximately 7\%
\par
of adolescents with asthma are at 'step 3' (requiring add-on therapy, usually of a long acting
\par
beta agonist, LABA).(42) In the absence of general population data, we assume the same
\par
proportion of adults is at step 3. During the period 1990 - 1998, GPRD data suggests the
\par
prevalence of asthma in the UK general population rose from approximately 3\% to 5\%.(43)
\par
This equates to an increase of approximately 0.025\% per annum. Assuming a linear
\par
increase, and based on a UK population in 2004 of 59,834,300,(44) the estimated prevalence
\par
of step 3 patients in 2011 is approximately 437,297, with an incidence of 10,471 each year.
\par
Over a ten year period therefore, the potential population who could benefit from the
\par
information yielded by the studies proposed in this paper is 542,007 or 524,380 (discounted
\par
at 3.5\%; Table ‎ A-1).
\par
