@article{xuMatrixBasedProactiveData2016,
  title = {A Matrix-Based Proactive Data Relay Algorithm for Large Distributed Sensor Networks},
  author = {Xu, Yang and Hu, Xuemei and Hu, Haixiao and Liu, Ming},
  year = 2016,
  journal = {Sensors},
  volume = {16},
  number = {8},
  pages = {1300},
  publisher = {Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute},
  doi = {10.3390/s16081300},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Publisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute
\par
\textbf{\emph{sensors}}
\par
Article
\par
A Matrix-Based Proactive Data Relay Algorithm for
\par
Large Distributed Sensor Networks
\par
Yang Xu *, Xuemei Hu, Haixiao Hu and Ming Liu
\par
School of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China,
\par
Chengdu 611731, China; huxuemei1990@126.com (X.H.); 201511060118@std.uestc.edu.cn (H.H.);
\par
mingliu.uestc@gmail.com (M.L.)
\par
* Correspondence: xuyang@uestc.edu.cn; Tel.: +86-189-8094-7876
\par
Academic Editor: Xue-Bo Jin
\par
Received: 21 June 2016; Accepted: 2 August 2016; Published: 16 August 2016
\par
Abstract: In large-scale distributed sensor networks, sensed data is required to be relayed around
\par
the network so that one or few sensors can gather adequate relative data to produce high quality
\par
information for decision-making. In regards to very high energy-constraint sensor nodes, data
\par
transmission should be extremely economical. However, traditional data delivery protocols are
\par
potentially inefficient relaying unpredictable sensor readings for data fusion in large distributed
\par
networks for either overwhelming query transmissions or unnecessary data coverage. By building
\par
sensors' local model from their previously transmitted data in three matrixes, we have developed
\par
a novel energy-saving data relay algorithm, which allows sensors to proactively make broadcast
\par
decisions by using a neat matrix computation to provide balance between transmission and
\par
energy-saving. In addition, we designed a heuristic maintenance algorithm to efficiently update
\par
these three matrices. This can easily be deployed to large-scale mobile networks in which decisions
\par
of sensors are based on their local matrix models no matter how large the network is, and the local
\par
models of these sensors are updated constantly. Compared with some traditional approaches based
\par
on our simulations, the efficiency of this approach is manifested in uncertain environment. The results
\par
show that our approach is scalable and can effectively balance aggregating data with minimizing
\par
energy consumption.
\par
Keywords: proactive data relay; information fusion; large distributed sensor networks;
\par
matrix-based computing
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Large distributed sensor networks have been widely used in both military and civilian
\par
applications [1], such as target tracking [2], disaster response [3] and field surveillance [4]. In these
\par
applications, although each sensor is able to get some crude information, the data is usually imprecise
\par
or noisy with very low fidelity. As a consequence, the data in this form cannot be directly used for
\par
automatic planning or supporting human decisions, and has to be fused with other relevant data [5].
\par
To fuse distributed data in a network, the key is to relay multiple-source data and aggregate
\par
enough amount of data to a single node in order to achieve high confidence information fusion [6].
\par
However, with the progress of the development of state-of-the-art mobile sensor applications, networks
\par
emerge with new characteristics that pose challenges to existing information fusion approaches. The
\par
astronomical growth of networks involving thousands of sensors is a typical challenge in this respect.
\par
In these large networks, no single sensor can respond as the center and gain complete statistic of
\par
states of the entire network. In addition, because these networks are dynamically changing due to
\par
the mobility of sensors, or movements as a result of the surrounding air or ocean currents [7], node
\par
Sensors 2016, 16, 1300; doi:10.3390/s16081300 www.mdpi.com/journal/sensorsSensors 2016, 16, 1300 2 of 19
\par
failures are not rare. Additionally, most sensors in these large networks have lightweight processing
\par
units, which make high constraint of energy consumption ideal for their operations.
\par
Following the importance of information fusion, many data relay protocols have been developed
\par
to diffuse data in sensor networks. Flooding [8] adopts a straightforward protocol for sensors to
\par
rebroadcast any new data received, but suffers from the intrinsic excessive energy consumption and
\par
network congestion due to the large number of duplicated messages. Traditional hierarchical [8] or
\par
centralized routing [9--12] approaches, which are designed for fixed structure or where only sink
\par
nodes respond to information fusion are not also ideal for data fusion in mobile sensor networks.
\par
The predefined backbone nodes in these approaches will exhaust their power very quickly because
\par
of the heavy transmission burden and enormous cost on routing maintenance. To avoid these
\par
problems, many other distributed approaches have been developed for cases whereby all nodes
\par
can be treated as sink nodes. Those approaches include Information via Negotiation(SPIN) [13],
\par
Scalable Broadcast Algorithm (SBA) [14], The Lightweight and Efficient Network Wide Broadcast
\par
(LENWB) [15], Dominant Pruning [16] and Dynamic Probabilistic Flooding Algorithm [17]. Even
\par
though these approaches can guarantee a high quality information fusion, not all sensors would
\par
need all the data because any piece of information can only be fused by the sensor that aggregates
\par
enough relative data first. Therefore, the unnecessary data coverage may end with huge amount of
\par
energy consumption and redundant communication cost. SPIN can address this limitation to some
\par
extent by negotiating before broadcast, and allows sensors to transmit only data that others would
\par
need. Unfortunately, it is a reactive protocol, and a large number of negotiated messages makes it
\par
time-consuming and cost-ineffective for distributed data fusion. Some algorithms [18] can proactively
\par
relay data by forecasting the needs before a given set of data is broadcast. For example, a sensor would
\par
rather like to rebroadcast the data that is relative to the data of its neighbors only. However, when
\par
sensors cannot gain a complete view over an entire network, an intelligent proactive algorithm could
\par
be very difficult to implement [19].
\par
In this paper, we present a novel matrix-based energy-saving and proactive algorithm to relay data
\par
in large distributed sensor networks. To deliver the core competence of this approach, we employ no
\par
querying process, and each node proactively forwards data to its neighbors by estimating the needs of
\par
neighbors based on its local knowledge about itself and the neighbors. From this viewpoint, we encode
\par
local knowledge of sensors into three neat matrices: local connection matrix (C); local data distribution
\par
matrix (D); and utility matrix (U) to encode utility of adding data into a data set. With these matrices, a
\par
compact and light-weight algorithm is proposed, where each node makes a series of simple all-in-one
\par
matrix computation to evaluate the benefits and cost of actions. Comparing the benefits against cost
\par
allows serious decisions to be made whether a piece of sensor data should be broadcast to make a
\par
good tradeoff between data relay to get high quality information and prolonging the lifetime of the
\par
network. In addition, energy consumption in data relay is taken into consideration towards cautious
\par
decision-making processes. As sensors do not have global knowledge of the network, a heuristic
\par
algorithm is proposed to maintain the size and value of these matrices from incoming messages to
\par
adapt to sensor mobility. By introducing matrix computations, and as our matrices only store local
\par
knowledge and updated in time, our approach is lightweight and can easily be deployed in large
\par
mobile sensor networks. Further, to manifest feasibility of this approach, we discuss the simulations
\par
carried out, and the results show that our approach can perform well in dynamic environments to
\par
effectively balance aggregating data with minimizing energy consumption.
\par
2. Related Work
\par
Many data relay protocols have been designed for distribute data fusion in wireless sensor
\par
network [20--22]. They can be categorized as two groups according to where the fusion occurs.
\par
One strand of protocols are based on fix or predefined sink nodes. They are designed for multiple
\par
sensors to forward data to specific sink nodes for data fusion, such as Directed Diffusion [9], in
\par
which a sink floods its interests to build reverse paths from all potential sources to the sink. RumorSensors 2016, 16, 1300 3 of 19
\par
Routing [10], Constrained anisotropic diffusion routing (CADR) [11] and GRAdient Broadcast [12]
\par
are some variations of Directed Diffusion. In Rumor routing, sink floods queries while sensors flood
\par
events which make Rumor routing performs better than Directed Diffusion when number of events is
\par
small. CADR introduces an information utility measure to select which sensors query and dynamically
\par
guide data routing. GRAB builds and maintains a cost field to provide sensors the direction to forward
\par
sensing data. It is a robust data delivery algorithm addressed nodes failures and link failures. However,
\par
since these protocols are based on single-gateway architecture that makes them not fit for large scale
\par
mobile sensor network [21]. First of all, sensors near sink nodes have heavy burden to relay data and
\par
their energy will be exhausted in short time. Second, sensors are typically not capable of long-haul
\par
communication and the latency in communication can not be ignored. Third, energy on finding ways
\par
to sink nodes is huge which makes them not suitable for mobile networks where network topology
\par
dynamically changes.
\par
To allow the system to be able to cover a large area of interest without degrading the service,
\par
networking clustering has been pursued in some routing approaches. LEACH [21] and LEACH
\par
series [23] are hierarchical routing algorithms for sensor networks. Cluster heads change randomly
\par
over time in order to balance the energy dissipation of nodes. Each node transmits directly to the
\par
cluster-head. They are completely distributed and requires no global knowledge of network. However,
\par
they use single-hop routing where each node transmits directly to the cluster-head and the sink.
\par
Therefore, it is not applicable to networks deployed in large regions . Further more, the idea of
\par
dynamic clustering brings extra overhead, e.g. head changes, advertisements etc., which may diminish
\par
the gain in energy consumption.
\par
For the second strand of protocols, there is no predefined sink nodes, sensors are teated equally as
\par
probable sink nodes, and data fusion can be done by any sensor unless it aggregates enough relative
\par
data. The most straight forward protocol is flooding [8], in which sensors rebroadcast any new data it
\par
receives. Apparently, FLOODING consumes too much energy on the transmission of redundant data.
\par
To reduce the redundant data of flooding, some reactive and proactive protocols are proposed. Sensor
\par
Protocols for Information via Negotiation(SPIN) [13] is a reactive protocol which avoids redundant
\par
data transmission by meta-data negotiation with neighbors. sensors only forward data to the neighbors
\par
that need it. However, it is infeasible for distributed fusion where the size of meta-data is close to useful
\par
data because the frequent query data transmission is not cost-effective and introduces non-negligible
\par
time delay.
\par
Proactive protocols do not need queries to avoid redundant data transmission but proactively
\par
decide if rebroadcasting data received based on the local topology and static information of redundant
\par
data, such as Scalable Broadcast Algorithm (SBA) [14], The Lightweight and Efficient Network Wide
\par
Broadcast (LENWB) [15], Dominant Pruning [16] and Dynamic Probabilistic Flooding Algorithm [17].
\par
SBA and Dominant Pruning maintain the local network topology by hello messages. In SBA, sensors
\par
only rebroadcast the data that at least one of its neighbors do not know. In Dominant Pruning,
\par
rebroadcasting nodes proactively choose one or more of its neighbors as rebroadcasting nodes by
\par
Greedy Set Cover algorithm. In Dynamic Probabilistic Flooding Algorithm, sensors obtain neighbor
\par
information by basic flooding and divide neighbors into three types: parent (upper level), sibling
\par
(same level), and child (lower level) nodes. A node with the more children nodes and less siblings
\par
needs the lower retransmission probability. These three protocols try to cover all the nodes with
\par
all the data to guarantee at least one node aggregates enough relative data to fuse into information.
\par
However, in large sensor network, too much energy is consumed on unnecessary coverage of such
\par
a huge number of sensor nodes, for a piece of information is only needed to be fused by one sensor.
\par
In addition, in mobile sensor network, too much energy is consumed on the transmission of hello
\par
message to maintain local topology.Sensors 2016, 16, 1300 4 of 19
