4. Matrix-Based Data Relay Algorithm
\par
In large-scale distributed sensor networks, because of the huge system size and energy constraints
\par
of sensors, sensors are unlikely to have a global observation to support optimal communication
\par
decisions. Sensors only make rational communication decisions soley based on their local dataset
\par
from their previously transmitted messages around the network. To make rational communication
\par
decisions, sensors need local topology of the network to indicate who are potential receivers, the local
\par
data distribution to indicate what data neighbors have, and the utility between data pieces to figure
\par
out the benefit of broadcasting one piece of data.
\par
In this section, a Proactive Energy-Saving Data Relay algorithm CDU will be proposed to help
\par
sensors compute the benefit and cost for all the data in pendingQue by neat matrix computations. The
\par
framework is shown in Figure 2. First, sensors need local states to support their decisions. In our
\par
model, three parts are necessary to a sensor: connection matrix C denotes its local network topology,
\par
data distribution matrix D about the local data distribution of the sensor and its neighbors and the
\par
data Utility matrix U. For each time step t, each sensor i is required to update CDU matrixes by the
\par
model maintenance function introduced in the next section. Next, a neat computation with the CDU
\par
matrixes can produce the expected benefit of transmitting pending data Bt
\par
i . In addition, connection
\par
matrix Ct
\par
i also be used to predict the cost of transmitting data Et
\par
i . By balancing between Bt
\par
i and Et
\par
i , if
\par
the sensor find that the expected utility Ft
\par
i is positive, it will broadcast the data as the network will be
\par
benefit of more likely fusing valuable information.
