5. Model Maintenance Algorithm
\par
Sensors can make rational decisions by comparing the benefits and energy consumption of
\par
broadcast through matrix computation introduced in the previous section. Because of the mobility of
\par
sensors, and the dynamic changing nature of data distribution, the three matrices C,D and U need to
\par
be updated in time. The more precise the model is, the better the decisions. However, in large sensor
\par
networks, considering the massive energy cost on communication, no single sensor can get the precise
\par
and global connection and data distribution model but only partial observations.
\par
In this section, considering the intrinsic energy cost in the operations of these networks, we
\par
propose heuristic updating approaches to maintain a local model for each sensor from its incoming
\par
messages. With these updating approaches, an integrated decision algorithm is considered to help
\par
each sensor make rational decisions with partial observations.
\par
5.1. Initialization
\par
Before deployment in large scale sensor networks, locations of sensors are not pre-determined,
\par
and the network topology is unknown to any sensor. Sat this stage, the three matrices of each sensor are
\par
initialized to empty matrices. After deployment, sensors start the introduction phase by broadcasting
\par
hello messages for once to initialize their connection matrix C.
\par
5.2. The Rules to Maintain The Dimensions
\par
As we described in Section 3, the size of these three matrices is related to Ni and Li. The column
\par
number of C and row number of D correspond to the size of Ni. The column number of D and the
\par
row and column number of U also correspond to the size of Li. When an element is added into any of
\par
these two sets, one column or row of 0 will be added to the corresponding matrices. If one element is
\par
moved out of these sets, the corresponding column or row of matrices will be deleted. Therefore, the
\par
principles to maintain these two sets are paramount, and given below:
\par
{$\bullet$} vi will add an element vj in set Ni: when receiving a data dk and vj {$\in$}dk.path is not in Ni.
\par
{$\bullet$} vj will be removed from Ni: when vi neither has positive connection probability with it nor has
\par
any knowledge of it, Cvi,vj =0 and {$\sum$}dh{$\in$}Li Svj,dh =0.
\par
{$\bullet$} vi will add an element dk into Li:Sensors 2016, 16, 1300 10 of 19
\par
-- when receiving data dk that is not in Li.
\par
-- when generating data dk based on its detection.
\par
{$\bullet$} vi will delete the element dk from Li: when dk {$\in$}dataO, where dataO stores data that has been
\par
fused or outdated.
\par
