5.6. Integrated Algorithm
\par
With these updating algorithms, network connection, data distribution and utility matrix can be
\par
updated to help sensors get local observations to support rational communication decisions even in
\par
dynamically changing networks. The whole data relay process with local knowledge can be seen in
\par
Algorithm 4.
\par
This algorithm consists of two parts. In the first part, sensors update these three matrices based
\par
on the approaches mentioned in last three subsections (line 1--7). In the second part, vi will try to fuse
\par
with these new data and make communication decisions for those that have not been fused (line 8--21).
\par
Principally, vi checks them one by one to see if any new information can be fused (line 8--11). If the
\par
quality of the new information exceeds the predefined threshold, data related to this information willSensors 2016, 16, 1300 12 of 19
\par
be fused and added into the outdated data set. vi will then inform other sensors to stop propagation of
\par
these outdated data pieces (line 11--13). If no information can be fused, this data will be added into the
\par
pending queue(line 15). After the information fusion process completes, vi makes decisions for data
\par
in pending queue based on our lightweight matrix computation (line 16) by balancing between the
\par
benefits and energy cost of broadcasting. If the benefit is higher than the energy cost (line 18), vi will
\par
add itself onto the path of this data and broadcast it (line 19--20), and update the data set dataB with
\par
this data. Otherwise, this data will be ignored (line 21).
