6. Experimental Section
\par
In this section, we evaluate the performance of our proactive data relay algorithm CDU through
\par
simulations. In most scenarios, we used a field size of 600 \texttimes 600 m2 where 500 mobile nodes were
\par
randomly scattered for target detection, and fused data detected into information. For each time step,
\par
1\% of sensors were made to move. Sensors communicated with each other in a broadcast medium, and
\par
the power of sensor radio transmitter was fixed so that any node within a 25 meter radius was within
\par
communication range. Sensor nodes within a communication range of another sensor are described
\par
as the neighbors. The power consumption (0.66 W in transmit mode, 0.395 W in receive mode) were
\par
chosen based on data from currently available radios [12]. The transmission time for a packet was
\par
fixed at 10ms. In each run, 100 events about targets randomly occurred. Each target could be detected
\par
by 9 sensors, and each detection generated a piece of data with a confidence c, c {$\in$} [0, 1] is related to
\par
the distance between detected sensor and target [25]. For each event, one piece of information can be
\par
fused only when more than 6 data related to this target is aggregated by one sensor and the combined
\par
confidence is higher than 0.75.
\par
We mainly compare the performance of our algorithm CDU with the Flooding [8], the Scalable
\par
Broadcast Algorithm (SBA) [14], and GRAdient Broadcast(GRAB) [12] algorithms. GRAB is on
\par
behalf of routing algorithms where only predefined sink nodes are able to fuse while the other three
\par
treat all sensors as potential sink nodes. Flooding is the most straightforward data relay algorithm,
\par
where each sensor broadcasts whatever new data it receives immediately without any reasoning.
\par
For SBA, only data that can reach new neighbors is broadcast. In SBA, neighbor knowledge in two
\par
hops is maintained by periodic "hello" packages. Also, in GRAB, each sensor maintains a cost field,
\par
and records the cost to the sink node in proportion to the distance to the sink node. Sensors onlySensors 2016, 16, 1300 13 of 19
\par
broadcast data received from the sensor where cost is higher and relay data to sink nodes to fuse.
\par
Typical of most simulations, the network has one random sink node. When the number of nodes is
\par
1000, 2 sink nodes are randomly chosen. In CDU, Flooding, and SBA, any sensor can fuse data into
\par
information if enough data is aggregated, while in GRAB only sink node can fuse. The value of U
\par
matrix in CDU is generated based on the D-S fusion rule [30]. Other relevant parameters defined are
\par
{$\sigma$} = 0.97, {$\beta$}= 2.0, lengthO f Path= 2.
\par
To test if CDU works well, we measured the Number of fused information by all the sensors,
\par
the Total energy cost on communication of all sensors, and the efficiency = Number of fused
\par
information/Total energy cost, calculated as the energy consumed on communicating to fuse each
\par
piece of information. The efficiency indicates how the algorithms balance broadcasting to fusing more
\par
information and minimizing energy consumption. Results for each experiment are based on one
\par
hundred trials.
\par
In the simulations, we first evaluate the impact of control parameters of CDU, including {$\beta$} and
\par
the length of path. Then we compare the performance of CDU with the other three related algorithms
\par
mentioned above in default settings with the progress of simulations. After that we compare these
\par
algorithms while independently changing the following environmental factors: the size of the field to
\par
test if CDU is scalable; the ratio of moved sensors to test if CDU can adapt to dynamic networks; and
\par
the threshold of fused targets. Finally, since GRAB is one of the routing protocols, we compare the
\par
final energy distribution between CDU and that of GRAB.
\par
6.1. Different {$\beta$} and Different Length of Path
\par
The value of {$\beta$} is a parameter used by CDU to balance forwarding data and minimizing energy
\par
consumption, which is defined in Equation (1). The path is a data structure used in CDU to store the
\par
sensor visited in each data. The length of path affects rich degree of the local matrix model.
\par
To understand how {$\beta$} and the length of path affect efficient data relay, we varied {$\beta$} from 0 to 4,
\par
and varied the length also from 1 to 4. Experimental results of a number of fused information, energy
\par
cost on communication, and efficiency are illustrated in Figure 3. As we can see, the higher the
\par
value of {$\beta$}, the definition of useless data becomes more strict, and more data will be stopped from
\par
broadcasting especially those data with lower confidence or well known by neighbors. To some degree,
\par
it affects the number of fused information. As shown in Figure 3a,b, the number of fused information
\par
is proportional to {$\beta$} and the energy cost decreases as {$\beta$} increases. When {$\beta$}= 0, any data received will
\par
be broadcast and all the information can be fused. Figure 3c shows the efficiency for different {$\beta$}. When
\par
{$\beta$}= 2.0, the efficiency reaches a high position. The balance of data relay and saving energy is good.
\par
When {$\beta$} {$>$} 2.0, the efficiency still keeps a high value. However, the information fused is much less.
