\par
Abstract
\par
As the structure of complex electronic systems is large, there is inherent uncertainty, so it is often inefficient or infeasi-
\par
ble to perform detailed fault diagnostics directly. Fault diagnostics for complex electronic systems need to be performed
\par
based on the sensor monitoring data, so in this article, sensory information fusion is introduced to minimize diagnostic
\par
uncertainty and computational complexity. Sensory information fusion combines the sensor data by measuring the
\par
mutuality of the information and dynamically selecting the most diagnostic decision-relevant sensor subset. This article
\par
proposes a sensory information fusion--based diagnostic methodology and framework, which is made up of a dynamical
\par
sensor selection that combines sensory information and detailed fault diagnostics based on the sensory information
\par
fusion results. A numerical example is conducted to illustrate the proposed sensory information fusion--based diagnos-
\par
tics. The diagnostic results as well as comparisons with different training data sizes confirm that the proposed sensory
\par
information fusion--based diagnostics is superior when dealing with uncertain and incomplete information. Furthermore,
\par
the sensory information fusion--based diagnostics is shown to simplify the diagnostic complexity and accurately and effi-
\par
ciently solve the diagnostic problems for complex electronic systems. The proposed methodology is also flexible enough
\par
to accommodate other fault diagnostic methods so as to perform applicable diagnostics for other complex electronic
\par
systems of varying sizes.
\par
Keywords
\par
Sensory information fusion, fault diagnostics, sensor selection, diagnostic Bayesian networks, complex electronic system
\par
Date received: 3 October 2014; accepted: 22 June 2015
\par
Introduction
\par
With the fast industrial development and extraordina-
\par
rily technical competition, most current systems and
\par
equipments contain embedded electronics, especially
\par
for the complex electronic systems. For example, in
\par
modern weaponry systems, electronic systems continue
\par
to penetrate into the flight control, guidance, power,
\par
environmental control, life support, instrument dis-
\par
plays, and other traditional non-electronic parts; it
\par
increases the scale of complex electronic systems as well
\par
as the investments. For instance, avionics systems have
\par
accounted for nearly 50\% ratio in the overall cost of a
\par
fighter. Avionics systems and radar, navigation, com-
\par
munication, observe and control, large-scale integrated
\par
circuit, and other complex electronic systems have
\par
improved the efficacy of the system platforms. At the
\par
same time, their faults proportion also becomes increas-
\par
ingly high in the total fault platforms. As a result, fault
\par
diagnostics has become increasingly important to
\par
enhance system availability and reduce costs when seek-
\par
ing to ensure system safety, life-cycle cost control, and
\par
operational efficiency. Therefore, fault diagnostics has
\par
become a very important research field for dealing with
\par
complex electronic systems. Considered a system engi-
\par
neering discipline, it includes the processes and technol-
\par
ogies used to design, analyze, build, verify, and operate
\par
a system to prevent faults, minimize their effects, reduce
\par
the life-cycle risk,1 and avoid unexpected incidents, per-
\par
formance degradation, or major damage to a system.2
\par
1Department of Electronics \& Management, Xihua University, Chengdu,
\par
China
\par
2Uncertainty Decision-Making Laboratory, Sichuan University, Chengdu,
\par
China
\par
Corresponding author:
\par
Jiuping Xu, Uncertainty Decision-Making Laboratory, Sichuan University,
\par
No. 24 South Section 1, Yihuan Road, Chengdu 610064, China.
\par
Email: xujiuping@scu.edu.cn110 Proc IMechE Part O: J Risk and Reliability 230(1)
\par
Since the faults may represent either single, multiple,
\par
or relevant failure modes in a complex electronic
\par
system, uncertainty become the major problem for the
\par
fault diagnostics. As electronic systems are large and
\par
complex, anomalies and faults are governed by differ-
\par
ent failure mechanisms.3 Because of the dynamic beha-
\par
vior, the multiple functions, the complex structures,
\par
and the numerous non-line and uncertain parameters,4
\par
an entire fault diagnostic analysis with direct inference
\par
can lead to a large inefficient knowledge base and poor
\par
diagnostic quality.5 Therefore, it is often difficult and
\par
inefficient to perform detailed fault diagnostics directly
\par
on complete complex electronic systems. Such prob-
\par
lems have led to a reconsideration of possible diagnos-
\par
tics approaches.6
\par
Generally, fault diagnostics for a complex electronic
\par
system is performed based on the sensor monitoring
\par
data. However, traditional fault diagnostics methods
\par
are commonly incapable of taking full advantage of the
\par
obtained system sensory information to achieve diag-
\par
nostics, which integrates and fuses the information.7 To
\par
address the fault diagnostics computational difficulty
\par
which is constrained by limited time and resources, a
\par
myopic approach with only one observation8,9 is com-
\par
monly used. However, this myopic analysis is unable to
\par
effectively reduce uncertainty and computational cost.10
\par
Therefore, a non-myopic selection needs be conducted
\par
to simultaneously consider several observations before
\par
a diagnostics decision is made. It is critically important
\par
to perform selective fusion in a more accurate and effi-
\par
cient manner by selecting complementary sensors and
\par
reducing sensor redundancy11 to avoid unnecessary or
\par
unproductive sensor actions and computations. Sensory
\par
information fusion (SIF) can serve these purposes well.
\par
SIF for the diagnostics of a complex electronic system
\par
can be defined as the process of combining sensor data
\par
that dynamically select a sensor subset to minimize
\par
uncertainty and computational complexity in fault
\par
diagnostics and maximize the overall expected utility in
\par
diagnostics decision making.12 Traditional feature selec-
\par
tion approaches could be classified as two categories,
\par
one is the filter approach and another is the supervised
\par
learning wrapper approach,13 which aims at looking for
\par
characteristic variables to achieve high classification
\par
accuracy. But it depends on the classifier to find charac-
\par
teristic variables, and both of the two category
\par
approaches cannot describe the mutuality between the
\par
sensory information and the fault diagnostics well.
\par
Especially, when data reflect strong noise and multi-
\par
dimensional features, it can easily lead to supervised
\par
learning model over-fitting.
\par
This article proposes a SIF-based diagnostics metho-
\par
dology to perform applicable and efficient fault diag-
\par
nostics for complex electronic systems. The remainder
\par
of this article is organized as follows. In section ``SIF-
\par
based diagnostic methodology,'' the proposed SIF-
\par
based fault diagnostic methodology and its conceptual
\par
architecture. In section ``Proposed diagnostics model,''
\par
the proposed diagnostics modeling framework is for-
\par
malized and the modeling procedure is presented in
\par
detail. A numerical example is conducted to apply the
\par
proposed SIF-based diagnostics in section ``A numeri-
\par
cal example.'' Conclusions are given in section
\par
``Conclusion.''
\par
SIF-based diagnostic methodology
\par
Diagnostics is the process of identifying the cause of a
\par
failure within the sensory information,14 but as previ-
\par
ously mentioned, direct complete detailed fault diagnos-
\par
tics of a complex electronic system is inefficient and
\par
difficult to perform. The diagnostics methodology pro-
\par
posed here aims to perform SIF-based diagnostics on a
\par
complex electronic system. Before the detailed fault
\par
diagnostics, a SIF-based diagnostics sensor selection is
\par
conducted. The subsequent detailed fault diagnostics is
\par
then performed according to the SIF results. In this
\par
way, the fault diagnostics is conceptually refined as a
\par
SIF-based diagnostics process. The proposed methodol-
\par
ogy is flexible enough to accommodate various applica-
\par
ble SIF and diagnostics approaches. Figure 1 shows the
