\par
are all continuous. To meet the DBN demand for pro-
\par
cessing the discrete variables, the sensor data from each
\par
fault symptom node are discretized and the fault symp-
\par
toms sample set is constituted.
\par
Step 4: identification of the diagnostic structure. The diag-
\par
nostic structure is a directed acyclic graph which shows
\par
the cause-result relationships between the fault types
\par
and fault symptoms. The DBN topology composed of
\par
the fault symptom nodes from the selected sensor subset
\par
is built using an effective structure learning algorithm.
\par
The fundamental diagnostic structure is established
\par
using the experience-based fault tree, which represents
\par
the basic relationships between the fault symptoms and
\par
fault types. A data-mining approach with mining asso-
\par
ciation rules is then used to determine the more precise
\par
statistical relationships between the selected symptom
\par
nodes and fault nodes, and the corresponding diagnos-
\par
tic structure is then represented by the DBN. The nodes
\par
in the diagnostics structure represent the system's
\par
events or variables. The directed arcs between the vari-
\par
ables symbolize the direct causalities or dependencies.
\par
Each node has a probability and the priori probabilities
\par
of the root nodes are learned and obtained.
\par
Step 5: constitution of the learning fault sample set. It consti-
\par
tute the fault sample set according to the fault types
\par
and symptoms sample set. Original data from a diag-
\par
nostic radar transmitter module were recorded under a
\par
normal state. As some faults cannot be set, the fault
\par
symptoms data were collected according to set up
\par
motor controller hard crash by changing the energiza-
\par
tion current. Then the training samples were obtained
\par
based on the original data according to the identified
\par
DBN topology through probability sampling. The
\par
diagnostic samples were obtained from the 1024 fault
\par
symptoms data and then discretized as fault symptoms
\par
for the numerical example. Beside, as some monitoring
\par
data can be intangible or missing in a complex elec-
\par
tronic system, incomplete data conditions are consid-
\par
ered. An expectation maximization (EM) algorithm21 is
\par
utilized to obtain priori probabilities for each fault
\par
symptom.
\par
The learning is aimed at making the DBN express
\par
the knowledge contained in the monitoring data. Then,
\par
the priori conditional probabilities P(SjjFi) (1 4 i 4 m;
\par
1 4 j 4 n) of every fault symptom nodes in the DBN
\par
are learned and obtained. The more of learning sam-
\par
ples, the more rational the obtained conditional prob-
\par
abilities will be.28
\par
Step 6: reasoning and fault diagnostics. Acquire the obser-
\par
vation symptoms from the sensor monitoring data at
\par
the diagnostic point and discretize the monitoring data
\par
to obtain a diagnostic sample. Diagnostic reasoning
\par
based on the diagnostic sample is conducted to obtain
\par
the probabilities P(FijSj) (1 4 i 4 m; 1 4 j 4 n) through
\par
the DBN. The fault types corresponding to the maxi-
\par
mum probability values are the diagnostic results.
