\par
Table 5 shows the diagnostic comparisons, which shows
\par
that the DA using the SIF-based approach was increas-
\par
ingly higher with the incremental training data size, and
\par
was better than the DA of both the ANN and SVM
\par
diagnostics methods in almost every data size situation.
\par
The TCs for all the approaches increase as the data size
\par
increase, while the TCs for the SIF-based approach in
\par
each data size situation were much shorter than the oth-
\par
ers. This demonstrates that the proposed SIF-based
\par
approach was superior for all data size situations from
\par
1000 to 10,000 and also validates that the proposed
\par
SIF-based diagnostics has superiority when dealing
\par
with uncertainty and incomplete information, as it
\par
accurately and efficiently simplifies the diagnostic com-
\par
plexity for complex electronic systems.
\par
Inadequate information or inappropriate diagnostics
\par
methods might be the main reason for incorrect diag-
\par
nostics results. Inadequate information indicates a lack
\par
of monitoring data and historical records from ade-
\par
quate monitoring sensors. The lack of knowledge of
\par
the system modules or too many uncertain fault symp-
\par
toms also might result in incorrectness. Inappropriate
\par
diagnostics methods are neither accurate nor applica-
\par
ble, such as an inconsistent diagnostics that do not fit
\par
the modeling procedure to allow for coordination with
\par
the SIF-based process.
\par
Conclusion
\par
In this article, a SIF-based diagnostics methodology
\par
and framework was proposed to achieve applicable and
\par
efficient fault diagnostics for complex electronic sys-
\par
tems. To avoid unnecessary or unproductive sensor
\par
actions and computations in complex electronic system
\par
fault diagnostics, the SIF-based approach was con-
\par
ducted to perform selective fusion by selecting comple-
\par
mentary sensors to minimize uncertainty and
\par
computational complexity. The SIF-based diagnostics
\par
was conceptually refined as a two-stage diagnostics
\par
process: a dynamical sensor selection which combines
\par
sensory information and a detailed DBN fault diagnos-
\par
tics based on the SIF results. The modeling process
\par
together with SIF and DBN was presented. For illus-
\par
trative purposes, a numerical example was conducted
\par
to apply the SIF-based diagnostics to an anomaly radar
\par
transmitter module on a test avionics as a representa-
\par
tive complex electronic system. A quantitative measure
\par
for sensor synergy based on mutual information was
\par
introduced to prune the sensor space and screen out
\par
the most informative and cost-effective sensor subset118 before proceeding with the detailed DBN fault diagnos-
\par
tics. Corresponding probabilities for the event nodes
\par
were obtained through parameter learning, and associ-
\par
ated diagnostic reasoning and current fault symptom
\par
samples were used to perform the fault diagnostics.
\par
The diagnostics results and comparisons with different
\par
training data sizes validated that the proposed SIF-
\par
based diagnostics has superiority when dealing with
\par
uncertainty and incomplete information even with
\par
uncertain fault symptoms. SIF-based diagnostics sim-
\par
plifies the diagnostics complexity and accurately and
\par
efficiently solves the diagnostics problem for complex
\par
electronic systems. Furthermore, the proposed metho-
\par
dology is flexible enough to accommodate various
\par
applicable fault diagnostics methods and can hierarchi-
\par
cally perform the diagnostics on varying electronic sys-
\par
tems from the system-level, and module-level to the
\par
component-level. In future work, more attention is to
\par
be paid to researching unknown systematic structure
\par
circumstances and examining SIF-based diagnostics
\par
structure learning and accuracy improvement.
