\par
Korea
\par
e-mail: yjunggi@gmail.com
\par
U. Kang Y. Lee (\&)
\par
Department of Computer Science, Gachon University, 1342
\par
Seongnamdaero, Sujeong-gu, Seongnam-Si, Gyeonggi-do,
\par
Korea
\par
e-mail: lyh@gachon.ac.kr
\par
U. Kang
\par
e-mail: ugkang@gachon.ac.kr
\par
1 Introduction
\par
It is common that patients visit hospitals to attain a wide
\par
range of general medical services. In recent years, medical
\par
services have become more diversified, yet, specialized and
\par
tailored for effective disease prevention and post-treatment
\par
management. Consequently, the quality of life has become
\par
enriched with prolonged life expectancy [1]. Advances in
\par
hospital information system have enabled medical practi-
\par
tioners and hospital staffs to manage patients more effi-
\par
ciently with economic benefits and time-saving processes
\par
[2]. Moreover, development of information and commu-
\par
nication technology (ICT), in particular, is serving an in-
\par
creasingly important role by promoting convenience and
\par
rapidly expanding the scope of communication [3--5].
\par
Specialized medical services in conjunction with ICT have
\par
been able to meet the demands of disease prevention, post-
\par
management and patient-tailored services in modern
\par
1236 Inf Technol Manag (2016) 17:5--14
\par
society. Furthermore, clinical decision support system
\par
(CDSS) coupled with u-Healthcare service have drawn
\par
attention in order to sustain life-time health management
\par
[6, 7]. In this context, it can be mentioned that, 25 years
\par
ago CDSS was developed from decision support system
\par
(DSS) for efficient business management. The main func-
\par
tion of DSS is knowledge-based decision support for hu-
\par
man resource management [8]. Due to its practical
\par
importance, DSS is now widely utilized in various fields,
\par
including medicine.
\par
CDSS is a computer-based decision-making system,
\par
which has been integrated with ICT and applied to clinical
\par
practice to achieve a wide range of medical knowledge.
\par
Typically, in CDSS, a computer perceives and processes
\par
the desired medical knowledge [9, 10]. This system should
\par
be able to provide appropriate services satisfying different
\par
requirements [11]. When compared to the past, CDSS now
\par
can aid in a more accurate diagnosis and treatment
\par
guidelines using artificial intelligence and various analysis
\par
techniques [12]. Thus, CDSS is able to process complex
\par
requirements of medical professionals [13] and can eval-
\par
uate whether the results of previous studies could influence
\par
patient treatment or not.
\par
It is noteworthy that past studies on conventional CDSS
\par
have rarely dealt with the approaches for simulation, us-
\par
ability test, perception research, and evaluation of socio-
\par
technical analysis. Therefore, in this regard, the current
\par
study aimed to analyze the content of research articles,
\par
based on methodology and content analysis, by reviewing
\par
decision-making related studies and investigate patterns in
\par
natural language text through unstructured analysis of
\par
performance or specific changes in treatment. Using these
\par
methodological studies, the academic outlook of medical
\par
informatics can be forecasted, and the direction of aca-
\par
demic research as well as the trend in academic develop-
\par
ment can also be provided.
\par
2 Related work
\par
2.1 Literature analysis
\par
With the advancement of ICT, a massive amount of med-
\par
ical information from different medical institutions can be
\par
maintained and controlled by computers through a com-
\par
puterized information system [14]. Healthcare providers
\par
can efficiently manage patients and medical costs by pro-
\par
cessing various medical data using the computerized sys-
\par
tem [15]. In recent years, advancement of CDSS has
\par
enabled medical practitioners to predict and automatically
\par
diagnoses diseases. Furthermore, a literature analysis can
\par
be of significant help in enhancing the CDSS performance
\par
in the field of medicine [16] and can serve as a useful
\par
starting point for developing a new decision-making
\par
methodology.
\par
Through a literature review on system improvement,
\par
Peleg M proved the development potential of CLG-based
\par
DSS by reviewing studies on computer-interpretable
\par
guidelines (CIGs) [17]. Moreover, attempts have been
\par
made to develop an anaphoric relation recognition system
\par
using a hybrid method by integrating the advantages of this
\par
recognition system based on rule, machine learning and
\par
semantic structure to realize an optimum performance.
\par
Most often, nouns mentioned in sentences are replaced by
\par
pronouns or other nouns in the following sentences in order
\par
to enhance the efficiency of language use. Anaphoric re-
\par
lation recognition is substantially important in a conver-
\par
sational agent system by identifying the relationship
\par
between these nouns [18]. Eadie et al. [19] examined
\par
heterogeneity problems on the purpose and function of
\par
computer-assisted diagnosis (CAD) system by reviewing
\par
147 sample studies on the application of CAD system in
\par
cancer diagnosis. An attempt has been made to demonstrate
\par
the potential enhancement of computational tools that can
\par
analyze complex problems by organizing and evaluating
\par
case studies through a literature analysis of healthcare risk
\par
factors [20]. Garg et al. [21] verified the effect of CDSS
\par
through literature review based on databases, such as
\par
MEDLINE and EMBASE. Through a literature analysis of
\par
databases, such as MEDLINE, PsycINFO and Sociological
\par
Abstracts, Jung et al. [22] performed a study, based on
\par
various cultural and demographic factors verifying pa-
\par
tients' preferences for healthcare.
\par
2.2 Text mining
\par
Text mining is a series of processes in a big data technique
\par
that seeks to extract meaningful information from textual
\par
sources [23]. Text mining is another way of knowledge
\par
discovery and has been defined as a method of exploring
\par
patterns in natural language texts to extract certain infor-
\par
mation for a specific purpose [24]. Through text mining
\par
from various textual sources, interesting and unusual
\par
knowledge had been discovered from unstructured text [18,
\par
25]. The method of text mining for natural language text
\par
has been commonly used in information retrieval. This
\par
method has drawn much attention in recent years after a
\par
rapid increase in textual data, since this application allows
\par
easy access to data acquisition. For this reason, the current
\par
new task is to review and find valuable information from an
\par
enormous amount of automatically collected textual data.
\par
Out of the several techniques in text mining, the semantic
\par
method is commonly used in identifying the basic signs
\par
and the relationship of these signs with their meanings.
\par
This approach is crucial in expanding original query words
\par
or calculating importance weight on specific topics. In
\par
123Inf Technol Manag (2016) 17:5--14 7
\par
relation to this, a study attempted to integrate a regular
\par
model in order to improve search capacity of the charac-
\par
teristics of term dependency based on the semantic tech-
\par
nique. This has provided an opportunity to establish a more
\par
efficient search model by detecting implications on query
\par
term relations and a ranking model integrating them [26].
\par
Application areas and used texts for text mining are
\par
extensively broad. Data processing for the analysis of sci-
\par
ence and technology is called science \& technology (S\&T)
\par
text mining [27]. Nowadays, acquisition and application of
\par
conceptual knowledge are actively performed [28]. Bio-
\par
medical textual mining is one of text mining techniques
\par
applied to articles in life science studies [29]. In addition to
\par
the life science area, text mining is frequently used in the
\par
field of clinical practice. A representative case, for exam-
\par
ple, is extraction of significant information through text
\par
mining of medical records for injury of elderly patients
\par
caused by fall [30]. Science studies applying text mining
\par
perform co-word analysis using key noun phrases extracted
\par
by database tomography [5]. Text mining is assumed to be
\par
applied since co-word analysis is generally used for textual
\par
data [31]. Database tomography is considered to be an
\par
application of text mining, since frequency analysis is done
\par
by extracting noun phrases from unstructured texts, in-
\par
cluding the title, abstract and the main text [32, 33].
