\par
3 Literature review methods
\par
PubMed is considered to be the premier bibliographic data-
\par
base and a search engine that primarily access the MEDLINE
\par
database, developed by the National Center for Biotech-
\par
nology Information (NCBI). PubMed comprises over 21
\par
million citations and abstracts for biomedical literature in-
\par
dexed in the MEDLINE database. MEDLINE citation data
\par
comprises of approximately 92 \% of the data distributed to
\par
PubMed, and sponsoring publishers provide bibliographic
\par
data to the National Library of Medicine (NLM) prior to
\par
journal publication. MEDLINE is the primary component of
\par
PubMed maintained by the NLM, which provide citations to
\par
journal articles in medicine, veterinary science, dentistry,
\par
nursing, health science, biotechnology and other medical
\par
bibliographic references. The current work conducted a lit-
\par
erature review on titles and abstracts from the MEDLINE
\par
database from January 1 to December 31, 2013.
\par
As shown in Fig. 1, articles without an abstract and im-
\par
pact factor but with keywords, each having its own unique
\par
meaning, were excluded in the first place. Following this, 30
\par
articles having the top three impact factors and containing
\par
the main cited keywords were reviewed. Keywords were
\par
CDS, CDSS, DSS, clinical decision, clinical decision sup-
\par
port, clinical decision support system, decision support
\par
system, decision support, expert system and decision
\par
4 Simulations
\par
4.1 Usefulness of CDSS
\par
Objectivity of theme interpretation has been enhanced
\par
through extraction of keywords. This process has fa-
