4.5 Evaluations of CDSS
\par
The results of CDSS evaluation are shown in Table 2.
\par
Eight studies were theoretical research. Ten studies were
\par
conducted on the system and its performance, while twelve
\par
studies verified the effectiveness of CDSS through assess-
\par
ment. These results indicated that, studies on improvement
\par
in systematic performance and effectively-used evaluations
\par
were conducted comparably.
\par
In Table 2, the major subjects and related diseases were
\par
grouped based on the analysis bythe multidimensional
\par
scaling, as shown in Fig. 2.
\par
Several data-related diseases stated in the articles are pre-
\par
sented in Table 3, which included diabetes, obesity, coronary
\par
artery, hypertension and malaria. There were four papers on
\par
12312 cancer, and a large number of studies investigated cancers with
\par
a high mortality rate. Two studies were performed on acute
\par
respiratory infections, and fourteen articles analyzed patient
\par
records or assessed CDSS. There were five papers on the
\par
evaluation of system performance (system trials). These studies
\par
were conducted for more rapid and accurate decision-making
\par
and improved hospital services through evaluation of the sys-
\par
tem. Moreover, five studies suggested that the quality of patient
\par
care services could be enhanced via simplifying the complex
\par
processes cost-effectively by providing CDSS service.
\par
4.6 Results of literature review and comparison
\par
of titles
\par
The analyses results of abstracts and titles were compared
\par
to examine the relevancy between the title and the content.
\par
The most commonly used words in the titles were 15
\par
``decision'', followed by 13 ``support'', 10 ``clinical'' and 9
\par
``system'' (including ``systems''). The main keywords were
\par
directly used in titles, and comparably used in abstracts.
\par
There were four studies on cancer in the abstracts and the
\par
word ``cancer'' was mentioned in all the four titles. This
\par
finding implies the trend of stating the related disease in the
\par
title. There were eight studies on theoretical research areas,
\par
and seven keywords were directly used in the titles. On the
\par
other hand, 12 studies were related to data, but the word
\par
``data'' was used only in two of the titles. According to the
\par
comparison of CDSS's outcomes proposed by the studies,
\par
the keywords were appropriately stated in the titles, in-
\par
cluding 4 ``care'', 4 ``improve'' (including ``improving''), 4
\par
``patient'', 3 ``health'', 3 ``evaluation'', 5 trial (including
\par
``trials''), and 4 patient (including ``patients''). In addition,
\par
the need for a rule for decision-making was directly men-
\par
tioned in the titles, with four keywords on rule, rules and
\par
rule-based. Although the outcomes and topics of the studies
\par
were clearly stated in the titles, it was difficult to identify
\par
the texts or the methods with data.
\par
In the comparison of the average Impact Factors by search
\par
term, the mean score of decision making was 39.06 and the
\par
overall mean score was 13.00, indicating whose influence on
\par
several academic fields. The average Impact Factor of arti-
\par
cles searched using acronyms such as CDS, CDSS and DSS
\par
was 3.01, slightly lower than the overall mean score. The
\par
cause of failure in tracking the information sources necessary
\par
for research is the lack of precise definition on research
\par
theme by many investigators. The theme and abstract of an
\par
article have to be written based on the significant content.
\par
5 Conclusions
\par
Decision-making system serves the role of supporting self-
\par
management and decision-making of patients for a better
\par
123
\par
Inf Technol Manag (2016) 17:5--14
\par
health management. According to the results of literature
\par
review, DSS was applied to various diseases, and there
\par
were a large number of studies on cancers with a high
\par
mortality rate. Although numerous studies used the rele-
\par
vant data in their methodology of research, only a few
\par
among them used the words ``data'' or ``method'' in their
\par
titles. There were studies on the performance and impact of
\par
DSS and better health management among physicians and
\par
patients by improving the accuracy of decision-making or
\par
exploring the time-saving methods.
\par
Based on the analysis of methodology and content
\par
study, the academic outlook of medical informatics could
\par
be forecasted, and the direction of academic research
\par
along with the trend in academic development could be
\par
provided. Through literature review, the overall flow in
\par
research topics and the areas of interest in medical in-
\par
formatics were identified and several conditions were
\par
investigated, such as preference and distribution of
\par
topics, and development trends of medical informatics.
\par
The quality of the research papers could be improved by
\par
tackling the problems arising from system development
\par
and realization processes, through analysis and inter-
\par
pretation of multilateral parameters, such as research
\par
topics and methods.
\par
This process provides ideas for an empirical study and
\par
helps investigators to select specific research questions.
\par
Moreover, literature review not only reveals the charac-
\par
teristics that have to be observed within the subjects but
\par
also helps to explain the facts that have been verified
\par
through observations. A theoretical study takes the role of
\par
increasing knowledge by invalidating or validating the
\par
theories of previous studies and suggesting new verifiable
\par
phenomena. A comparison of studies exploring identical
\par
topic areas enables us to identify the changes and degree of
\par
growth of a specific academic field.
\par
At present, it is difficult for a broad interpretation, since
\par
overall research areas have not been investigated. Further
\par
studies with broader research areas, analyses by author,
\par
citation frequency analysis and others are expected to en-
\par
sure the validity of results.
