\par
1. Introduction
\par
Nowadays e-commerce is becoming more and more popular in the
\par
business market, many manufacturers, such as Lenovo, Dell Computer,
\par
Sony, Clarks, Nike, Cisco System, and P\&G, are using online channel to
\par
sell their products directly to consumers, which leads to channel com-
\par
petition and conflict between the online and traditional channels. In
\par
order to alleviate the channel competition and conflict, the manufactur-
\par
er often offers a monetary support to its retailer to implement a local ad-
\par
vertising campaign (i.e., the manufacturer's cooperative advertising). In
\par
the business market, Apple, Dell, and HP are actively utilizing this strat-
\par
egy to help their retailers implement a local advertising campaign (Pei,
\par
Toombs, \& Yan, 2014). As more channels sell the same manufactured
\par
products, market competition becomes more intense, which makes
\par
demand information become a critical resource to firms.
\par
As an efficient tool in improving information precision, information
\par
sharing between supply chain players is becoming increasingly preva-
\par
lent (Gal-Or, Geylani, \& Dukes, 2008). Manufacturers often have wide
\par
ranging information about the entire market and the products, while
\par
retailers have point-of-sale data, deep knowledge about regional
