\par
consumer purchases, etc. Hence, when information sharing arrange-
\par
ments have been implemented, different signals can be pooled, yielding
\par
more accurate forecast information about market demand (Gal-Or et al.,
\par
2008). In the business market, Wal-Mart and Warner-Lambert, maker
\par
of Listerine brand mouthwash, agreed to share their demand informa-
\par
tion six months in advance of the expected retail sale date to improve
\par
order accuracy (Seifert, 2003). Apparel manufacturer VF and its retailer
\par
ShopK have benefited from exchanging information during product dis-
\par
tribution. Considering these successful examples, it seems intuitive that
\par
information sharing has a uniform effect on firm performance and al-
\par
ways can benefit the parties involved in such an arrangement. However,
\par
this result is not obvious and the strategy needs careful study.
\par
In this paper, we develop a conceptual framework to investigate how
\par
the manufacturer's cooperative advertising coordinates the dual-channel
\par
distributions under the environment of demand uncertainty and influ-
\par
ences the information sharing of supply chain players. We first present
\par
a profit maximization model to obtain optimal strategies under different
\par
settings for each firm: (1) the non-information sharing setting and
\par
(2) the information sharing setting. We then compare these strategies
\par
and derive the important results. Finally, we address the issue of informa-
\par
tion distortion while sharing information, and propose an effective mech-
\par
anism to eliminate any possible information distortion and thus an
\par
optimum result can be achieved.
\par
The rest of our paper is organized as follows. In the next section, we
\par
provide a summary of the relevant literature. Then we present our
\par
model framework and analysis. Finally, conclusions and managerial
\par
implications are presented.
