0148-2963/\copyright{} 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.710 2. Literature review
\par
2.1. Cooperative advertising and channel coordination
\par
A number of papers study the cooperative advertising for the
\par
channel coordination in the literature. For example, Huang, Li,
\par
and Mahajan (2002) study cooperative advertising in a manufacturer--
\par
retailer supply chain without considering price. They demonstrate that
\par
cooperative advertising plays a more important role on the supply
\par
chain performance in a partnership. Karray and Zaccour (2006) exam-
\par
ine the impact of cooperative advertising on firm's performance when
\par
the retailer introduces a private label to market. Their results reveal
\par
that the retailer would accept the cooperative advertising program
\par
only if the private label has a strong competition with the national
\par
brand. Yan (2010) shows that cooperative advertising helps improve
\par
the performances of both the manufacturer and the e-retailer effective-
\par
ly. Aust and Buscher (2012) reveal that vertical cooperative advertising
\par
in a cooperation relationship can help the manufacturer--retailer supply
\par
chain achieve the highest total profits and lead to lowest retail price for
\par
consumers. Furthermore, Jogensen and Zaccour (2014) and Aust and
\par
Buscher (2014) provide a good summary of work in cooperative adver-
\par
tising by discussing the studies done by Berger (1972), Berger and
\par
Magliozzi (1992), Li, Huang, Zhu, and Chau (2002), Yue, Austin, Wang,
\par
and Huang (2006), Karray and Zaccour (2006, 2007), Xie and Wei
\par
(2009), Yan (2010), He, Krishnamoorthy, Prasad, and Sethi (2011,
\par
2012), Aust and Buscher (2012), and Zhang, Gou, Liang, and Huang
\par
(2013). The aforementioned papers all focus on commonly defined co-
\par
operative advertising in which the retailer decides how much to
\par
spend on local advertising and the manufacturer shares the cost of
\par
invested advertising, while our research focuses on manufacturer's co-
\par
operative advertising in which the manufacturer decides how much
\par
monetary support should be provided to the retailer to implement a
\par
local advertising campaign and the retailer pays a zero percentage
\par
cost of invested advertising. In addition, there are several major differ-
\par
ences between the aforementioned papers and ours. First, the afore-
\par
mentioned papers do not focus on the issue of online vs. traditional
\par
channel competition, while we do. Second, the aforementioned papers
\par
do not address the strategic role advertising plays in the dual-channel
\par
(online vs. traditional channel) distributions, while we do. Third, the
\par
aforementioned papers do not consider the market uncertainty and
\par
the strategic importance of information, while we do. Finally, the afore-
\par
mentioned papers do not address how the advertising stimulates the re-
\par
tailer to distort its information in the information sharing, while we do.
\par
2.2. Information sharing in the dual-channel distributions
\par
A few studies examine information sharing between the retailer and
\par
the manufacturer in the environment of dual-channel distributions. Yue
\par
and Liu (2006) show that both the manufacturer and the retailer can
\par
benefit from information sharing only when the manufacturer's forecast
\par
is higher than the retailer's forecast. If the manufacturer's forecast is
\par
lower than the retailer's forecast, the manufacturer would benefit
\par
from information sharing while the retailer would not. Yan and Ghose
\par
(2010) examine the effect of forecast information accuracy on the
\par
performances of traditional and online retailers in the dual-channel
\par
distributions. Yan and Pei (2011) find that information sharing has a
\par
positive impact on the manufacturer, but has no impact on the retailer.
\par
The aforementioned papers do not address the important value of ad-
\par
vertising in the dual-channel distributions, while we do. Furthermore,
\par
the aforementioned papers do not address the issue of information dis-
\par
tortion when supply chain players share their information. In contrast,
\par
we address the issue of information distortion while sharing informa-
\par
tion and propose an effective mechanism to eliminate any possible in-
\par
formation distortion, so that both firms can achieve an optimum profit.
\par
To our knowledge, our research is the first one to address how the
\par
manufacturer's cooperative advertising coordinates the dual-channel
\par
R. Yan et al. / Journal of Business Research 69 (2016) 709--717
\par
distributions in an environment of demand uncertainty and involves
\par
in the information sharing and distortion in the extant literature. Specif-
\par
ically, when the manufacturer opens an online channel to compete with
\par
its retailer and provides a monetary support to the retailer to implement
\par
a local advertising to stimulate the market sales, the main questions
\par
addressed in our research can be summarized as follows:
\par
(a) Is it profitable to the manufacturer to provide a monetary support
\par
to the retailer to implement a local advertising campaign when
\par
there is no sharing of information or sharing of information?
\par
(b) Would both the manufacturer and the retailer benefit from their
\par
information sharing unconditionally, when the manufacturer's co-
\par
operative advertising is considered? If not, what condition(s) can
\par
be derived?
\par
(c) How does the manufacturer's cooperative advertising motivate
\par
the retailer to distort its shared information while the manufactur-
\par
er has a motivation to understate its forecast at the same time?
\par
What effective mechanism can be utilized to solve the problem
\par
of distortion of information and help the manufacturer and the
\par
retailer achieve an optimum profit?
\par
Based on our results, we derive optimal market strategies for both
\par
the manufacturer and the retailer to employ. Thus, business managers
\par
can benefit from our findings.
\par
3. Model framework
\par
In this paper, we consider a supply chain made up of a manufacturer
\par
and a traditional retailer. The manufacturer opens an online channel to
\par
sell its product directly to consumers. Consumers can buy products from
\par
either the online channel or the retailer. We assume that the manufac-
\par
turer and the retailer play a Stackelberg game, in which the manufactur-
\par
er is the leader and the retailer is the follower. Empirical study (Cotterill
\par
\& Putsis, 2000) shows that Stackelberg structure reflects a strategic
\par
interaction between the manufacturer and its retailer.
\par
When consumers buy from online, we consider an important pa-
\par
rameter g(g N 0) in our model. The parameter g represents the product
\par
compatibility to the web, which is the compatibility of the product with
\par
online sales based on the characteristics of the product and the nature of
\par
online channel. A substantial body of literature (e.g., Chiang, Chhajed, \&
\par
Hess, 2003; Yan, 2011) indicates that when the same product is pur-
\par
chased through an online channel, consumer's consumption value
\par
(i.e., amount willing to pay) about this product would be less than the
\par
identical product purchased through a traditional channel. Kacen,
\par
Hess, and Chiang (2002) provide further evidence that the product
\par
compatibility to the web, based on empirical analysis of data, turns
\par
out to be less than one for many product categories. The products
\par
that have a strong compatibility to the web often sell well online
\par
(e.g., books, digital products, magazines, software, and computers).
\par
However, few daily foods, beverages, autos, and real estates are sold on-
\par
line. In this study, our research focuses on these products with the value
\par
of product compatibility to the web in the interval of 0 b g b 1.
\par
