Proposition 2. When there is no sharing of information, it is in the interest of the manufacturer to offer a monetary support to the retailer to implement a
\par
local advertising campaign; particularly if the manufacturer's forecast is more accurate, the manufacturer would benefit more from such an offering; in the
\par
meantime, the retailer benefits from the offered manufacturer's cooperative advertising as well.
\par
Proposition 2 shows that the manufacturer's cooperative advertising helps coordinate the dual-channel distributions and improve the dual-
\par
channel performance. When there is no sharing of information, the manufacturer uses only its own forecast to estimate the market demand
\par
and invest in cooperative advertising to stimulate the market sales. The more accurate forecast helps improve the manufacturer's decision making
\par
on investing in cooperative advertising, which leads to higher profitability to the manufacturer. Furthermore, when advertising is invested in the
\par
market, the manufacturer can benefit from the increased sales due to the direct and indirect effectiveness of advertising on retail and online sales.
\par
Although the manufacturer pays the full cost of the invested advertising, the increased profit due to advertising is larger than the cost of the invested
\par
advertising. Consequently, it is beneficial for the manufacturer to offer a full support in advertising to the retailer. In the meantime, the retailer also
\par
can benefit from the manufacturer's cooperative advertising since this advertising helps increase the retail sales directly. Therefore, the important
\par
managerial implication is that the manufacturer needs to actively urge the retailer to implement a local advertising campaign and provide a full
\par
support to the retailer when there is no sharing of information. The retailer needs to take the manufacturer's offer and actively promote the
\par
manufacturer's product through local advertising.
\par
Next, the important question is what is the value of the manufacturer's cooperative advertising to both the manufacturer and the retailer when
\par
there is sharing of information? We thus investigate this question in the next section.
\par
