Belk College of Business
\par
9201 University City Blvd, Charlotte,
\par
NC 28223
\par
kzhao2@uncc.edu
\par
\textbf{Ram L. Kumar}
\par
University of North Carolina at Charlotte
\par
Belk College of Business
\par
9201 University City Blvd, Charlotte, NC 28223
\par
rlkumar@uncc.edu
\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
\emph{Drawing upon the resource-based theory of IT business value research, a firm's ability}
\par
\emph{to effectively integrate and deploy IT resources in combination with other resources,}
\par
\emph{can create unique competitive advantage and intangible assets for a company. This}
\par
\emph{study aims to investigate the impact of IT governance on IT capability, and the effects of}
\par
\emph{both IT governance and IT capability on firms' sustainable competitive advantage. We}
\par
\emph{hypothesize that both IT governance and IT capability are positively related to firm}
\par
\emph{performance, and IT governance is positively linked to IT capability. A research model}
\par
\emph{is proposed.}
\par
\textbf{Keywords: }IT governance, IT capability, IT business value, firm performance
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
In this paper, we examine the impact of IT governance on IT capability, and effects of both IT governance
\par
and IT capability on firms' market value creation and sustainable accounting performance. A firm's IT
\par
capability refers to its ability to mobilize and deploy IT-based resources in combination or co-present with
\par
other resources and capabilities (Bharadwaj 2000).
\par
The relationship between IT capability and firm performance has been studied by prior literature
\par
(Bharadway 2000; Santhanam and Hartono 2003; Muhanna and stoel 2010), which concludes that firms
\par
with superior IT capability achieve superior firm performance. IT business value research (Barua and
\par
Mukhopadhyay 1995; Mukhopadhyay 1995; Bharadwaj et al. 1999; Bharadwaj 2000; Santhanam and
\par
Hartono 2003; Brynjolffson and Hitt 2000) examines the impact of IT on business value and
\par
organizational performance. However, research to examine the effects of IT governance on IT capability is
\par
limited (Lim et al. 2012), and the information systems field lacks studies that simultaneously investigate
\par
the impacts of both IT governance and IT capability on firms' market value creation and sustainable
\par
accounting performance. To fill this gap, we intend to answer the following research questions in this
\par
study:
\par
\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014 }\textbf{1}\emph{IS Strategy, Structure, and Organizational Impacts}
\par
1. \emph{How is IT governance linked to IT capability?}
\par
\emph{2. How do both IT governance and IT capability impact firm performance?}
\par
To answer these questions, we draw upon and integrate three research streams to develop our model: (1)
\par
Studies that investigate the impact of IT capability on firm performance (Bharadwaj 2000; Santhanam
\par
and Hartono 2003; Wang and Alam 2007; Muhanna and stoel 2010). (2) Studies that examine the effects
\par
of corporate governance and IT governance on firm performance (Brown and Caylor 2006; Boritz and
\par
Lim working paper; Lunardi et al. 2009). (3) Study that examines the role of senior IT executives and IT
\par
governance on IT capability (Lim et al. 2012). IT business value literature (Barua and Mukhopadhyay
\par
1995; Mukhopadhyay 1995; Bharadwaj et al. 1999; Brynjolffson and Hitt 2000) helps us understand how
\par
investment and innovation in IT impact firms' business performance. IT capability literature (Bharadwaj
\par
2000; Santhanam and Hartono 2003; Wang and Alam 2007; Muhanna and stoel 2010) illustrates that IT
\par
capability is an important factor differentiating competitive firms from less competitive firms. IT
\par
governance literature (Amstrong and Sambamurthy 1999; Bassellier et al. 2003; Li et al. 2007) provides
\par
us a guideline with a theoretical basis to investigate firms' IT resources and their ability to effectively
\par
integrate and deploy IT resources in combination with other resources to create unique competitive
\par
advantage.
\par
Using a sample of U.S. firms ranked by Information Week based on their technology strategies and
\par
practices, we examine the impact of IT capability on firm performance. In particular, we examine how IT
\par
governance and IT capability help to achieve firms' competitive advantage using both a market value
\par
measure and a sustainable accounting performance measure. In addition, we study how IT governance
\par
affects IT capability.
\par
This study contributes to accounting information systems (AIS) and management information systems
\par
(MIS) literature in several ways. First, this is the first study to investigate the impact of IT governance on
\par
IT capability. Second, our study contributes to the IT business value literature by simultaneously
\par
examining the differential effects of IT governance and IT capability on firms' sustainable accounting
\par
performance and market valuation. Third, our study also contributes to the AIS literature with a
\par
comprehensive measurement of IT governance. Fourth, our study represents one of the few studies that
\par
empirically test the resource-based theory in the IT governance domain.
\par
The rest of the study is organized as follows. The next section provides the literature review. The
\par
subsequent section introduces the theoretical background and develops hypotheses. The following section
\par
describes the variables and presents our research model. Then, the proposed research methods and data
\par
collection procedures are illustrated. The final section discusses the expected contributions and
\par
implications of the study and provides some concluding comments.
\par
\textbf{Literature Review}
\par
A large body of research has explored the impact of IT capability on firm performance. The pioneering
\par
empirical study by Bharadwaj (2000) suggested a link between IT capability and firms' accounting-based
\par
measures of current performance. This study contends that IT capability creates unique competitive
\par
advantages and intangible assets for a firm and firms with a high IT capability achieve and sustain
\par
superior performance based on profit- and cost-based performance measures using data in the early
\par
1990s. Similar to Bharadwaj (2000), a subsequent analysis by Santhanam and Hartono (2003) controlled
\par
for prior financial performance, and concluded that firms with superior IT capability exhibit superior
\par
current and sustained firm performance when compared to average industry performance.
\par
Based on post year 2000 (Y2K) data, more recent studies have investigated the impact of IT capability on
\par
firms' competitive advantage with mixed results. Wang and Alam (2007) explored the relationship
\par
between IT capability and firm valuation, future earnings uncertainty and financial analysts' forecast
\par
accuracy, and found that IT capability is value-relevant and provides incremental explanatory power for
\par
firm valuation beyond traditional accounting information. Muhanna and Stoel (2010) used two unique
\par
archival data sets representing the immediate pre-Internet (1992-1994) and the post-Internet (1999-2006)
\par
commercialization eras to examine the effects of IT capability and IT spending on market value and actual
\par
accounting performance, and concluded that IT capability is value-relevant, and is positively associated
\par
with actual future earnings; However, Masli et al. (2011) considered the structural shifts in the return
\par
from IT capability over time, and examined the impact of superior IT capability on firm performance over
\par
\textbf{2 }\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014}the 1988-2007 period. Their findings suggested that firms with superior IT capability are able to attain
\par
higher firm performance levels until 1999. However, such performance advantage disappears in the post-
\par
1999 time period. Hence, it is necessary to better understand the impact of IT capability on firms' market
\par
valuation as well as sustainable accounting performance in the post-Internet eras.
\par
A further investigation of the role of IT capability on firms' competitive advantage is needed. In this study,
\par
we attempt to reconcile these seemingly conflicting results and advance our understanding of the
\par
association between IT and firm performance by proposing and testing a model that focuses on market
\par
valuation as well as firms' sustainable accounting performance. A key distinguishing feature of our study
\par
is that we simultaneously examine the effects of both IT governance and IT capability on firm
\par
performance. In contrast, prior studies have focused on investigating the effects of each of these two IT-
\par
related factors in isolation from each other.
\par
The most similar study to ours is a recent paper by Lim et al. (2012) who investigated the impact of the
\par
role of IT executives on the relationship between IT capability and firm performance. They concluded that
\par
there is a positive relationship between hierarchical power of senior IT executives and the likelihood that a
\par
firm will develop superior IT capability, and that the contribution of IT capability to a firm's\textbf{ }competitive
\par
advantage is much stronger in firms with powerful senior IT executives. However, Lim et al. (2012) only
\par
studied IT executives' role. In contrast, in our paper, we construct a comprehensive measurement of IT
\par
governance, in addition to IT executive's role, we also include other important factors (outside auditor
\par
oversight and IT leadership importance).
\par
\textbf{Theoretical Background and Hypotheses}
\par
The resource-based theory (RBV) has been used in IT business value research to answer the question of IT
\par
business value and competitive advantage from IT (Mata et al. 1995; Powell and Dent-Micallef 1997;
\par
Bharadwaj 2000; Wade and Hulland 2004; Ray et al. 2005). Drawing upon RBV theory, a firm's ability to
\par
effectively build, integrate and deploy IT resources in combination with other resources, can create unique
\par
competitive advantages and intangible assets for a company (Bharadwaj 2000; Santhanam and Hartono
\par
2003). The primary purpose of this study is to investigate how IT governance and IT capability affect the
\par
firms' market value and sustainable accounting performance. The reason that we use both market value
\par
measure and sustainable accounting performance measure is described in the section of variable
\par
definitions. To accomplish this, RBV theory, therefore, seems well positioned to inform examinations of
\par
the relationship between IT governance, IT capability and firm performance.
\par
\textbf{\emph{IT governance and firm performance}}
\par
According to previous literature of the impact of corporate governance structure on firm performance
\par
(Daily and Dalton 1993; Brown and Caylor 2006), we expect that there is a linkage between IT governance
\par
and firm performance since firms with stronger IT governance may carve out competitive advantage in
\par
driving technology decisions and remaining costs under control. IT governance is an integral part of
\par
corporate governance, and is implementing processes, structures, and relational mechanisms in the
\par
enterprise that enable both IT and business person to execute their responsibilities in support of
\par
IT/business alignment and the creation of IT business value (Grembergen and De Haes 2009; Wilkin and
\par
Chenhall 2010). Weill (2004) indicates that all organizations have IT governance. Firms with effective
\par
governance have actively designed a set of IT governance mechanisms (e.g., committees, processes, IT
\par
organizational structure, etc.) that encourage behaviors consistent with the firms' strategies and values
\par
(Weill 2004). Good IT governance draws upon corporate governance principles to manage and use IT to
\par
achieve superior firm performance. Boritz and Lim (working paper) have examined the relationship
\par
between IT governance and firm performance, and suggests that IT governance mechanisms contribute to
\par
improved firm performance after taking into account their impact on IT internal control material
\par
weaknesses. IT assets have been embedded in an organization's daily operations and strategies, such as
\par
transactions, processes, services and analysis. Studies suggest that a big portion of the business value
\par
generated by IT comes from complementarities between IT and organizational practices (Brynjolfsson
\par
and Hitt 2000; Basu and Jarnagin 2008). Effective IT governance differentiates the organizations' unique
\par
assets in use of IT, while ensuring compliance with the firms' overall mission, vision and principles.
\par
Therefore, we believe that firms' with effective and efficient IT governance may maintain unique assets in
\par
human IT resources such as IT skills and experience, IT-enabled resources such as IT knowledge assets
\par
\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014 }\textbf{3}\emph{IS Strategy, Structure, and Organizational Impacts}
\par
and IT processes, and are more likely to have competitive advantage to achieve superior firm
\par
performance.
\par
We consider three aspects of IT governance mechanisms: oversight (outside committees), leadership IT
\par
background and IT leadership importance. We believe that for firms with good oversight function, their
\par
outside and independent boards are more likely to effectively monitor the inside boards and leadership in
\par
using IT to realize IT/business alignment. Executive leadership has long been embraced as necessary for
\par
corporates to fully explore the benefits of IT (Freeman 1969; O'Toole 1966; Rockwell 1968; Chatterjee et
\par
al. 2001). Bassellier et al. (2003) state that the set of IT-related experiences that executives possess
\par
enables them to exhibit IT leadership in their area of business. IT experience increases their
\par
understanding of IT, which in turn enables them to increase their leadership in the IT domain. Top
\par
Managers, executives, board of directors and committee members are more likely to assume leadership in
\par
regard to IT when they have the appropriate IT experience and knowledge (Bassellier et al., 2003). Thus,
\par
we argue that firms with leadership IT background (e.g. IT skills and IT experience) may have unique
\par
human IT resources in using IT to create business value of IT. We also argue that firms with IT leadership
\par
importance, say, consider the critical role of IT leadership such as CIO position and compensation, longer
\par
tenured CIO, and IT strategy committee, are more likely to be motivated to make efficient IT investment,
\par
implementation and maintenance, and have stronger IT organizational structure to achieve business
\par
value of IT investment. As a result, it may achieve superior firm performance. Therefore, we propose the
\par
following hypotheses:
\par
\emph{H1a: IT governance will be positively associated with the firms' market valuation.}
\par
\emph{H1b: IT governance will be positively associated with the firms' sustainable accounting performance.}
\par
\textbf{\emph{IT capability and firm performance}}
\par
The definition of IT capability varies. Bharadwaj defines IT capability as ``its ability to mobilize and deploy
\par
IT-based resources in combination or co-present with other resources and capabilities'' (Bharadwaj 2000,
\par
pp. 171). Lim et al. refer to IT capability as firms' ability to integrate, build, and reconfigure IT with
\par
organizational and managerial processes in order to align with a rapidly changing competitive
\par
environment (Lim et al. 2012). Wang and Alam (2007) point that IT capability depends on how a firm
\par
uses IT investments with other resources in innovative ways to create unique competitive advantages and
\par
intangible assets, such as technical and managerial skills, knowledge-based assets, customer orientation
\par
and synergy (Wang and Alam 2007). In this study, we refer to IT capability as firms' ability to innovatively
\par
implement and deploy IT resources in the process of business to obtain IT/business strategies and create
\par
distinctive advantage.
\par
Organizational intangible assets have been recognized as important drivers of firms' competitive
\par
differentiates (Bharadwaj 2000). Organizations and IT users care more about whether IT investment
\par
creates intangible resources such as increased markets and sales, and bring business value for the firms.
\par
Building on RBV theory, firms' IT capability is valuable, rare, inimitable, and/or non-substitutable
\par
(Wernerfelt 1984; Newbert 2007; Lim et al. 2012). We believe that firms with superior IT capability are
\par
more likely to have compatible IT infrastructure, stronger human IT resources, and effective intangible
\par
IT-enabled resources. Therefore, such firms are much better at building and integrating innovative firm-
\par
specific IT resources with other business resources and managing the technical and market risks
\par
associated with the deployment and use of those resources. In addition, due to the stronger human IT
\par
resources comprising the technical and managerial IT skills, firms with superior IT capability are better
\par
able to make the right decisions about IT spending, IT investment and IT development, and they are more
\par
likely to turn those IT investments into true value in terms of enhanced productivity and efficiency,
\par
improved marketing reflections, increased product quality/differentiation, and improved customer
\par
service, and so on (Bharadwaj 2000; Muhanna and Stoel 2010). As a result, it will enhance firms'
\par
sustainable earnings and earnings potential, and improve firms' ability to deploy IT for strategic goals.
\par
This expectation in turn should be reflected in the firm's sustainable accounting performance and market
\par
value. This leads to the following hypotheses:
\par
\emph{H2a: IT capability will be positively associated with the firms' market valuation.}
\par
\emph{H2b: IT capability will be positively associated with the firms' sustainable accounting performance.}
\par
\textbf{4 }\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014}\textbf{\emph{IT governance and IT capability}}
\par
IT governance involves a set of mechanisms for ensuring the attainment of necessary IT capabilities (Loh
\par
and Venkatraman 1992; Henderson and Venkatraman 1993; De Haes and Grembergen 2005; Brown and
\par
Grant 2005). IT\emph{ }governance\emph{ }affects a firm's capability to leverage IT synergies across business units (Gu et
\par
al. working paper). Thus, we propose that there is relationship between IT governance and IT capability
\par
since IT governance has a positive impetus to achieve firms' superior IT capability.
\par
Firms with stronger IT governance are more likely to have the business and IT knowledge needed to
\par
nurture organizational learning. Daily and Dalton (1993) stated that outside board members may enhance
\par
the firms' reputation due to their own experience, accomplishment, and exposure. In addition, outside
\par
board members are aligned with the notion of resource independence theory indicates that the
\par
effectiveness and efficiency of the firm relies on the ability of key organizational members to act as
\par
boundary spanner and oversight function (Pfeffer and Salancik 1978; Daily and Dalton 1993). Therefore,
\par
we believe that firms with stronger oversight function, their outside board members (e.g. big 4 audit
\par
committees), are more likely to oversee the inside board members for IT activities and may enhance firms'
\par
ability to integrate IT resources in combining with other resources. In addition, if firms have more
\par
independent directors, they may have stronger monitor function to help them reduce firms' IT-related
\par
risks and lead to sustainable IT capability.
\par
IT Governance Institute indicates that IT governance is as critical at the board and management level as
\par
corporate governance, and provides frameworks to assist enterprise leaders ensure that IT supports
\par
business goals and maximizes IT investment, with appropriate management of risks and opportunities
\par
(Wilkin and Chenhall 2010). Firms whose leadership teams have more IT experience and knowledge are
\par
more likely to have ability and skills to deploy IT innovations. For example, given the fast growing of
\par
technical innovation for information systems, if firms' top management has IT experience, they are more
\par
likely to implement and infuse new technology to improve firms' productivity and promote firms'
\par
competence.
\par
In addition, with operational dependence on IT, CIO and IT strategic committee who have the
\par
fundamental knowledge about IT (including IT risk, expense and competitive risk) manage critical
\par
corporate information assets (Nolan and McFarlan 2005). Therefore, firms with IT leadership importance
\par
(e.g. CIO position and compensation, tenured CIO and IT strategy committee, etc.) are more likely to
\par
drive technology decisions and effectively manage critical corporate information assets, and result in
\par
superior capability to integrate their IT into business operations. Thus, we propose the following
\par
hypotheses:
\par
\emph{H3: IT governance will be positively associated with IT capability.}
\par
\textbf{Variable definitions and research model}
\par
Based on the hypotheses, firm performance is affected by firms' IT governance and IT capability. IT
\par
governance also plays a role in firms' IT capability. The research model is shown as Figure 1.
\par
\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014 }\textbf{5}\emph{IS Strategy, Structure, and Organizational Impacts}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Dependent variables (Firm performance measurements)}}
\par
We measure the firm performance in this study from both accounting performance and market value
\par
perspectives. Two measures of firm performance makes this paper more comprehensive compared to
\par
other studies such as Lim et al. 2012. We believe that IT capability and IT governance are not only linked
\par
to actual sustainable future earnings, they may also be associated with market expectations of future
\par
earnings. This is an improvement from previous studies which take one measurement into consideration.
\par
We use two measures of firm performance: Average return on assets (AROA) and Tobin's q. Return on
\par
assets (ROA) identifies a companies' ability to generate profits from its assets, and has been widely used
\par
in previous studies (Hitt and Brynjolfsson 1996; Tam 1998; Barua et al. 1995; Rai et al. 1997; Floyd and
\par
Wooldridge 1990; Bharadwaj 2000; Dehning and Stratopouslos 2002). However, ROA measure only
\par
focuses on the current year profitability. To capture the long-term and sustainable profitability, we use the
\par
average of ROA over three years as the measure of sustainable accounting performance. The consideration
\par
of multiple years into the future allows for a possible time lag between investments in IT or IT capability
\par
and realization of potential value (Muhanna and Stoel 2010). It is an improved measurement of firms'
\par
sustainable accounting performance. Average ROA over three years (AROA) is calculated is
\par
( \emph{t ROA }+ 1 
\par
\emph{t ROA }+ 2
\par
\emph{t ROA })/3.
\par

\par
In addition, we use Tobin's q as market value of firm performance, which is a forward-looking, risk-
\par
adjusted, and less susceptible to changes in accounting practices. Tobin's q has been widely used to
\par
represent the market expectations of future firm performance and can be more likely to capture and
\par
represent IT contribution to intangible value (Bharadwaj et al. 1999; Masli et al. 2011; Lim et al. 2012).
\par
Consistent with previous literature (Chung and Pruitt 1994; Masli et al. 2011; Lim et al. 2012; Bharadwaj
\par
et al. 1999), Tobin's q is a ratio of market value to book value of total assets, and is calculated as:
\par
Tobin's q = (MVE+PS+DEBT)/TA, where MVE = market value of equity = (closing price of share at the
\par
end of the fiscal year)*(number of common shares outstanding);
\par
PS = liquidating value of the firm's outstanding preferred stock;
\par
DEBT = (current liabilities -- current assets) + (book value of inventories) + (long term debt), and TA =
\par
book value of total assets.
\par
Therefore, both AROA and Tobin's q serve as the dependent variables in this study.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Independent variables (IT capability measurements)}}
\par
IT capability superiority: we use the top ranking of the annual \emph{IW500} as a proxy for firms that have
\par
superior IT capability. We code a firm as 1 if it appears in the top ranking as ``IT leaders'' in \emph{IW500};
\par
otherwise we code it as 0.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Independent variables (IT governance score)}}
\par
\emph{IT governance:} we construct the IT governance matrix, which is developed upon the corporate
\par
governance literature (Brown and Caylor 2006) since IT governance is one aspect of corporate
\par
governance. According to the literature, G-index and E-index seem to be two of the most widely used
\par
corporate governance measures. G-index is constructed by the data collected by IRRC. Basically they are
\par
the composite measures of 24 corporate governance provisions that each company has. E-index is
\par
constructed by 6 measures out of 24 measures. However, corporate governance elements collected by
\par
IRRC is one dimension of corporate governance, which is about shareholder's right and conflict between
\par
managers and shareholders. Both G-index and E-index focus on external governance (Brown and Caylor
\par
2006). In addition to external governance, we try to capture internal governance related to
\par
managers/board of directors' IT-related characters. Therefore, we follow the Gov-Score constructed by
\par
Brown and Caylor 2006, and construct ITGOV-score representing both internal and external IT
\par
governance. Gov-Score is more relevant to what we intend to achieve in this paper. Gov-Score is created
\par
as a summary governance measure based on 51 firm-specific provisions representing both internal and
\par
\textbf{6 }\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014}external governance. Similarly, we construct ITGOV-score based on both internal and external IT
\par
governance factors including oversight, leadership IT background, and IT leadership importance. This
\par
construct is also related to the definition of IT governance. IT background and importance are factors
\par
since they are driving force for effective and efficient IT governance to help to ensure the fusion of
\par
business and IT. The reason we include oversight factors in our measurement is because the board
\par
effectiveness in its monitoring function is determined by its independence, size, and composition (insider
\par
and outsider) (John and Senbet 1998), and the oversight function is to control the formulation and
\par
implementation of IT strategy.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Control variables}}
\par
Based upon a review of prior studies on IT investment and firm performance, we control for firm size,
\par
earnings and possible halo effect of prior performance which may have impact on firms' IT capability
\par
superiority. We include one-year sales growth rate (SG) in our model to control for future earnings
\par
growth. Market-to-book value ratio (MB) is included to control for future growth potential. In addition,
\par
we control for advertising (ADV), research and development (R\&D), and capital (CAP) expenditures that
\par
are potentially value-relevant intangible assets not included on the balance sheet, and might be associated
\par
with firm performance. The definition and description of the variables in our model are summarized in
\par
Table 1.
\par
