\textbf{Proposed Research Methodology}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Data sources and collection}}
\par
Following the prior studies (Bharadwaj 2000; Santhanam and Hartono 2003; Muhanna and Stoel 2010,
\par
Lim et al. 2012), \emph{InformationWeek} 500 (\emph{IW}500) annual ranking index will be used in this study to
\par
identify firms with superior IT capability. \emph{IW500} is a ranking system for IT investment and innovation,
\par
published annually by Information Week. \emph{IW}500 ranks firms by the quantity of a firm's technology or
\par
service investments as well as the quality of the firm's innovative use of IT resources (InformationWeek
\par
500 1995, Wang and Alam 2007). The companies listed on the \emph{IW500} are public firms with revenue
\par
greater than \$250 million. In this study, due to the availability and feasibility of data collection, we use a
\par
more recent data set from the period 2009-2010 to test our hypotheses regarding the value relevance and
\par
sustainable accounting performance of IT capability and IT governance. We follow Santhanam and
\par
Hartono (2003)'s study, and use top ranking firms that rated annually as leaders since we argue that firms
\par
rated in the top rankings may have more innovative investment in IT resources, and stronger ability to
\par
integrate their IT resources with other resources to realize business strategies. We generated a total of 248
\par
firms with superior IT capability. We select 248 control firms from Compustat data by matching the firm
\par
size and industry, and it results in 496 firms as our total sample.
\par
We collect financial data from annual Compustat database, such as firms' total assets, earnings, and book
\par
value of equity, etc. We retrieve firm stock data from CRSP database and obtain IT governance data from
\par
the combination of proxy statement, firms' website, and Mergent Online database.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Two-stage econometrics methods}}
\par
A two-stage econometric method will be used to test the chain hypothesis that IT governance will affect
\par
firms' IT capability superiority, and in turn drives firm performance. Since IT capability firms are ranked
\par
by the quantity of a firm's technology or service investments as well as the quality of the firm's innovative
\par
use of IT resources based on surveys, there are some unobserved variables (omitted variables), for
\par
example, firms' strategies and standards, which are likely to be correlated with our explanatory variable of
\par
IT capability. Therefore, IT capability can be an endogenous explanatory variable. To control for
\par
endogeneity, two-stage estimation will be used in our study.
\par
\textbf{8 }\emph{Thirty Fifth International Conference on Information Systems, Auckland 2014}\textbf{Expected contributions and implications}
\par
IT investment has become increasingly important in strategic decisions making for organizations, and IT
\par
business value remains one of the most interesting questions for researchers and practitioners. Prior
\par
studies have argued that IT capability, a firm's ability to effectively integrate IT resources together with
\par
other organization resources, can create unique competitive advantages and intangible assets for an
\par
organization (Bharadwaj 2000; Santhanam and Hartono 2003; Muhanna and Stoel 2010). In this study
\par
we propose coupling IT governance with IT capability to evaluate their impacts on firm performance using
\par
both market-based firm valuation framework and sustainable accounting performance measurement.
\par
This study is expected to make several contributions to the growing literature on the business value of IT
\par
capability. First, this study documents that IT capability adds to a firm's forward-looking measure of firm
\par
performance and sustainable accounting performance beyond prior accounting performance. Second, this
\par
study also fills a void in prior literature by examining how IT governance affects IT capability. Third, this
\par
study extend the prior studies by simultaneously investigate the impacts of both IT governance and IT
\par
capability on firms' competitive advantages. Finally, in this study we demonstrate a new way to construct
\par
a comprehensive measure of IT governance using secondary data.
\par
This study should also be of interest to professionals that guide boards and executive management and
\par
leadership teams in making IT investment decisions and using IT to create business value.
\par
