ABSTRACT
\par
In river basins that cross jurisdictional boundaries, water quality
\par
degradation has become a crucial problem and results in
\par
intensive competition among water users, especially in developing
\par
countries. Under this situation, implementing water quality
\par
management and control across jurisdictional boundaries can
\par
strengthen watershed pollution controls, and prevent pollution
\par
from being passed on and promote active pollution control within
\par
the related jurisdictional regions. This study examined the
\par
limitations of the current trans-boundary water quality
\par
management system in China and reviewed the planning and
\par
implementation of two pilot integrated trans-boundary water
\par
management systems established in the Jiangsu and Zhejiang
\par
provinces. Based on our ndings, we proposed a new policy
\par
framework for trans-boundary water quality management. As a
\par
rst step toward integrated watershed management in China, this
\par
policy framework can help assess the actual water pollution status
\par
of various regions and serve as a basis for an integrated
\par
watershed management system. The framework can be easily
\par
applied in other countries with trans-boundary water pollution
\par
issues, particularly in the context of developing countries.
