\par
sources, and excessive discharge violations have been relatively rare (Gander, 2014; Lee
\par
et al., 2012; Qi \& Altinakar, 2013). In China, however, trans-boundary pollution disputes
\par
are mostly caused by environmental incidents and incidental spills or by inconsistencies
\par
within the water quality objectives of different administrative regions (Millington \&
\par
Ongley, 2003; Zeitoun, Goulden, \& Tickner, 2013). Overall, the international community
\par
has been moving away from the traditional`command + control' dispute resolution
\par
model to a`consultation + participation' model for trans-boundary water pollution (Carri-
\par
ger, Fisher, Stockton, \& Sturm, 2013; Douven et al., 2014; Haregeweyn, Berhe, Tsunekawa,
\par
Tsubo, \& Meshesha, 2012; Mutekanga, Kessler, Leber, \& Visser, 2013; Rasul, 2014). Although
\par
this solution model is more time-consuming, it is more likely to be accepted by the various
\par
parties involved in the dispute (Bidwell \& Ryan, 2006; De Bruyne \& Fischhendler, 2013;
\par
Smith, 2013). In addition, there are many similarities between international trans-boundary
\par
watershed pollution problems and those across administrative regions within a single
\par
country, and this management is usually based on each country's domestic laws and
\par
watershed agreements (Chen, Pernetta, \& Duda, 2013; Johnson, Ravnborg, Westerman,
\par
\& Probst, 2002). The solution model for international trans-boundary water pollution pro-
\par
blems can provide a useful reference for China.
\par
In China, full implementation of an integrated watershed management system faces
\par
certain dif culties. Assessment of the water environment of an administrative region (typi-
\par
cally smaller than a province) is primarily conducted via the local management of pollution
\par
sources and water quality. The following issues were identi ed in our study. First, it is dif-
\par
cult to measure the total discharge from pollution sources, and access to relevant and
\par
accurate information is often subject to interference from the local administration.
\par
Second, in some areas, local pollution is transferred downstream by relocating the pollut-
\par
ing enterprises. Third, the current system, which only pays attention to pollution within an
\par
administrative region such as a county or autonomous region, is not conducive to either
\par
solving trans-boundary water pollution problems or forming a reasonable early warning
\par
and emergency response system for trans-boundary water pollution. Therefore, in the
\par
present circumstances, it is crucial to establish a water environmental management
\par
system based on administrative areas within a river basin, as well as the trans-boundary
\par
(including provincial, city and county administrative boundary) river water quality stan-
\par
dards. This is a feasible rst step toward fully integrated watershed management, and
\par
will contribute to achieving China's water pollution reduction goals and improving the
\par
regional water environment.
\par
In this paper, the status quo of the trans-boundary water quality management system
\par
and its implementation in China are examined, and two case studies for Zhejiang Province
\par
and Jiangsu Province are presented. Based on intensive investigations in the major river
\par
watersheds in China, a policy framework for trans-boundary water quality management
\par
and related implementation system is designed. In this policy framework, the trans-bound-
\par
ary water quality objectives are considered as an important pillar. In this way, the actual
\par
water pollution status of various regions can be assessed, and subsequently, an appropri-
\par
ate administrative accountability system can be established. Under the policy framework,
\par
an early warning and emergency response system is proposed to reduce incidents of river
\par
pollution and the resulting losses. Mechanism for regional environmental compensation
\par
between the upstream and downstream watersheds is also discussed based on scienti c
\par
trans-boundary water quality objectives. The methodology and policy frameworkENVIRONMENTAL HAZARDS 115
\par
developed in this work can be also referenced for other countries with similar trans-
\par
boundary water quality issues.
\par
2. Status quo of China's trans-boundary water quality management
\par
system
\par
2.1. The current legal basis and issues
\par
China's major watersheds often cross provincial administrative regions, and pollution dis-
\par
putes often occur between the upstream and downstream provinces. In the absence of
\par
clear criteria and appropriate management, such disputes require a long time to be prop-
\par
erly addressed. Some provisions in current Chinese laws and regulations are related to this
\par
issue. For example, in the Water Pollution Prevention and Control Law (2008 Revision),
\par
Article 12 stipulates that the environmental protection department under the State
\par
Council may establish water environment quality standards applicable to water bodies
\par
of major river and lake basins within the provincial boundaries. The standards should
\par
be established together with the water conservancy administration department and the
\par
provincial government concerned. Article 26 stipulates that water resources protection
\par
agencies for major river and lake basins determined as such by the State shall be respon-
\par
sible for monitoring the water environment quality conditions of such basins within the
\par
boundaries of the provinces where they are located and shall, without delay, report the
\par
monitoring of ndings to the environmental protection department under the State
\par
Council and the water conservancy administration department under the State Council.
\par
According to the Water Pollution Prevention and Control Law (2008 Revision) and the
\par
related regulations, the Ministry of Environmental Protection must set the environmental
\par
standards for cross-jurisdictional boundary water quality, and watershed water resources
\par
protection agencies are responsible for monitoring the environmental quality of water
\par
bodies at provincial borders. However, the laws and regulations specify neither how to
\par
supervise and manage the water quality for such bodies of water, nor how to enforce
\par
the laws and regulations. Moreover, there are no clear stipulations on how to track the
\par
responsibilities of upstream administrative regions when the quality of jurisdictional
\par
boundary waters does not meet the standards. In other words, if cross-jurisdictional
\par
boundary water quality does not meet the applicable environmental standards, it is
\par
very dif cult to take a speci c action regarding both the law and practice. Similarly, exist-
\par
ing laws do not stipulate that the Ministry of Water Resources is responsible for maintain-
\par
ing a certain level of river ow to ensure that the river meets the water quality standards.
\par
Therefore, to prevent disputes, it should be clearly stipulated whether the Ministry of
\par
Water Resources or the Ministry of Environmental Protection are responsible to supervise
\par
and manage violations of the current cross-jurisdictional boundary water quality
\par
standards.
\par
The disproportionate growth of the regional economy of various regions has made
\par
trans-boundary water pollution problems in various major Chinese watersheds increas-
\par
ingly prominent (Wu \& Zheng, 2013; Zhang et al., 2011; Zhao, Qian, \& Huang, 2012).
\par
Numerous incidents of cross-jurisdictional water environment pollution have occurred
\par
with a signi cant social impact, and cross-jurisdictional disputes on water quality fre-
\par
quently occur (Chen et al., 2013; Marsden, 2011). Causes that directly contribute to116 W. ZHANG ET AL.
\par
these problems are the mismanagement of pollution sources in various regions, wide-
\par
spread excess discharges from private enterprises, and the discharge of large amounts
\par
of untreated municipal sewage into rivers. Moreover, further causes include the lack of leg-
\par
islative norms, poor enforcement of national and local regulations, defective management
\par
system, prevalence of local protectionism, lack of integrated management of both water
\par
resources and pollution sources regarding the watershed, insuf cient monitoring of the
\par
water environment, and lack of public participation. Both institutional reform and the
\par
implementation of an integrated watershed management system will play an essential
\par
role in solving this problem. Water pollution controls require the combination of the
\par
watershed and administrative management systems. Due to the lack of effective legisla-
\par
tive and administrative procedures to solve trans-boundary water pollution problems,
\par
the environmental protection and water resources authorities are unable to effectively
\par
perform their duties. Therefore, it is dif cult for the watershed management to be truly
\par
effective. Once trans-boundary water pollution problems occur, they are often not prop-
\par
erly solved.
\par
2.2. Cases of local water quality management
\par
China has earned recognition for remarkable economic development. Over the past
\par
decades, the economic strength of Jiangsu Province and Zhejiang Province continuously
\par
ranks among the tops in the country. At the same time, the accumulation and discharge of
\par
pollutants into the waters of many upstream regions have wrought environmental havoc
\par
in downstream jurisdictions, creating inequities in distribution similar to the uneven
\par
nature of the nation's watershed development. The rapid growth of trans-boundary
\par
water pollution in the two provinces mirrors the general situation in China; therefore,
\par
their trials for watershed management can provide references for other regions in the
\par
country.
\par
2.2.1. Jiangsu Province
\par
Jiangsu Province boasts a network of rivers and lakes (Figure 1). There are three major
\par
watersheds in Jiangsu Province: (1) Yangtze River watershed: the Yangtze River runs
\par
over 400 km through the province from west to east; (2) Huaihe River watershed: the
\par
Grand Canal runs 690 km from north to south, and other rivers include Qinhuai River,
\par
Northern Jiangsu General Irrigation Canal, Xinmu River, and Nantong-Yanzhou Canal;
\par
and (3) Taihu Lake watershed: this watershed includes Taihu Lake and Hongze Lake.
\par
Trans-boundary water pollution disputes have seriously affected regional environmental
\par
security and social stability. Therefore, an assessment program for water quality at
\par
water sections was implemented in Jiangsu Province in 2009, and a watershed manage-
\par
ment system was established based on the water quality assessment. Implementation
\par
rules for addressing trans-boundary water disputes are stipulated in the Jiangsu Province
\par
Regional Compensation Rules for Environmental Resources (2008 version). According to
\par
the rules, if the pollutant index of the exiting water section fails to meet the standards,
\par
the upstream administrative region should provide appropriate environmental compen-
\par
sation to the downstream region. For the administrative region directly discharging into
\par
Taihu Lake, if the pollutant index of the exiting water section fails to meet the standards,
\par
this region should pay compensation to the province. The compensation is 15,000 RMBENVIRONMENTAL HAZARDS 117
