2.2.2. Zhejiang Province
\par
Zhejiang Province borders Jiangsu Province and Shanghai municipality (Figure 3). The
\par
north of the province lies just south of the Yangtze Delta, and consists of plains around
\par
the cities of Hangzhou, Jiaxing, and Huzhou, where the Grand Canal enters from the north-
\par
ern border to end at Hangzhou. Major rivers include the Qiantang River, Yong River, and
\par
Tiaoxi River. Serious trans-boundary con icts over water pollution have continued under
\par
the traditional water management mode. In 2009, Zhejiang Province implemented the
\par
Zhejiang Province Assessment Method for Trans-boundary River Junction Water Quality
\par
Protection Management. It assesses the water protection and management of administra-
\par
tive regions at the river junctions, and the assessment results are used to evaluate the per-
\par
formance of governmental of cials and involved in estimating payment transfers.
\par
Implementation of this regulation is based on the standard of three water quality par-
\par
ameters: COD, NH3-N, and TP. The assessment is conducted once per year, and the assess-
\par
ment subjects cover the administrative regions including both cities and counties. The118 W. ZHANG ET AL.
