\par
3. Design of a policy framework for trans-boundary water quality
\par
management
\par
3.1. The trans-boundary water quality objectives
\par
Development of trans-boundary water quality objectives helps with accurately assessing
\par
upstream and downstream water quality. Based on the assessment result, appropriate
\par
environmental accountability can be implemented to promote active pollution control
\par
across various regions. Moreover, the water quality objectives may help to determine pol-
\par
lution responsibilities, reduce cross-municipal boundary water pollution disputes, and
\par
establish necessary early warning and emergency response system. Environmental com-
\par
pensation can be also determined on the basis of the accomplishment of the water120 W. ZHANG ET AL.
\par
Figure 4. Temporal changes in the average value of water quality parameters in four watersheds in
\par
Zhejiang Province from 2007 to 2012.
\par
quality objectives. The trans-boundary water quality objectives should be jointly devel-
\par
oped by the upstream and downstream regions. The water quality objectives applying
\par
to the cross-jurisdictional boundary sections should be determined based on the function
\par
of the water body and the economics and technology feasibility of the region. The water
\par
quality objective at the cross-jurisdictional boundary sections is not simply to comply with
\par
standard limits but, rather, is a system of series of targets and response mechanism. Such a
\par
system should help determine the water quality status in different regions and assist in
\par
establishing an administrative accountability system to resolve upstream and downstream
\par
disputes. Based on the water quality objectives, an early warning and emergency response
\par
system can be established.
\par
The owchart of the water quality objective system is illustrated in Figure 5. In principle,
\par
the system for the water quality objectives is suggested including three elements: initial
\par
target, starting target, and emergency target.
\par
Figure 5. Flowchart of the water quality objective system.ENVIRONMENTAL HAZARDS 121
\par
(1) Initial target: The initial target can be determined according to the environmental
\par
functions of the water bodies at the cross-jurisdictional boundary sections. If the
\par
water pollution level is below the initial target, no intervention is required. Routine
\par
monitoring and reporting (information sharing between the upstream and down-
\par
stream regions) are required.
\par
(2) Starting target: The starting target represents signi cant pollution in water bodies. The
\par
starting target corresponds to the condition that the normal water function is
\par
damaged, and its values are set to three times the value of the initial target. A
\par
water pollution level between the initial target and the starting target indicates that
\par
the degree of water pollution is signi cant, and an early warning should be issued.
\par
The environmental protection department of the upstream region should promptly
\par
inform the environmental protection department of the downstream regions. The fre-
\par
quency of water quality monitoring should be increased, and actions should be taken
\par
on water intakes in the downstream regions.
\par
(3) Emergency target: The emergency target represents the occurrence of pollution acci-
\par
dents such as abnormal pollutant discharges. The emergency target values are set as
\par
six times the value of the initial target. If the water pollution level is between the start-
\par
ing target and the emergency target, the environmental protection department of the
\par
upstream region should promptly inform the environmental protection department of
\par
the downstream regions, and both the upstream and downstream regions should
\par
follow the pre-established emergency response plan.
\par
If one or more water quality indicators exceed the emergency target, the environmental
\par
protection department in the upstream area needs to promptly inform both the environ-
\par
mental protection department in the downstream areas and a higher level authority.
\par
Based on a pre-established plan, the upstream and downstream areas must take immedi-
\par
ate emergency actions on the upstream discharge control, water quality monitoring, and
\par
downstream water intakes.
\par
The above three water quality targets include only the routine water quality indicators.
\par
Therefore, for the emergent pollution incidents caused by toxic and harmful substances,
\par
the level of response should be determined by the environmental protection departments
\par
of both the upstream and downstream areas as well as the watershed water resources pro-
\par
tection authorities. After establishing a water quality objective system across the municipal
\par
boundaries, the environmental protection departments in both the upstream and down-
\par
stream areas can establish a water quality management system based on these objectives.
\par
Such a system should include detailed monitoring, reporting, warning measures, and
\par
emergency plans. This management system can be linked to the water environmental
\par
function zone, total maximum loads, water allocation, drinking water source management,
\par
and wastewater discharge control. In this way, a comprehensive information system can
\par
be established to provide effective decision supports.
\par
3.2. An accountability system based on trans-boundary water quality objectives
\par
According to China's environmental management system, the local government (includ-
\par
ing provincial, city, and county level) is responsible for the environmental quality of122 W. ZHANG ET AL.
\par
their administrative areas. Therefore, an administrative accountability system based on the
\par
trans-boundary water quality objectives should be established. The environmental protec-
\par
tion performance in both upstream and downstream regions can be assessed by a panel of
\par
experts. The assessment results can be rated as excellent, good, pass, or fail, as rec-
\par
ommended in Table 1.
\par
Local governmental of cials from adjacent upstream and downstream areas are
\par
responsible for convening the departments in charge of environmental protection,
\par
water conservation, sheries, and urban construction. The primary topic of the joint con-
\par
sultation is to both inform each other of trans-boundary river water quality and discuss
\par
control of trans-boundary water pollution, as well as the operation of locks, dams, and
\par
sh farms. According to the three-level trans-boundary water quality target system, at
\par
the initial target, the environmental protection, water conservancy, and shery depart-
\par
ments from adjacent upstream and downstream areas should communicate information
\par
about the trans-boundary water quality, the amount of water ow, the operation of locks
\par
and dams, and sh farming. If water quality is between the initial and starting targets, the
\par
environmental protection department in the upstream area should inform the environ-
\par
mental protection department in the downstream area the same day that the data
\par
were acquired. If the water quality is between the starting and emergency targets, the
\par
environmental protection department in the upstream area should immediately inform
\par
the environmental protection department in the downstream area, and both should
\par
take the measures detailed by a pre-established plan. In addition, there should be daily
\par
communication regarding water discharge through locks and dams. The environmental
\par
protection departments of the upstream and downstream areas in charge of permitting
\par
projects shall notify the relevant departments of the environmental impact caused by
\par
the local construction projects, so that these areas can evaluate the extent and type of
\par
possible pollution. This noti cation shall include the nature and purpose of the project
\par
and the nature and extent of the project's potential impact. If the upstream and down-
\par
stream areas cannot reach a consensus regarding the potential trans-boundary impact
\par
of the construction project, the dispute can be submitted to the provincial environmental
\par
protection departments.
\par
Based on the shared information and joint meetings, the environmental protection
\par
departments for the upstream and downstream areas can form a joint inspection group
\par
and conduct on-site inspections of water pollution controls in both areas. These inspec-
\par
tions strengthen the supervision and implementation of current environmental protection
\par
measures, such as those for major sources of watershed pollution and urban sewage treat-
\par
ment plants. Adjacent shery authorities for trans-boundary rivers can also consult with
\par
each other to form a joint inspection group to conduct on-site inspections of trans-
