3.3. Emergency communications and response mechanisms
\par
Establishing an early warning and emergency response system is necessary to reduce
\par
incidents of water pollution and the resulting losses. Design of this system depends
\par
on good trans-boundary water quality objectives and their implementation. If water
\par
quality is above the`emergency target' set in the trans-boundary water quality objec-
\par
tives, or if a polluting incident occurs in the upstream and downstream areas, then
\par
the governments in both areas should immediately initiate an emergency response
\par
plan. The party who discovers the water pollution shall immediately notify the other
\par
party, and provide enough technical information to assess the type and extent of pol-
\par
lution. The content of this notice includes the nature and extent of the pollution incident,
\par
the situation that requires an emergency response, and the status of the pollution
\par
incident.
\par
On-site sampling and monitoring data are usually critical for accountability determi-
\par
nation and dispute resolution. In case that the`emergency target' is exceeded, the
\par
environmental protection departments in the upstream and downstream areas should
\par
arrive at the site within the speci ed time to sample the relevant section and pollution
\par
sources. If one party is absent for no reason or does not monitor according to the regu-
\par
lations, the monitoring data obtained by the other party are used for all subsequent
\par
decisions. If there is a dispute about the monitoring data between the two parties, the
\par
water samples should be kept, and provincial environmental monitoring authorities will
\par
be in charge of analysis. Monitoring data obtained from provincial monitoring authorities
\par
can be used as the basis for determining the trans-boundary water pollution incident. The
\par
government and environmental protection department of the upstream area should
\par
immediately take measures, such as limiting production or temporarily suspending pro-
\par
duction, to block the pollution source.
\par
For fairness and impartiality, the upstream and downstream areas may both have the
\par
right to go to the other party's area to investigate and collect evidence of the pollution
\par
incident caused by an upstream sewage discharge and notify the relevant department
\par
from the other party. If the relevant departments in the downstream area believe that
\par
there is undue delay in the handling of the incident, they can immediately report the
\par
delay to the provincial environmental protection department to investigate or take correc-
\par
tive action. For the dams identi ed as important by both the upstream and downstream
\par
areas, when the water quality is abnormal and needs discharging in the upstream area, the
\par
downstream regional government should be promptly informed. The upstream and
\par
downstream governments should take full account of the interests of both parties and
\par
any objective conditions and timely consult with each other about the discharge plan.
\par
In the case of a water discharge, the water conservation department in the upstream124 W. ZHANG ET AL.
\par
area should notify those in the downstream area in advance so that the downstream area
\par
may take preventive measures.
\par
3.4. Disputes coordination and compensation
\par
Regional environmental compensation between the upstream and downstream water-
\par
sheds is important for its comprehensive development, and both payments from the
\par
central government and compensation between the upstream and downstream regions
\par
should be based on reasonable and scienti c trans-boundary water quality objectives.
\par
Trans-boundary water pollution disputes can be jointly consulted and handled between
\par
the upstream and downstream governments in accordance with law. If a consensus
\par
cannot be reached after the consultation, the incident can be reported to the provincial
\par
government for investigation and decision. If the investigation determines that an
\par
upstream sewage discharge caused the downstream pollution incident, the government
\par
in the upstream area shall be responsible for determining the cause and the polluter pri-
\par
marily responsible for the incident. They shall then coordinate with the downstream areas
\par
for compensation claims from the responsible polluter. If it is determined that the pol-
\par
lution incident was caused by the upstream region, but a speci c responsible polluter
\par
cannot be identi ed or is unable to provide appropriate compensation, then the upstream
\par
regional government will take the responsibility and provide appropriate compensation. If
\par
the upstream area causes downstream water pollution due to force majeure such as
\par
natural hazard events, then the government of the upstream area is exempt from the cor-
\par
responding liability and compensation.
\par
In order to protect the development and environmental rights of both the upstream
\par
and downstream areas, regional environmental compensation can be established and
\par
implemented. Two compensation methods are designed based on the water quality
\par
objectives. It is recommended that the monthly average of the water quality data
\par
issued or approved by the provincial environmental protection department can be used
\par
as the index of the water quality for the month. If the water pollution index exceeds
\par
the starting target, the upstream area should provide appropriate environmental compen-
\par
sation to the downstream area. However, if the incoming cross-sectional water quality of
\par
the upstream area is also above the starting target, then the upstream area does not need
\par
to compensate the downstream area. The compensation payment should be calculated
\par
based on the pollutant loads, such as the mass amount of organics and nutrients. For
\par
example, in the case of Jiangsu Province, the compensation is calculated as follows:
\par
15,000 RMB (2,381 US\$) per ton of organic pollutants (accounted as COD); 100,000 RMB
\par
(15,873 US\$) per ton of ammonia nitrogen (NH3-N); 100,000 RMB (15,873 US\$) per ton
\par
of TP. The pollution index should be determined according to the dominant pollutants
\par
in a speci c watershed. For the parties that failed to implement the compensation, the
\par
persons in charge should be given administrative sanctions in accordance with the rel-
