\par
4. Conclusions
\par
In China, the majority of watersheds cross administrative regions, and water pollutants do
\par
not comply with administrative boundaries. Therefore, trans-boundary water pollution dis-
\par
putes often occur between adjacent administrative regions. Integrating watershed man-
\par
agement is a challenging and systematic project that cannot be accomplished
\par
overnight. In this study, a policy framework for trans-boundary water quality management
\par
is designed based on water quality objectives. The water quality objectives are suggested
\par
to include initial target, starting target, and emergency target. On the basis of the water
\par
quality objectives, three systems considered as key elements of the policy framework
\par
were discussed: (1) assessment of watershed environmental protection performance
\par
and accountability system; (2) early warning and emergency response system; and (3)
\par
regional environmental compensation system. This policy framework can help identify
\par
the cause of trans-boundary pollution and distinguish the party responsible for the pol-
\par
lution so that the measures necessary to address the trans-boundary water pollution
\par
can be taken.
\par
Since the regional management of the quality of trans-boundary water environments
\par
can be used as the rst step toward integrated watershed management, it can be
\par
implemented at the boundaries of provinces, small cities, and counties using step-by-
\par
step controls to determine the responsibility for water pollution, which in turn provides
\par
experience that serves as the basis for achieving an integrated watershed management
\par
system.
