\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Discrete Bayesian networks (BNs) can be effective for risk- and reliability assessments, in
\par
which probability estimates of (rare) failure events are frequently updated with new
\par
information. To solve such reliability problems accurately in BNs, the discretization of
\par
continuous random variables must be performed carefully. To this end, we develop an
\par
efficient discretization scheme, which is based on finding an optimal discretization for the
\par
linear approximation of the reliability problem obtained from the First-Order Reliability
\par
Method (FORM). Because the probability estimate should be accurate under all possible
\par
future information scenarios, the discretization scheme is optimized with respected to the
\par
expected posterior error. To simplify application of the method, we establish parametric
\par
formulations for efficient discretization of random variables in BNs for reliability problems
\par
based on numerical investigations. The procedure is implemented into a software prototype.
\par
Finally, it is applied to a verification example and an application example, the prediction of
\par
runway overrun of a landing aircraft.
\par
