\textbf{1 Introduction}
\par
For operational risk and reliability management, it is often desirable to compute the
\par
probability of a rare event {$F$} under potentially evolving information. Examples include
\par
warning systems for natural and technical hazards, or the planning of inspection and
\par
intervention actions in infrastructure systems. Ideally, this is achieved through Bayesian
\par
updating of Pr({$F$}) with the new information {$Z$} to the posterior probability Pr({$F\vert Z$}). When
\par
physically-based or empirical models for predicting the rare event exist, such updating is
\par
possible with structural reliability methods (SRM) (Sindel and Rackwitz, 1998, Straub, 2011,
\par
Straub et al., 2016). However, it is often difficult to perform the required computations in
\par
near-real-time, due to a lack of efficiency or robustness. A modeling and computational
\par
framework that does facilitate efficient Bayesian updating is the discrete Bayesian network
\par
(BN). Hence it was proposed to combine SRMs with discrete Bayesian networks for near-
\par
real-time computations (Friis-Hansen, 2000, Straub and Der Kiureghian, 2010a, Straub and
\par
Der Kiureghian, 2010b).
\par
BNs are based on directed acyclic graphs (DAGs), to efficiently define a joint probability
\par
distribution {$p$} {$\mathbf{Y}$} over a random vector {$\mathbf{Y}$} (Jensen and Nielsen, 2007, Kjaerulff and Madsen,
\par
2013). The DAG of a BN, which is often referred to as the qualitative part of a BN, consists
\par
of a node for each variable in {$\mathbf{Y}$}\textbf{ }and a set of directed links among nodes representing
\par
dependence among the variables. In the case of discrete BNs, conditional probability tables
\par
(CPTs) quantitatively define the type and strength of the dependence among the variables.
\par
The entries of the CPT of a variable {$Y$} ! are the probabilities for each state of {$Y$} ! conditional on
\par
all possible combinations of states of its parents.
\par
For hybrid BNs, which include both discrete and continuous variables, exact inference is
\par
available only for two special cases, which are BNs with Gaussian nodes, whose means are
\par
linear functions of their parents, and BNs, whose nodes are defined as a mixture of truncated
\par
basic functions (MoTBFs) (Langseth et al., 2009, Langseth et al., 2012). Otherwise,
\par
approximate inference algorithms are available for hybrid BNs based on sampling techniques,
\par
e.g. (Lerner, 2002, Hanea et al., 2006). However, these are computationally demanding and
\par
not generally suitable for near-real-time decision support (Hanea et al., 2015). As an
\par
alternative, the continuous random variables can be discretized, which enables the use of
\par
exact inference algorithms that exist for general discrete BNs. These include the variable
\par
elimination algorithm (Zhang and Poole, 1994) and the junction tree algorithm (Lauritzen and
\par
Spiegelhalter, 1988, Jensen et al., 1990).
\par
The size of discrete BNs, and the associated computational effort, increases approximately
\par
exponentially with the number of discrete states of its nodes, which motivates the
\par
development of efficient discretization algorithms. While efficient discretization in the
\par
context of machine learning and BNs in general has been investigated by multiple researchers
\par
(Dougherty et al., 1995, Kotsiantis and Kanellopoulos, 2006), research on efficient
\par
discretization in the context of engineering risk analysis or structural reliability has been
\par
limited. In general, it is to be distinguished between static and dynamic discretization. While
