\par
the former discretizes the BN a-priori before entering evidence (offline), the latter is based on
\par
an iterative scheme that updates the discretization scheme in function of the evidence (online).
\par
Dynamic discretization for risk analysis applications has been developed mainly by (Neil et
\par
al., 2008), based on the work by (Kozlov and Koller, 1997). The procedure starts with an
\par
initial discretization of a hybrid BN, for which an approximate entropy error is calculated. If
\par
the error complies with a convergence criterion, the current discretization is accepted.
\par
Otherwise the discretization is iteratively altered, by splitting the intervals with the highest
\par
entropy error, until the convergence criterion is fulfilled. The approach is implemented in the
\par
software AgenaRisk (Agena, 2005). Other dynamic discretization algorithms for reliability
\par
analysis have been proposed, e.g. in (Zhu and Collette, 2015) for dynamic BNs. The
\par
advantage of dynamic discretization is its flexibility when evidence is entered in the BN, i.e.
\par
when the model is updated with new observation.
\par
Static discretization has the advantage of being computationally faster and simple to
\par
implement. Some considerations for static discretization of BNs in reliability applications
\par
have been presented in (Friis-Hansen, 2000, Straub, 2009, Straub and Der Kiureghian, 2010a).
\par
As pointed out by (Friis-Hansen, 2000), for applications in which extreme events are
\par
important, discretization of the distribution tails should be performed with care. Static
\par
discretization facilitates a careful representation of these tails. However, the accuracy of the
\par
static discretization varies with the available evidence. The difficulty is thus to find a
\par
discretization scheme that is optimal under a wide variety of posterior distributions.
\par
In this paper we derive a procedure for efficiently performing static discretization of
\par
continuous reliability problems. An optimal discretization scheme is sought, which minimizes
\par
the expected approximation error with respect to possible future observations (evidence). To
\par
solve this optimization problem, we propose to approximate the reliability problem by the
\par
First-Order Reliability Method (FORM). Section 2 of the paper describes the proposed
\par
methodology. Section 3 presents numerical parameter studies, and simple parametric relations
\par
for defining an efficient discretization scheme are derived. In Section 4, the procedure is
\par
applied to a set of verification examples and to the computation of the probability of runway
\par
overrun of a landing aircraft. While the theory is introduced for problems with only one
\par
design point, considerations regarding problems with multiple design points are given in the
\par
last verification example and in the discussion.
