\textbf{2.4 Simplification of BNs through node removal}
\par
Removing random variables from a BN is one possibility to reduce the computational effort
\par
associated with a model. A formal approach for removing nodes from a BN is described in
\par
(Straub and Der Kiureghian, 2010b). In order to decide which nodes to remove from the BN
\par
the following questions should be considered:
\par
{$\bullet$} Which random variables are relevant for prediction? (These include `Component
\par
performance'.)
\par
{$\bullet$} Which random variables can potentially be observed? (These include the measurement
\par
variables.)
\par
{$\bullet$} Which random variables simplify the modeling of dependencies? (These are e.g.
\par
common influencing factors such as {$I$}! in Fig. 2.)
\par
{$\bullet$} For which random variables is it desirable to explicitly show their influence on
\par
component performance?
\par
If a random variable does not belong to any of these categories, the corresponding node in the
\par
BN can be removed. Since the computational efficiency of the model is governed by the size
\par
of the CPT of the 'Component state' node, the primary interest is in removing basic random
\par
variables {$X$}! from the BN. As a measure for the relevance of a basic random variable,
