\par
The optimization is implemented through a two-level approach. The optimization of the
\par
continuous parameters width {$w$}! and position of the discretization frame {$v$}! for all {$i$} = 1, \dots{} , {$n$}
\par
is carried out using unconstrained nonlinear optimization for fixed values of {$n$}! . The
\par
optimization of the discrete {$n$}! is performed through a local search algorithm. Note that the
\par
optimization is performed offline, i.e. prior to running the BN, hence it does not affect the
\par
goal of near-real time performance of the BN. Furthermore, in section 3 a heuristic is derived
\par
that can replace the time-consuming solution of the optimization problem.
\par
2.6.2 Efficient discretization of the original random variables {$\mathbf{X}$}
\par
Since the nodes in the BN represent random variables X in their original outcome space, the
\par
discretization schemes, which are derived for the corresponding standard normal random
\par
variables U, need to be transformed to the X-space. In the case of mutually independent
\par
random variables X!, any point on the i-th interval boundary in U-space -- if transformed -- will
\par
result in the same corresponding i-th interval boundary in X-space. This is not the case for
\par
dependent random variables X!, where a mapping of the interval boundaries in U-space to X-
\par
space will not lead to an orthogonal discretization scheme in X-space. To preserve
\par
orthogonality throughout the transformation, we propose to represent each interval boundary
\par
through a characteristic point and determine the boundary in X-space through a
\par
transformation of this point. For transforming the interval boundary of X!, the characteristic
