4.3.1 Selection of relevant random variables
\par
The applied RWO model includes 10 basic random variables. However, it is sufficient to
\par
include only a selection of these explicitly in the BN. Random variables that are not relevant
\par
for the prediction of RWO in the considered scenarios can be excluded. This is the case for
\par
random variables with a low FORM importance, whose value does not depend significantly
\par
on airport and aircraft type. Here, all random variables, whose absolute value of the FORM
\par
importance measure {$A$}!
\par
{$\primes$} is smaller than 0.1, are excluded (see Tab. 8). The one exception is
\par
landing weight, since its mean value is substantially influenced by the aircraft type.
\par
One can additionally exclude random variables that cannot be measured before the decision
\par
on whether to land or not is made. This holds for Touchdown point and the time at which the
\par
pilot initiates breaking. Since these basic random variables are also not needed to simplify the
\par
modeling of dependencies, it is not necessary to explicitly model them in the BN, as indicated
\par
in Tab. 8.
\par
4.3.2 BN model
\par
The resulting BN of the RWO warning system is shown in Fig. 14. During the aircraft
\par
approach, measurements can be obtained for the three basic random variables included in the
\par
BN.
\par
The random variables are discretized separately for each aircraft-airport combination (joint
\par
states of discrete parents) with 8 intervals each, following the proposed discretization
\par
procedure. In a second step, the discretization schemes are merged, i.e. the regions of the
\par
outcome space, which are discretized with fine intervals for at least one of the aircraft-airport
\par
combinations, are discretized with the respective fine intervals also in the merged
\par
discretization scheme. In the end 15 (landing-weight), 10 (headwind) and 9 (approach speed
\par
deviation) intervals are used to discretize the three basic random variables.
\par
For all observable quantities, the measurements {$m$}! are modeled with an additive observation
\par
error:
\par
{$m$}! = {$x$}! + {$E$}! (18)
\par
{$E$}! is modeled by a normal distribution with zero mean and standard deviation {$\sigma$}!!.
\par
For the random variable landing weight (at landing time), the standard deviation of the
\par
measurement error is {$\sigma$}!!"
\par
= 0.34 tons. Due to turbulences governing wind speeds, the
\par
measurement of the head wind speed at the time of the measurement is only an uncertain
\par
indicator for the head wind speed at landing time; we model the measurement error with a
\par
standard deviation {$\sigma$}!!"
\par
= 2.88 kts . The measurement uncertainty associated with the
\par
approach speed deviation at landing has standard deviation {$\sigma$}!!"\#
\par
= 4.21 kts.
\par
49 (Measurement LW), 57 (Measurement HW) and 57 (Measurement ASD) intervals are used
\par
to discretize the measurement nodes.
