\textbf{5 Discussion}
\par
When modeling with BNs, it is often necessary or beneficial to discretize continuous random
\par
variables. When the BN includes rare events that are a function of such random variables, the
\par
choice of the discretization scheme is non-trivial. In this contribution, we investigate this
\par
discretization based on FORM concepts, and propose a heuristic procedure for an efficient
\par
discretization in these cases. This is based on importance measures {$A$}! obtained through a
\par
FORM analysis, which represent the influence of the uncertainty associated with a random
\par
variable {$X$}!.
\par
The most important finding is that discretization should focus on the area around the most
\par
likely failure point (design point), identified by a FORM analysis. Furthermore, we find that
\par
optimally all random variables should be discretized with approximately equal numbers of
\par
intervals, independent of their importance, as long as \textbar{$A$}! \textbar{} is not close to zero. The widths of
\par
the intervals should be selected based on the FORM importance {$A$}! of the random variables.
\par
With increasing importance, the interval width should be reduced, leading to finer
\par
discretization for larger {$A$}!. This relation is particularly evident for {$A$}! {$\geq$} 0.8. We show that
\par
it is possible to fit a parametric function to approximate the relation between {$A$}! and the
\par
optimal width of the region on which the discretization should focus.
\par
This parametric function is used to derive a heuristic procedure for finding an efficient
\par
discretization. This allows the extrapolation of the optimization results to problems with more
\par
random variables. As demonstrated by the verification examples, the heuristic procedure leads
\par
to accurate results.
\par
This paper is restricted to static discretization. Application of the proposed procedure within
\par
dynamic discretization (e.g. (Neil et al., 2008)) should be investigated. The results of the
\par
procedure can serve as an initial discretization scheme, which is iteratively adjusted within
\par
dynamic discretization. This might strongly enhance the convergence performance of these
\par
algorithms.
\par
The gain in computational efficiency resulting from the proposed procedure over alternative
\par
static discretization approaches is problem specific. Some insights can be gained from Figure
\par
8. A discretization with intervals of equal width centered at the origin would require between
\par
approximately 2 to 5 times more intervals per random variable to achieve the same accuracy.
