Because of the exponential increase of computational effort with number of random variables,
\par
this leads to a considerable increase in efficiency. The gain compared to discretization with
\par
equal-frequency intervals is expected to be even higher, since equal frequency intervals
\par
focuses the fine intervals on the region of high probability density rather than on the tails of
\par
the distribution.
\par
This paper focuses on component reliability problems, which are characterized by a single
\par
design point. Nevertheless the heuristics derived can also be applied to system reliability
\par
problems. System reliability problems can in general be treated as combinations of
\par
component reliability problems. Parallel and series systems are to be distinguished. For
\par
parallel systems discretization should be performed based on the joint design point of the
\par
problem. For series systems, following the same line of thought as in the runway overrun
\par
example, discretization can be performed separately for each component problem
\par
(corresponds to the discrete cases i.e. airport- aircraft combinations in the RWO example). In
\par
a second step the discretization schemes can be merged. In the same way it is possible to
\par
apply the heuristic to multi state components. One can treat each limit state surface (LSF)
\par
defining the boundary between two states separately and merge the discretization schemes
\par
afterwards.
\par
The number of basic random variables in a single LSF that can be modeled explicitly in a BN
\par
is limited to around 5 to 8. This is due to the exponential growth of the target nodes CPT with
\par
increasing number of parents and is independent of the discretization method. Despite this
\par
limitation, BNs are applicable to many practical problems -- particularly if one considers that
\par
usually not all basic random variables need to be modeled explicitly as nodes, as
\par
demonstrated in the presented example.
\par
While in this paper the focus was on the discretization of the basic random variables, it is
\par
straightforward to incorporate the BNs discussed into larger models.
\par
\textbf{6 Conclusion}
\par
We investigate discretization of continuous reliability problems such that they can be treated
\par
in a discrete Bayesian network framework. Reliability problems with linear LSF in standard
\par
normal space are considered. These can be seen as FORM approximations of reliability
\par
problems. For these linear LSFs, optimal discretization schemes are found, which are optimal
\par
with respect to an error measure calculated through a preposterior analysis. Since FORM is
\par
known to give good approximations also for most non-linear reliability problem, the resulting
\par
discretization schemes are efficient also for non-linear LSFs. The main findings presented in
\par
this paper are:
\par
{$\bullet$} An optimal discretization scheme should discretize finely the area around the FORM
\par
design point.
